(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.c4876ec1bb.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl1_q01","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher removes the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) from a developing chick limb bud. Which of the following best describes the expected consequence?","choices":{"A":"Digit fusion develops along the hand plate, because apoptosis between the digital rays is suppressed when AER-derived FGF8 signals are absent from the distal limb mesenchyme.","B":"An ectopic limb bud forms on the contralateral side, because FGF10 released from the lateral plate mesoderm is redirected toward the opposite limb field without AER-mediated inhibition.","C":"The limb ceases outgrowth and fails to form structures distal to the level of removal, because the AER secretes FGF8 required for continued mesoderm proliferation in the progress zone.","D":"Mirror-image digit duplication occurs on the anterior limb border, because anterior mesenchyme is exposed to unopposed Sonic Hedgehog from the zone of polarizing activity.","E":"The limb develops normally with intact proximal structures but shows shortened metacarpals, because only distal ossification depends on AER-derived FGF8 while proximal endochondral centers are autonomous."},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AER is located at the lateral-most rim of the limb bud and secretes FGF8 (and FGF4), which sustains undifferentiated mesenchymal proliferation in the underlying progress zone. Without the AER, outgrowth stops and only the proximal structures that had already formed are retained. Digit fusion (syndactyly) results from failed apoptosis between digital rays, not AER removal. Ectopic limb formation requires FGF10/FGF8 signaling to initiate a new bud, not a consequence of AER loss. Mirror-image digit duplication results from transplanting an extra ZPA anteriorly, not from AER removal. Isolated metacarpal shortening describes achondroplasia, not AER absence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AER = FGF8 source; if AER removed, limb truncated at that developmental stage — classic experimental manipulation tested in developmental biology. COMLEX: AER removal is a limb defect mechanism; connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (amelia = no AER/no FGF8 expressed).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis","whyNot":{"B":"Ectopic limb formation require","E":"Isolated metacarpal shortening"}},{"id":"sdl1_q02","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) occupies the posterior mesenchyme of the developing limb bud and controls digit identity along the anterior-to-posterior axis. Which signaling molecule mediates this patterning function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8), which is secreted from the AER at the distal rim of the limb bud and maintains undifferentiated mesenchymal cells in the progress zone for continued outgrowth.","B":"Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), which is most highly concentrated near the posterior mesenchyme where the fifth digit forms and least concentrated anteriorly near the first digit.","C":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 (FGF10), which is released by lateral plate mesoderm to induce the overlying ectoderm to form the AER and initiate the initial phases of limb bud outgrowth.","D":"Wnt signaling protein, which is released by the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm to induce dermomyotome formation from the paraxial mesoderm somites bilaterally.","E":"Indian Hedgehog (IHH), which is secreted by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the epiphyseal growth plate to coordinate the rate of chondrocyte proliferation and longitudinal bone elongation."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh), creating a posterior-to-anterior concentration gradient that assigns digit identity: highest Shh = fifth digit, lowest = first digit. FGF8 is the AER signal maintaining proximal-to-distal outgrowth. FGF10 is secreted by lateral plate mesoderm to initiate AER formation and limb bud outgrowth. Wnt induces dermomyotome differentiation from somites. Indian Hedgehog (IHH) is a growth plate regulator, not a limb patterning molecule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA = Shh gradient = digit identity (A-P axis); AER = FGF8 = proximal-to-distal outgrowth. These two structures are the key patterning signals tested. COMLEX: Connect to SDL 1 Obj 4 — transplanting ZPA anteriorly causes mirror-image polydactyly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis","whyNot":{"D":"Wnt induces dermomyotome diffe"}},{"id":"sdl1_q03","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During week 4 of embryogenesis, the paraxial mesoderm segments into somites. Sonic Hedgehog released by the notochord and ventral neural tube induces differentiation of the ventral portion of each somite into a specific structure. What does this ventral somite component become?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which migrates to surround the notochord and neural tube and differentiates into the vertebral column, ribs, and other axial skeletal elements.","B":"The dermomyotome, which is induced by Wnt signaling from the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm and gives rise to both the dermis of the skin and the skeletal muscle precursors.","C":"The myotome, which subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial portions under spinal nerve innervation and generates the intrinsic back muscles and the muscles of the trunk and limbs.","D":"The meningotome, which proliferates medial to the sclerotome under notochordal signaling and eventually differentiates into the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which lies peripheral to the paraxial mesoderm and splits into somatic and splanchnic layers to form the pelvic bones, limb bones, and body wall cavities."},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral portion of each somite becomes the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the notochord and ventral neural tube. The sclerotome forms the vertebral bodies, neural arches, ribs, and tendons. The dorsal portion becomes the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling, which further subdivides into the myotome (skeletal muscles) and dermatome (dermis). The meningotome is a subdivision of the sclerotome that forms the meninges, not the ventral somite itself. Lateral plate mesoderm is a separate compartment from paraxial mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paraxial mesoderm → somites → sclerotome (ventral, Shh) + dermomyotome (dorsal, Wnt). Sclerotome → vertebrae and ribs. Dermomyotome → myotome (muscle) and dermatome (dermis). COMLEX: Three MSK sources — paraxial mesoderm, neural crest cells (facial bones), and lateral plate mesoderm (limb/pelvic bones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — paraxial mesoderm somites give rise to sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome; AER (FGF8) drives distal limb outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns the A/P axis","whyNot":{"C":"The dorsal portion becomes the","D":"The meningotome is a subdivisi"}},{"id":"sdl1_q04","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During vertebral column development, each sclerotome somite subdivides into cranial and caudal sub-segments that then undergo re-segmentation. Which of the following correctly describes this process and its anatomical result?","choices":{"A":"The caudal sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form a vertebral body, leaving the notochord remnant between segments to become the nucleus pulposus.","B":"The entire sclerotome ossifies as a single unit per segment, and the notochord degenerates completely without contributing to the intervertebral disc, leaving only loose mesenchyme between vertebrae.","C":"The cranial sub-segment of each somite fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite to form paired vertebral bodies per spinal level, with sclerotome remnant forming the annulus fibrosus.","D":"The caudal sub-segment fuses with the ipsilateral dermomyotome to form the posterior elements of the vertebra, while the cranial sub-segment becomes the nucleus pulposus under FGF10 signaling.","E":"Adjacent sclerotome somites remain intact and separate without fusion, with the intervertebral disc cavity enlarging spontaneously under Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the persisting notochord."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Re-segmentation is critical for vertebral column formation: the caudal portion of one sclerotome somite fuses with the cranial portion of the next inferior somite to build each vertebral body. This creates an offset so that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites. The notochord remnant trapped between the newly formed vertebral bodies persists as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral disc. The annulus fibrosus is derived from the sclerotome, not from the cranial sub-segment directly. Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, through which spinal nerve roots exit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults\"). Annulus fibrosus → from sclerotome. This is a classic embryology board question. COMLEX: Re-segmentation also creates the intervertebral foramina, allowing spinal nerve exit — relevant to somatic dysfunction and radiculopathy discussions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus","whyNot":{"B":"The notochord remnant trapped "}},{"id":"sdl1_q05","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the developing embryo, TBX genes play a key role in distinguishing which type of limb a limb bud will produce. Which of the following correctly matches the TBX gene to the limb field it specifies?","choices":{"A":"HOX genes establish whether a limb bud becomes an upper or lower extremity by controlling TBX5 and TBX4 expression at the level of the limb field within the lateral plate mesoderm.","B":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb field of lateral plate mesoderm specifying upper extremity identity, while TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying lower extremity identity.","C":"TBX4 is expressed in the upper limb field specifying shoulder and arm identity, while TBX5 is expressed in the lower limb field specifying hip and leg identity, with FGF10 as the common downstream effector.","D":"TBX genes specify anterior versus posterior limb identity within a single limb bud, working in concert with Sonic Hedgehog from the ZPA to assign digit positions along the hand or foot plate.","E":"Both TBX4 and TBX5 are co-expressed in both limb fields and only distinguish upper from lower limb identity through differential binding to the FGF10 promoter in the lateral plate mesoderm."},"correct":"B","explanation":"TBX5 is expressed in the upper limb lateral plate mesoderm and specifies upper extremity identity. TBX4 is expressed in the lower limb field and specifies lower extremity identity. HOX genes determine position along the craniocaudal axis (where a limb forms), not which type of limb forms. The lecture slides confirm: HOX = \"where along the column,\" TBX = \"upper vs lower,\" with FGF10 as the shared primary growth stimulant. TBX genes do not control A/P digit patterning, which is the ZPA/Shh role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Limb bud gene hierarchy — HOX genes determine craniocaudal position; TBX5/TBX4 specify upper/lower limb identity; FGF10 initiates AER induction; AER (FGF8) drives outgrowth; ZPA (Shh) patterns A/P axis. COMLEX: Upper limb bud forms at C5-T1; lower limb bud at L2-S3 levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus","whyNot":{"A":"HOX genes determine position a","D":"TBX genes do not control A/P d"}},{"id":"sdl1_q06","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A student is reviewing vertebral ossification for an upcoming exam. Which of the following correctly identifies the primary ossification centers of a typical vertebra and the secondary ossification centers that appear at puberty?","choices":{"A":"Primary centers: one in the centrum (vertebral body) and two in the neural arch (one on each side); secondary centers: tip of spinous process, tips of two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses at the superior and inferior vertebral body rim.","B":"Primary centers: three in the centrum, two in the pedicles, and two in the laminae; secondary centers: one in the spinous process appearing at puberty, completing ossification by age 25.","C":"Primary centers: one in the centrum and one in the neural arch fused midline; secondary centers: one annular epiphysis at the disc surface appearing at age 5 and fusing by early adulthood.","D":"Primary centers: one in the spinous process and two annular epiphyses; secondary centers: one in the centrum and two in the neural arch appearing at puberty after the posterior elements are complete.","E":"Primary centers: two in the centrum (anterior and posterior) and one in the neural arch; secondary centers: two in the transverse processes and one in the tip of the spinous process, all appearing before birth."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each typical vertebra has three primary ossification centers: one in the centrum (future vertebral body) and one on each side of the neural arch (two total). At puberty, five secondary ossification centers appear: one at the tip of the spinous process, one at each tip of the two transverse processes, and two annular epiphyses (one superior, one inferior) at the rim of the vertebral body. The secondary centers fuse with the vertebral body and complete ossification during early adulthood. This pattern is important for understanding Scheuermann disease and stress-related vertebral injuries in adolescents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertebral ossification — 3 primary centers (centrum + 2 neural arch). Secondary centers appear at puberty. The primary centers are in the centrum and neural arch (course SDL 1 slides p18–19). COMLEX: Spina bifida occurs when the neural arch centers fail to fuse posteriorly — connects to SDL 1 Obj 4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lateral plate mesoderm forms limb buds; vertebrae form by sclerotome re-segmentation; notochord remnant becomes the nucleus pulposus"},{"id":"sdl1_q07","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The myotome subdivides into epaxial and hypaxial muscle groups during embryogenesis. Which of the following correctly distinguishes epaxial from hypaxial muscles and their innervation?","choices":{"A":"Epaxial muscles form the flexors of the upper and lower limbs and are innervated by anterior divisions of the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses, while hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic back extensors innervated by posterior rami.","B":"Epaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves, while hypaxial muscles form muscles of the anterior neck, trunk, limbs, and perineum and are innervated by anterior rami.","C":"Epaxial muscles derive from the dermomyotome under Wnt signaling and form the intercostal muscles, while hypaxial muscles derive from the sclerotome under Sonic Hedgehog signaling and form the paraspinal muscles.","D":"Hypaxial muscles form the intrinsic muscles of the back and are innervated by posterior rami, while epaxial muscles migrate into the limb buds to form flexor and extensor compartments innervated by the plexuses.","E":"Both epaxial and hypaxial muscles derive from the lateral plate mesoderm and are equally innervated by both anterior and posterior rami, depending on their final anatomical position in the body wall."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Epaxial muscles arise from the medial part of the myotome, remain dorsal, and form the intrinsic (erector spinae and deep back) muscles. They are innervated by posterior rami of spinal nerves. Hypaxial muscles arise from the lateral myotome, migrate ventrally and into limb buds, and form the muscles of the anterior neck, thorax, abdomen, limbs, and perineum. They are innervated by anterior rami, which become the cervical, brachial, intercostal, lumbar, and lumbosacral plexuses. Both groups arise from the myotome (paraxial mesoderm), not lateral plate mesoderm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epaxial = intrinsic back muscles, posterior rami; hypaxial = everything else (limbs, trunk, perineum), anterior rami. This explains why the intrinsic back muscles are supplied at every segmental level by posterior rami — each myotome level contributes one epaxial segment. COMLEX: Posterior rami somatic dysfunction affects epaxial muscles and is the basis for paraspinal muscle tender points and restricted spinal motion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum"},{"id":"sdl1_q08","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During limb development, hypaxial myotome cells migrate into the limb bud and split into dorsal and ventral muscle masses. Which of the following correctly identifies the muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb and their nerve supply?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors of the upper limb and is innervated by branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus, including the median and ulnar nerves.","B":"The dorsal muscle mass forms the extensor muscles of the upper limb but is innervated by the anterior division of the brachial plexus, specifically the musculocutaneous and median nerve branches.","C":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms extensors innervated by the tibial and obturator nerves, which branch from the lumbosacral plexus to supply the posterior compartments of the lower limb.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass forms both flexor and extensor muscles, with each group innervated by opposite divisions of the brachial plexus based on their final anterior or posterior position in the limb.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb forms the extensor muscles and is innervated by branches of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, primarily the radial and axillary nerves."},"correct":"E","explanation":"During upper limb development, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior side of the developing limb and forms the extensor compartment (triceps, wrist extensors, finger extensors, intrinsic extensors). It is innervated by the posterior division of the brachial plexus — specifically the radial nerve (C5-T1) and axillary nerve (C5-C6). The ventral muscle mass forms the flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division via the median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves. The tibial and obturator nerves are lower limb nerves and have no role in upper limb innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal muscle mass = extensors = posterior division brachial plexus (radial + axillary). Ventral muscle mass = flexors/adductors = anterior division (median + ulnar + musculocutaneous). Lower limb: dorsal mass = extensors (now anterior after medial rotation), still supplied by posterior-division nerves (femoral, common fibular, gluteal); ventral mass = flexors/adductors, supplied by anterior-division nerves (tibial, obturator). COMLEX: Radial nerve palsy (wrist drop) from midshaft humerus fracture disrupts the dorsal muscle mass innervation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum","whyNot":{"D":"The ventral muscle mass forms "}},{"id":"sdl1_q09","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Skeletal muscle fibers develop through a defined sequence of cellular stages: myoblasts fuse into long, multinucleated myotubes whose nuclei are initially central. In the maturing fiber, what causes the nuclei to move from a central position to the periphery of the cell?","choices":{"A":"Satellite cells fuse with the surface of the myotube and pull the existing central nuclei outward toward the sarcolemma","B":"Early contractions against the developing tendons mechanically displace the nuclei toward the ends and edges of the fiber","C":"Accumulating myofilaments fill most of the cytoplasm of the maturing myotube and slowly push the nuclei to the periphery","D":"Contact with the motor nerve at the neuromuscular junction draws all of the nuclei toward the synaptic region of the fiber","E":"Apoptosis removes the central nuclei, leaving only peripheral nuclei that are then replenished by further myoblast fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During differentiation, myoblasts fuse to form long, multinucleated myotubes with centrally placed nuclei. As myofibrils (organized actin and myosin filaments) accumulate, they take up most of the cytoplasm and push the nuclei to the periphery; by the end of the third month cross-striations typical of skeletal muscle appear (Langman). Satellite cells are reserve stem cells lying outside the fiber and do not reposition existing nuclei. Neither early contraction nor motor innervation drives the shift, and the central nuclei are displaced, not eliminated by apoptosis. Clinically, centrally placed nuclei in postnatal muscle on biopsy point to a myopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myoblasts fuse → multinucleated myotube with central nuclei → myofilaments fill the cytoplasm and push the nuclei to the periphery. Centrally placed nuclei in mature muscle = myopathy (e.g., centronuclear myopathy, myotonic dystrophy). COMLEX: Cross-striations appear by the end of the third month of development (Langman).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — myoblasts fuse into myotubes at 4 weeks; epaxial (posterior rami) muscles form the intrinsic back; hypaxial (anterior rami) muscles form trunk, limbs, and perineum","whyNot":{"D":"Neither early contraction nor ","E":"Neither early contraction nor "}},{"id":"sdl1_q10","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has short stature with shortened limb bones relative to a normal-length trunk, a large cranium, and limited elbow extension. Spinal radiographs show a narrow interpedicular distance in the lumbar region. Genetic testing identifies a gain-of-function mutation that impairs chondrocyte proliferation at growth plates. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein), whose loss-of-function destabilizes chondrocytes during endochondral ossification and causes pseudoachondroplasia with more pronounced joint laxity and no foreshortening of facial bones.","B":"GHR (growth hormone receptor), whose loss-of-function reduces IGF-1 production and causes proportionate dwarfism affecting limb length, trunk height, and cranial dimensions equally without facial bone involvement.","C":"IHH (Indian Hedgehog), whose gain-of-function would overstimulate chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, causing disproportionate elongation of limb bones relative to the trunk rather than shortening.","D":"COL1A1 (type I collagen alpha-1), whose loss-of-function disrupts bone matrix production and causes osteogenesis imperfecta with blue sclerae, frequent fractures, and normal limb-to-trunk proportions.","E":"FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3), whose constitutive activation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates, causing short limbs with a relatively normal trunk and a large head."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Achondroplasia is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, which constitutively inhibits chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates (endochondral ossification). Because long bones grow via endochondral ossification, they are shortened. The skull, however, grows via intramembranous ossification and is unaffected, giving a characteristically large cranium relative to the limbs. More than 85% of cases are sporadic and associated with advanced paternal age. COMP mutations cause pseudoachondroplasia, which is clinically similar but has greater joint laxity and no facial bone foreshortening. GHR mutations cause proportionate dwarfism. IHH gain-of-function would increase, not decrease, bone length. COL1A1 mutations cause osteogenesis imperfecta, not disproportionate dwarfism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia = FGFR3 gain-of-function; autosomal dominant, full penetrance; homozygosity lethal; most common cause of short-limbed dwarfism; >85% sporadic; associated with advanced paternal age (FA). Endochondral ossification impaired, membranous ossification intact → big head, short limbs. COMLEX: Spinal stenosis is a common complication of achondroplasia (impaired neural arch endochondral growth narrows canal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida","whyNot":{"B":"GHR mutations cause proportion","C":"IHH gain-of-function would inc"}},{"id":"sdl1_q11","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn is noted on physical examination to have fusion of the second and third digits bilaterally, with soft tissue and bony elements joined from the metacarpophalangeal to the distal interphalangeal joints. The remainder of the examination is normal. Which embryologic process failed to occur?","choices":{"A":"Apoptosis between the digital rays of the hand plate, which normally eliminates the interdigital mesenchyme and carves out each individual finger as a distinct structure.","B":"Migration of lateral plate mesoderm cells into the limb bud to form the skeletal precursors of each digit, resulting in absent mesenchymal condensations between the affected digital rays.","C":"Re-segmentation of sclerotome somites to create the intervertebral foramina, a homologous process to digit separation that fails when HOX gene expression is disrupted during vertebral column formation.","D":"Expression of FGF8 by the apical ectodermal ridge, which normally induces apoptosis in the interdigital mesenchyme by withdrawing growth factor support from non-digital ray tissue.","E":"Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the zone of polarizing activity, which normally assigns unique digit identities by establishing an anterior-to-posterior Shh concentration gradient across the hand plate."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Syndactyly results from failure of apoptosis (programmed cell death) in the interdigital mesenchyme between the digital rays. Normally, the cells of the hand plate between each digital ray undergo apoptosis, separating the fingers by approximately day 52. When this apoptosis fails or is incomplete, the digits remain fused — a spectrum ranging from simple skin webbing to complete bony fusion. The AER supports growth zone proliferation but does not directly induce interdigital apoptosis. The ZPA assigns digit identity via Shh but is not responsible for digital separation. Polydactyly, not syndactyly, results from aberrant molecular signals causing extra digital ray formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Syndactyly = failed apoptosis between digital rays. Polydactyly = aberrant ZPA signals or excess digit ray formation. In bats, the absence of interdigital apoptosis is what gives them wings — a useful memory hook. COMLEX: Digital anomalies may accompany chromosomal syndromes (e.g., trisomy 21, VACTERL); examining digits is part of the newborn screen for syndromic malformations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida","whyNot":{"D":"The AER supports growth zone p","E":"The ZPA assigns digit identity"}},{"id":"sdl1_q12","sdlNumber":1,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An infant born in the 1960s has five-fingered hands attached directly to the shoulder girdles, with complete absence of the humerus, radius, and ulna bilaterally. The fingers are functional with mild motor deficit. The mother had taken a sedative during the first trimester. Which mechanism best explains the pattern of limb malformation?","choices":{"A":"Complete absence of FGF8 from the AER caused total failure of limb bud outgrowth, with the hands spared because digital ray condensations had formed before the teratogen was introduced.","B":"A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation selectively impaired chondrocyte proliferation in the long bone growth plates of the arm and forearm while sparing the hand bones, which undergo intramembranous ossification.","C":"Failure of the ZPA to express Sonic Hedgehog during A/P patterning caused loss of all proximal limb segments, with only the most distal Shh-independent digit structures surviving teratogen exposure.","D":"Disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical limb elongation period prevented proximal and intermediate segment formation, while the AER-maintained distal bud still produced functional hands.","E":"Premature apoptosis of the dorsal muscle mass in the upper limb bud caused secondary regression of the humeral and forearm skeletal elements, which require mechanical stimulation from muscle contraction for normal development."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phocomelia — hands and feet projecting directly from the trunk — is the classic presentation of thalidomide embryopathy, which was used as a sedative/antiemetic in the late 1950s and early 1960s. The mechanism involves disruption of HOX gene expression during the critical window of limb development (the fourth and fifth weeks of gestation are the most sensitive period for thalidomide limb defects, Langman Ch 12). Because HOX genes pattern the proximal-to-intermediate limb segments, their disruption prevents humerus, radius, and ulna from forming, while the more distal AER-driven hand bud continues developing. Total absence of the AER would cause complete amelia with no limb at all. FGFR3 mutations cause achondroplasia, not proximal limb segment aplasia. ZPA disruption affects digit identity, not proximal segment formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phocomelia = thalidomide; mechanism = HOX gene disruption during the critical limb period (weeks 4–5 most sensitive, Langman Ch 12). Amelia = no AER = no FGF8 = no limb at all. Meromelia = partial limb aplasia. COMLEX: Thalidomide is teratogenic during organogenesis (weeks 3-8); limb defects are most readily induced in weeks 4–5 — the window of sensitivity coincides with when women often do not yet know they are pregnant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — FGFR3 gain-of-function causes achondroplasia; failed AER causes amelia; HOX disruption causes phocomelia; caudal neuropore failure causes spina bifida","whyNot":{"A":"Total absence of the AER would","B":"FGFR3 mutations cause achondro","C":"ZPA disruption affects digit i"}},{"id":"sdl1_q13","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A developmental biology researcher transplants the zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) from the posterior mesenchyme of a chick limb bud to the anterior mesenchyme of the same limb bud. The developing limb subsequently forms six digits instead of five, with the extra digits arranged in a mirror-image pattern on the anterior side. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying this finding?","choices":{"A":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF8 into the anterior mesenchyme, overriding the AER signal and causing progress zone cells to produce additional digit condensations proportional to local FGF8 concentration.","B":"The transplanted ZPA secretes FGF10 into the anterior mesenchyme, initiating a new limb bud outgrowth event that recruits additional lateral plate mesoderm cells to form a supernumerary complete digit column.","C":"The transplanted ZPA releases Wnt signaling proteins into the anterior limb field, which redirect dermomyotome cells from the paraxial mesoderm into the limb bud to form extra mesenchymal digit condensations.","D":"The transplanted ZPA releases Indian Hedgehog, which normally functions in growth plate chondrocyte regulation but ectopically activates HOX gene expression in the anterior limb field causing polydactyly.","E":"The transplanted ZPA expresses Sonic Hedgehog in the anterior mesenchyme, creating a second posterior-to-anterior Shh gradient in opposition to the original gradient, which induces mirror-image digit duplication in the anterior limb field."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ZPA normally sits in the posterior mesenchyme and expresses Sonic Hedgehog (Shh) at high concentration near the fifth digit and low concentration near the first digit. When a second ZPA is placed anteriorly, it creates an opposing Shh gradient, so both the posterior and anterior ends of the limb now interpret themselves as \"posterior.\" The result is mirror-image digit duplication: posterior-type digits (another fifth and fourth digit) form on the anterior side in place of the thumb. This classic experiment established that Shh is the key digit-patterning signal. The ZPA does not express FGF8, FGF10, Wnt, or IHH as its primary signaling output. FGF10 originates from lateral plate mesoderm, and AER expresses FGF8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ZPA transplant to anterior = mirror-image polydactyly = classic experimental proof that Shh controls A/P digit patterning. Removal of ZPA from medial hand = thumbs on both sides. High Shh concentration = digit 5 identity; low = digit 1. COMLEX: Polydactyly can result from ZPA signal overstimulation or aberrant molecular signals during hand plate formation (the hand plate forms after day 35, course SDL 1 text).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — polydactyly from aberrant ZPA/Shh signals; amelia from absent AER/FGF8; phocomelia from HOX gene disruption during limb elongation","whyNot":{"A":"The ZPA does not express FGF8,","B":"The ZPA does not express FGF8,","C":"The ZPA does not express FGF8,","D":"The ZPA does not express FGF8,"}},{"id":"sdl1_q14","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A developmental biologist implants beads that block Wnt signaling between the dorsal neural tube and the overlying surface ectoderm of a chick embryo at the somite stage. Sonic Hedgehog signaling from the notochord and ventral neural tube is left intact. Which somite derivative would be expected to fail to form?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, because its mesenchyme depends on dorsal Wnt signals to migrate around the notochord and lay down the cartilage model of the vertebrae","B":"The meningotome, because the dura, arachnoid, and pia arise from somite cells that require Wnt from the overlying ectoderm to separate from the sclerotome","C":"The nucleus pulposus, because notochordal cells trapped between vertebral bodies require Wnt from the dorsal neural tube to persist within each disc","D":"The dermomyotome, because dorsal Wnt induces it lateral to the sclerotome, so the dermis and the skeletal muscle precursors of the somite are lost","E":"The parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm, because Wnt from the dorsal neural tube is required for it to join the ectoderm and form the somatopleure of the limbs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two dorsoventral signals pattern each somite. Sonic Hedgehog from the notochord and ventral neural tube induces the sclerotome, while Wnt from the dorsal neural tube and overlying ectoderm induces the dermomyotome lateral to it; the dermomyotome then splits into the myotome (skeletal muscle) and dermatome (dermis). Blocking Wnt with Shh intact therefore removes the dermomyotome. The sclerotome is the Shh-dependent ventral derivative, so it still forms. The meningotome lies medial to the sclerotome and forms the meninges; the course text does not tie it to dorsal Wnt. The nucleus pulposus comes from the notochord, not from a somite. The parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm is not a somite derivative at all; it forms the somatopleure that builds the body wall and limb buds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somite patterning: ventral Shh (notochord, floor plate) → sclerotome (vertebrae, ribs); dorsal Wnt → dermomyotome → myotome (skeletal muscle) + dermatome (dermis). COMLEX: Dermatomes and myotomes share a segmental origin with the spinal nerve that follows them, the basis of segmental somatic dysfunction findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — the myotome splits into epaxial (posterior rami) and hypaxial (anterior rami) muscle groups","whyNot":{"A":"The sclerotome is the Shh-depe","B":"The meningotome lies medial to"}},{"id":"sdl1_q15","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the derivation of musculoskeletal tissues from mesodermal layers, focusing on somite differentiation and the roles of the apical ectodermal ridge (AER) and zone of polarizing activity (ZPA) in upper and lower limb development.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman presents with acute low back pain after lifting a heavy box at work. MRI shows a herniated intervertebral disc at L4-L5 compressing the L5 nerve root. A first-year medical student reviewing the case asks their attending which embryonic structure gives rise to the nucleus pulposus that is compressing the nerve root. Which of the following is the correct answer?","choices":{"A":"The sclerotome, which forms the fibrous outer ring of the intervertebral disc (the annulus fibrosus) and whose partial failure of migration creates the gelatinous herniated material seen on MRI.","B":"The notochord, a midline signaling structure in the embryo whose remnant, trapped between the re-segmented vertebral bodies, persists as the gelatinous nucleus pulposus of each intervertebral disc.","C":"The myotome, which gives rise to the paraspinal and intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami and does not contribute structural material to the intervertebral disc or nucleus pulposus.","D":"The meningotome, which forms the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater surrounding the spinal cord and nerve roots; its remnant does not persist within the nucleus pulposus or the disc space.","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which forms the pelvic and lower extremity bones and the body wall cavities and does not contribute to midline axial structures such as the vertebral bodies or intervertebral discs."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The notochord is the embryonic midline structure that induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate and signals the surrounding sclerotomes to form the vertebral column. During re-segmentation, the caudal sub-segment of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial sub-segment of the adjacent inferior somite, trapping a remnant of the notochord between the newly formed vertebral bodies. This notochord remnant differentiates into the nucleus pulposus — the gelatinous central portion of the intervertebral disc. The sclerotome forms the annulus fibrosus (the outer ring), not the nucleus pulposus. First Aid explicitly states: \"Notochord becomes nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc in adults.\"","boardPrep":"Step 1: Notochord → nucleus pulposus (FA direct quote, very high yield). Annulus fibrosus → sclerotome. In disc herniation, the nucleus pulposus (notochord remnant) ruptures through the annulus fibrosus (sclerotome-derived). COMLEX: Disc herniation at L4-L5 affects the L5 nerve root (exits below L5 pedicle); this root innervates the hypaxial muscles of the big toe dorsiflexion and lateral leg sensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — vertebral column re-segmentation creates nucleus pulposus from notochord remnant; annulus fibrosus from sclerotome"},{"id":"sdl1_q16","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"An embryology professor explains that each vertebra is derived from two adjacent somites rather than a single somite. A student asks why this re-segmentation arrangement exists. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of the re-segmentation process?","choices":{"A":"Re-segmentation distributes the notochord remnant evenly between each vertebral body, preventing compression of the spinal cord by ensuring the gelatinous disc material is centered in the intervertebral space.","B":"Re-segmentation allows Sonic Hedgehog from the notochord to diffuse uniformly across all vertebral segments, inducing symmetric ossification of the centrum, pedicles, and neural arches simultaneously.","C":"Re-segmentation is required for the primary ossification centers to appear concurrently in both the centrum and neural arch, because the two sub-segments provide the separate vascular territories needed for each center.","D":"Re-segmentation places the cleft that forms in the middle of each original somite between two vertebrae, where it becomes the intervertebral foramen through which spinal nerves exit to reach their myotomes","E":"Re-segmentation prevents the somites from fusing into a continuous bony column around the spinal cord by maintaining individual segmental identities through differential HOX gene expression gradients."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Re-segmentation is functionally critical because it ensures that the intervertebral foramen (the exit point for spinal nerves) sits adjacent to the middle of the original somite — not at the somite boundary. If vertebrae were simply derived from single somites without re-segmentation, spinal nerves would exit directly at the sclerotome-to-sclerotome boundary, which would be mechanically problematic. By re-segmenting, each vertebra straddles the junction of two somites, and the cleft in the middle of each original somite (the fissure of von Ebner) becomes the foramen through which the segmental nerve exits. This is why each spinal nerve serves the same-numbered myotome and dermatome. The notochord remnant distribution and ossification center vascularization are separate processes not dependent on re-segmentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Re-segmentation purpose = creates intervertebral foramina for spinal nerve exit. Each vertebra = caudal half of superior somite + cranial half of inferior somite. This is why each spinal nerve exits BELOW its named vertebra (except C nerves). COMLEX: Intervertebral foramina are the sites of nerve root compression in radiculopathy; somatic dysfunction of a vertebral unit affects the same segment that innervates that myotome and dermatome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — somite segmentation and HOX gene patterning establish vertebral identities before re-segmentation reorganizes them into the final vertebral column","whyNot":{"C":"The notochord remnant distribu"}},{"id":"sdl1_q17","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman at 20 weeks gestation has an ultrasound revealing a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine. The sac contains meninges filled with cerebrospinal fluid, but the spinal cord and nerve roots are not displaced and no neurologic deficits are identified. Maternal serum AFP was elevated on second-trimester screening. Which of the following correctly identifies this defect and its embryologic basis?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele, in which both CSF-filled meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through the posterior bony defect; neurologic deficits below the level of the lesion are invariably present due to cord involvement.","B":"Spina bifida occulta, a closed defect with no herniation of any meningeal or neural tissue; discovered incidentally on imaging and typically marked only by a tuft of hair or skin dimple with no sac formation.","C":"Meningocele, from failure of the spinous processes and laminae to close over the lumbar spinal cord; the sac contains only CSF-filled meninges because the neural tube closed normally and the cord stays in the canal.","D":"Rachischisis, the most severe form of spina bifida, in which neurulation completely fails and exposed neural tissue lies on the dorsal surface without any meningeal covering, sac, or skin closure.","E":"Encephalocele, occurring when the cranial neuropore fails to close by day 24 and allows brain parenchyma and meninges to herniate through an opening in the calvarium rather than the lumbar vertebral column."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meningocele is an open NTD in which the meninges (but not the neural tissue) herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. Because the spinal cord itself remains within the vertebral canal, neurologic function is typically preserved. This differs from myelomeningocele, where both meninges and spinal cord or cauda equina herniate, causing neurologic deficits below the lesion. Spina bifida occulta has no external sac; it is a subclinical bony defect. Rachischisis involves complete neurulation failure with no meningeal covering at all. Encephalocele involves the cranial, not lumbar, neuropore. Open NTDs are associated with elevated maternal serum AFP, because AFP leaks from the exposed neural tissue into the amniotic fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NTD classification — occulta (no herniation) → meningocele (meninges only, no deficits) → myelomeningocele (cord + meninges, has deficits) → rachischisis (most severe, no closure). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid supplementation before conception reduces NTD risk. COMLEX: SDL 8 covers spinal dysraphism in the pediatric context; Cranial neuropore closes ~day 24 and caudal neuropore ~day 27 (course text, SDL 1); caudal closure failure → spina bifida spectrum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — neural tube defects from failed caudal neuropore closure; folic acid prevents NTDs by supporting rapid cell division during neurulation","whyNot":{"E":"Encephalocele involves the cra"}},{"id":"sdl1_q18","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the stages of limb bud formation and vertebral column segmentation, including the processes of segmentation, re-segmentation.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A second-year medical student is reviewing the embryologic basis for the anatomical organization of limb muscles. She notes that the upper limb undergoes a 90-degree rotation by day 56 so that the elbow points posteriorly, while the lower limb rotates 90 degrees in the opposite direction. Which of the following correctly describes a consequence of the lower limb rotating opposite to the upper limb?","choices":{"A":"Both upper and lower limbs rotate in the same direction, which is why both the elbow and the knee point posteriorly in anatomical position and are both innervated by the posterior divisions of their respective plexuses.","B":"The lower limb rotation causes the dorsal muscle mass to remain posterior, keeping the knee extensors in the same relative position as the elbow extensors, making the lower limb innervation pattern identical to the upper limb.","C":"The opposite rotation of the lower limb causes the knee to point posteriorly just like the elbow, explaining why the quadriceps are innervated by the posterior division of the lumbosacral plexus rather than the femoral nerve.","D":"The lower limb rotation has no effect on muscle compartment arrangement because hypaxial muscle mass migration from the myotome determines final compartment position independently of any limb bud torsion.","E":"The lower limb extensors, which arise from the dorsal muscle mass, come to lie anteriorly after rotation, while the flexors lie posteriorly — the opposite of the upper limb, where dorsal muscle mass extensors remain posterior."},"correct":"E","explanation":"The upper limb rotates so that the thumb points laterally and the elbow points posteriorly, placing the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) posteriorly — innervated by posterior brachial plexus division (radial/axillary). The lower limb rotates in the opposite direction so that the big toe is medial and the knee points anteriorly. This places the dorsal muscle mass (extensors) anteriorly — the quadriceps and pretibial muscles. This is why the \"extensors\" of the lower limb are on the front and still innervated by a posterior-division nerve (the femoral nerve), even though they now lie on the front of the limb. The lower limb flexors (hamstrings, calf) remain posterior, innervated by the tibial nerve (anterior division). Timing differs by source: the course text places the rotation by day 56, and Langman (Ch 12) places it in the seventh week, with each limb turning about 90 degrees.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upper limb: dorsal mass = posterior extensors = radial nerve (posterior division). Lower limb: dorsal mass = anterior extensors = femoral nerve (posterior division; the compartment moved, the division did not). Opposite rotation = inverted compartment positions. COMLEX: This explains dermatome \"barber pole\" arrangement on the lower limb — skin innervation twists due to limb rotation, producing spiral dermatomal patterns.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — dorsal and ventral muscle mass innervation by posterior vs anterior plexus divisions; limb rotation determines final compartment position","whyNot":{"A":"The lower limb rotates in the ","B":"This places the dorsal muscle ","C":"The lower limb rotates in the "}},{"id":"sdl1_q19","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 22-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. Imaging reveals a midshaft humerus fracture. On examination he cannot extend the wrist, cannot extend the fingers, and has diminished sensation over the dorsum of the hand and lateral forearm. Which embryologic muscle mass is denervated by this injury and what is its developmental origin?","choices":{"A":"The ventral muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates anteriorly to form the flexor and adductor compartment and is innervated by the median and ulnar nerves, branches of the anterior division of the brachial plexus.","B":"The epaxial muscle compartment, which derives from the medial myotome and forms the intrinsic back muscles innervated by posterior rami; its injury causes paraspinal atrophy rather than wrist drop or dorsal hand sensory loss.","C":"The hypaxial prevertebral division, which forms muscles along the anterior vertebral column and is innervated by anterior rami; injury to this group causes neck flexion weakness rather than distal upper extremity motor deficit.","D":"The dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb, which migrates posteriorly to form the extensor compartment and is innervated by the radial nerve, a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus.","E":"The dorsal muscle mass of the lower limb, which forms the extensor compartment of the thigh and leg innervated by the femoral and common fibular nerves; this group is unrelated to upper extremity function."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial nerve palsy from a midshaft humerus fracture is the classic presentation of \"wrist drop\" with associated loss of finger extension and diminished dorsal hand sensation. The radial nerve is a branch of the posterior division of the brachial plexus, which innervates all muscles derived from the dorsal muscle mass of the upper limb. During embryogenesis, the dorsal muscle mass migrates to the posterior compartment as the limb rotates and differentiates into all extensor muscles of the arm, forearm, and hand. The ventral muscle mass forms flexors and adductors, innervated by the anterior division (median, ulnar, musculocutaneous nerves). Epaxial muscles are intrinsic back muscles and are not affected by a peripheral nerve injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus fracture = radial nerve injury = wrist drop + loss of finger extension + diminished lateral forearm/dorsal hand sensation (from superficial radial nerve). Radial nerve = posterior division = dorsal muscle mass embryologic origin. COMLEX: \"Saturday night palsy\" = radial nerve compression at the spiral groove (same nerve, different mechanism); somatic dysfunction at C7-C8 vertebral levels may reproduce radial nerve symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — dorsal muscle mass = posterior division brachial plexus; AER and somite myotome migration together establish limb muscle compartments"},{"id":"sdl1_q20","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman develops difficulty bringing her right thigh toward the midline after a pelvic lymph node dissection. Examination shows weak hip adduction and reduced sensation over a small area of the medial thigh; knee extension and hip abduction are normal. From which embryologic muscle mass did the weakened muscles develop?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal muscle mass of the lower limb, which migrated along the posterior side of the developing limb and is supplied by the gluteal, femoral, and common fibular nerves","B":"The ventral muscle mass of the lower limb, which migrated along the anterior side of the developing limb to form flexors and adductors on the obturator and tibial nerves","C":"The epaxial division of the myotome, which stayed close to the vertebral column to form the deep back muscles supplied segmentally by the posterior rami of the spinal nerves","D":"The prevertebral hypaxial division, which remained along the anterior surface of the lumbar vertebrae to form the psoas group supplied directly by the lumbar anterior rami","E":"The lateral plate mesoderm of the somatopleure, which formed the limb musculature in place without migration and receives its supply from the lumbosacral plexus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weak adduction with medial thigh numbness after pelvic surgery points to the obturator nerve, which supplies the adductor compartment. In the course text, hypaxial cells entering each limb bud split into a dorsal mass that forms extensors and a ventral mass that forms flexors and adductors; in the lower limb the ventral mass is supplied by the obturator and tibial nerves. The dorsal mass of the lower limb takes the gluteal, femoral, and common fibular nerves, and knee extension (femoral) and hip abduction (superior gluteal) are spared. Epaxial muscles are the intrinsic back muscles on posterior rami. The prevertebral hypaxial division forms muscles along the front of the vertebrae and would not explain an adductor deficit. Lateral plate mesoderm forms the limb bones and connective tissue; limb muscle comes from migrating myotome cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lower limb dorsal mass = extensors (gluteal, femoral, common fibular nerves); ventral mass = flexors/adductors (obturator, tibial nerves). Obturator nerve injury → weak adduction + medial thigh sensory loss. COMLEX: Obturator nerve injury is a known complication of pelvic lymph node dissection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — lower limb rotation brings the dorsal-mass extensors to the anterior thigh"},{"id":"sdl1_q21","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the differentiation of mesodermal cells into specific muscle types and the development of muscular tissues, including the critical periods in this process.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A researcher studying fetal skeletal muscle development collects biopsies at different gestational ages. She observes that the myotubes have begun producing myofilaments but their nuclei remain centrally located. By the end of the third month, cross-striations appear. She further notes that fetal movement begins around the third month of development. Which developmental event is most closely associated with the onset of fetal movement at this time?","choices":{"A":"Completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of fetal joint capsules by about the third month of development, allowing myotubes with forming myofilaments to move the joints for the first time.","B":"The first appearance of myofilaments in the early myotubes, which immediately generates sufficient contractile force for fetal movement before synovial joint cavities have fully formed or filled with fluid.","C":"The peripheral migration of muscle fiber nuclei as myofibrils accumulate, which is a histological marker of fiber maturation rather than the event that allows the fetal joints to be mobilized in the third month.","D":"The innervation of the myotome by anterior rami of the spinal cord at four weeks of development, which enables immediate coordinated muscle contractions upon completion of neurulation and axon outgrowth.","E":"The formation of secondary ossification centers in the epiphyses during the first two years of postnatal life, which allows load-bearing and initiates the muscle contractions required for coordinated movement."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fetal movement begins around the third month of intrauterine life (approximately 12 weeks), which correlates with the completion of synovial fluid secretion by the synovial membrane of joint capsules — an event that occurs by the end of the third month. Before this, joint cavities exist anatomically but the synovial layer has not yet begun secreting adequate fluid for smooth joint movement. Myofilaments appear earlier, but the ability to actually mobilize joints requires the mechanical prerequisite of functional synovial joints. Peripheral migration of the nuclei is a histological marker of fiber maturation, not the event that makes joint movement possible. Motor axons reach the myotome early, but coordinated movement requires both mature neuromuscular junctions and functional joints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synovial joint development is complete by the third month, enabling fetal movement. Oligohydramnios limits fetal movement and can cause contractures — e.g., talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) from restricted joint mobilization. COMLEX: Restricted fetal movement is a documented cause of positional deformities; this connects to SDL 1 Obj 4 (clubfoot from oligohydramnios) and SDL 8 (DDH from restricted hip movement).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — synovial joint development from loose mesenchyme by the 3rd month enables the functional use of muscles that were formed from myotome differentiation","whyNot":{"B":"Myofilaments appear earlier, b","D":"Motor axons reach the myotome "}},{"id":"sdl1_q22","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for short stature. He has shortened extremities relative to a normal-length trunk, a prominent forehead, midface hypoplasia, and trident-shaped hands. His parents are of normal stature. Lumbar spine radiographs show decreased interpedicular distance. Neurological examination is normal. Which of the following best explains why this child is at risk for spinal cord compression?","choices":{"A":"Increased growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary stimulates IGF-1-driven vertebral body growth asymmetrically, causing anterior osteophyte formation that narrows the spinal canal from the front.","B":"A COMP gene mutation disrupts chondrocyte survival during endochondral ossification, causing early disc degeneration and progressive posterior disc herniation that compresses the cord at multiple levels.","C":"Premature estrogen rise relative to testosterone during early childhood accelerates closure of vertebral growth plates, shortening vertebral bodies and narrowing the spinal canal before age five.","D":"The gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at all growth plates, including those that contribute to vertebral body height and the growth of the neural arches, producing a small spinal canal that can compress the cord.","E":"Failure of secondary ossification centers to appear in the annular epiphyses at puberty results in incomplete vertebral height and a congenitally narrow canal that worsens progressively during adolescence."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Achondroplasia from FGFR3 gain-of-function inhibits endochondral ossification at all growth plates, including those of the vertebral bodies and neural arches. In the lumbar spine, this produces a characteristically narrow interpedicular distance (the distance between the pedicles on AP imaging), creating a congenitally small spinal canal. Even minor disc pathology or osteophyte formation in adulthood can cause cord or cauda equina compression in these patients. This explains why spinal stenosis is a significant adult complication of achondroplasia. Growth hormone excess (gigantism/acromegaly) causes increased bone size via IGF-1 and would not cause a congenitally narrow canal. COMP mutations produce pseudoachondroplasia with joint laxity as the prominent feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Achondroplasia complications: (1) spinal stenosis from narrow canal; (2) sleep apnea from foramen magnum narrowing; (3) intelligence normal (membranous ossification unaffected). FGFR3 = constitutive activation = inhibits chondrocytes (gain-of-function paradox). COMLEX: Foramen magnum stenosis in achondroplasia can cause sudden death in infancy from cervical cord compression — an urgent pediatric complication requiring decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — endochondral ossification in growth plates driven by chondrocyte proliferation; FGFR3 normally limits growth plate activity, so gain-of-function causes premature closure","whyNot":{"B":"COMP mutations produce pseudoa"}},{"id":"sdl1_q23","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve stays on her usual oral anticoagulant through the first trimester. Her newborn has a hypoplastic nasal bridge, short limbs, and stippled epiphyses on radiographs. Which statement best explains these findings and the drug choice that would have avoided them?","choices":{"A":"Warfarin crosses the placenta and disrupts fetal bone and cartilage formation; heparin, which does not cross the placenta, is the anticoagulant used in pregnancy","B":"Heparin crosses the placenta and causes bleeding into the fetal growth plates; warfarin, which does not cross the placenta, is the anticoagulant used in pregnancy","C":"Warfarin blocks FGF8 release from the apical ectodermal ridge, and stopping it at the end of the first trimester would have restored normal limb outgrowth","D":"The drug acted in the first two weeks after fertilization, when the embryo is most prone to organ-specific skeletal defects from a maternal medication","E":"A new gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation explains the short limbs, because drugs taken during organogenesis do not disturb fetal bone or cartilage growth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists warfarin among the teratogens that affect the musculoskeletal system (\"abnormal/reduced bone growth\", SDL 1 abnormal-development slides), and First Aid (p632) gives the full pattern: bone and cartilage deformities such as stippled epiphyses and nasal and limb hypoplasia, plus optic nerve atrophy and cerebral hemorrhage. Heparin does not cross the placenta, so it is the anticoagulant used during pregnancy (FA p632); saying heparin crosses and warfarin does not reverses the placental transfer. FGF8 from the apical ectodermal ridge drives limb outgrowth, and its loss causes amelia (course SDL 1 text); no source ties warfarin to that pathway. Exposure before implantation has an all-or-none effect, and the embryo is most susceptible to structural defects during organogenesis, before week 8 (FA p632). A gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation causes achondroplasia, but it does not account for stippled epiphyses and nasal hypoplasia after a known drug exposure, and teratogens clearly can affect bone, as warfarin shows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warfarin embryopathy = bone and cartilage deformities (stippled epiphyses, nasal and limb hypoplasia), optic nerve atrophy, cerebral hemorrhage; use heparin in pregnancy because it does not cross the placenta. Before implantation = all-or-none; organogenesis (before week 8) = structural defects. COMLEX: Other musculoskeletal teratogens on the course slides: valproic acid and NSAIDs (spina bifida), leflunomide (limb and vertebral malformations), clindamycin (musculoskeletal hypoplasia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — teratogens act during organogenesis; warfarin, valproic acid, leflunomide, NSAIDs and clindamycin are the course's MSK teratogens","whyNot":{"B":"Heparin does not cross the pla","D":"Exposure before implantation h"}},{"id":"sdl1_q24","sdlNumber":1,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline and explain common abnormal musculoskeletal development and discuss the role of teratogens during critical periods of embryogenesis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A newborn male has a fluid-filled sac protruding from the lumbar spine at the L3-L4 level. The sac contains meninges, CSF, and displaced spinal cord with nerve roots. Lower extremity motor and sensory deficits are present below L3, and bladder and bowel function are absent. The mother had taken no prenatal vitamins. Maternal AFP was elevated on second-trimester screening. Which of the following best describes the embryologic event that failed and the preventable risk factor?","choices":{"A":"Failure of cranial neuropore closure by day 24 caused the neural tube defect; folate deficiency is the risk factor, but the resulting defect is anencephaly with absent forebrain rather than a lumbar spinal cord anomaly.","B":"Failed re-segmentation of the lumbar sclerotome somites produced fused vertebrae that compressed the spinal cord; iron deficiency anemia is the preventable risk factor associated with this form of spinal dysraphism.","C":"Failure of caudal neuropore closure by day 27 allowed sclerotomal mesenchyme to be excluded from the posterior arch, producing a myelomeningocele; folate deficiency is the preventable maternal risk factor.","D":"Failure of apoptosis in the lumbar interdigital mesenchyme caused soft tissue bridging over the developing neural tube, preventing normal spinous process formation and creating the fluid-filled posterior sac.","E":"Failed secondary ossification center development in the lumbar vertebral annular epiphyses allowed spinal cord herniation during the pubertal growth spurt; vitamin D deficiency is the relevant preventable risk factor."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Myelomeningocele is a severe open form of spinal dysraphism (only rachischisis is more severe), occurring when both meninges and spinal cord or nerve roots herniate through a posterior vertebral defect. The embryologic failure is incomplete closure of the caudal neuropore by approximately day 27 of development, which prevents the sclerotomal mesenchyme from migrating posterior to the neural tube to form the spinous processes and laminae. The exposed, unfused neural tissue and meninges herniate posteriorly. Maternal folate deficiency during the periconception period is the primary preventable risk factor; supplementation with 400 mcg/day starting at least 1 month before conception (Langman Ch 18) reduces NTD risk significantly. Cranial neuropore closure failure causes anencephaly. Re-segmentation failure causes vertebral fusion, not NTD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelomeningocele = meninges + neural tissue herniate; neurologic deficits below the level. Caudal neuropore closes day 27; cranial closes day 24 (cranial closes first). AFP elevated in open NTDs. Folic acid: supplement 1 month prior to conception (FA). COMLEX: Myelomeningocele at L3-L4 → paraplegia, neurogenic bladder/bowel; management includes early surgical closure to prevent infection and further neural damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — caudal neuropore closure by day 27 is required for sclerotomal mesenchyme to form posterior vertebral elements; failure prevents arch formation and exposes neural tube","whyNot":{"B":"Re-segmentation failure causes"}}],"4":[{"id":"sdl4_q01","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating knee pain uses a 5-step sequential diagnostic workflow. The first step is to determine whether pain is related to trauma. Which of the following correctly identifies the FIFTH and final step in this knee pain diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Determining whether the pain is related to acute trauma or a recent sports-related mechanism","B":"Ruling out a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis, malignancy, or fracture","C":"Assessing whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","D":"Evaluating whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or referred pain is responsible","E":"Determining whether the pain is localized and identifying the specific pain generator"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 5-step knee pain diagnostic workflow proceeds: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized and, if so, what structure is the pain generator? Identifying the specific anatomic pain generator is the final step that guides targeted treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A structured diagnostic workflow is essential — always rule out dangerous causes (infection, fracture, tumor) before assuming OA. COMLEX: Osteopathic palpatory exam can help localize the pain generator at step 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification","whyNot":{"B":"(2) Is a dangerous condition t","C":"(3) Is OA or arthritis respons"}},{"id":"sdl4_q02","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male presents with acute knee pain and swelling after falling from a ladder at work. He has point tenderness over the tibial plateau and cannot bear full weight. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step should the physician prioritize FIRST?","choices":{"A":"Proceeding directly to the arthritis differential and ordering an X-ray series for OA staging","B":"Evaluating for nerve pathology such as radiculopathy as the most likely cause of acute swelling","C":"Ruling out dangerous medical conditions including fracture, dislocation, or vascular injury","D":"Identifying the localized pain generator by palpating individual knee compartments systematically","E":"Initiating conservative management with PRICE therapy before completing the diagnostic workflow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Step 2 of the knee pain workflow — ruling out dangerous medical conditions — takes priority when trauma is identified in step 1. Fracture, dislocation, vascular injury, and compartment syndrome must be excluded before advancing to the arthritis or nerve pathology steps. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator assessment before ruling out these conditions is an error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules guide X-ray ordering after knee trauma (age >55, inability to bear weight, isolated patella tenderness, or fibular head tenderness). COMLEX: Always document neurovascular status distal to a suspected fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification","whyNot":{"A":"Proceeding to OA or localized ","B":"Fracture, dislocation, vascula"}},{"id":"sdl4_q03","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral knee pain worsening with activity and stiffness lasting 15 minutes in the morning. She has no trauma, no fever, and normal neurological exam. The physician has excluded dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. Which step in the knee pain diagnostic workflow should be evaluated NEXT?","choices":{"A":"Returning to step 1 to re-evaluate for an unrecognized traumatic mechanism of injury","B":"Evaluating whether osteoarthritis or another type of arthritis is the underlying cause","C":"Skipping to step 5 and identifying the localized pain generator without further workup","D":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize the meniscal and ligamentous anatomy in detail","E":"Referring to neurology to rule out lumbar radiculopathy as the source of bilateral knee pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding dangerous conditions (step 2) and nerve pathology (step 4), the 5-step workflow directs the clinician to step 3: evaluating for OA or another arthritis. The clinical features — bilateral distribution, activity-related worsening, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and no trauma — are classic for OA and fit perfectly within this step. An MRI of meniscal and ligament anatomy belongs to finding a local pain generator, not to the arthritis step, where X-ray is the gold standard and MRI is kept for complex cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA clinical criteria include age ≥45, usage-related pain in one or few joints, and morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction of the knee may accompany OA and can be treated with OMT as adjunctive therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — knee pain workflow: 5 sequential questions from trauma to localized pain generator identification"},{"id":"sdl4_q04","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female soccer player plants her foot and receives a direct lateral blow to her knee, creating a valgus force. She immediately collapses with a \"pop,\" develops hemarthrosis within hours, and has medial joint line tenderness, positive anterior drawer, and positive valgus stress test with laxity at 30 degrees. This presentation is MOST consistent with which combination of injuries?","choices":{"A":"PCL, LCL, and lateral meniscus — the unhappy triad variant from a posterior hyperflexion mechanism","B":"ACL, PCL, and lateral meniscus — multiligamentous injury from a combined valgus and rotational force","C":"ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus — the classic unhappy triad from lateral valgus force on a planted foot","D":"MCL, LCL, and popliteal ligament — posterolateral corner injury from combined varus and hyperextension","E":"Patellar tendon, ACL, and medial collateral ligament — extensor mechanism disruption from deceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The unhappy triad consists of ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus injury caused by a lateral valgus force with the foot planted — the exact mechanism described. The anterior drawer sign indicates ACL disruption, valgus stress laxity confirms MCL injury, and medial joint line tenderness suggests medial meniscal involvement. First Aid notes that lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial in conjunction with ACL+MCL injury, but the classic teaching triad remains ACL+MCL+medial meniscus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus (classic); First Aid notes lateral meniscus is more frequently co-injured. Lachman test (knee at 30°) is more sensitive than anterior drawer for ACL. COMLEX: Valgus force is the classic mechanism for this CP8 knee injury pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q05","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old rugby player falls directly onto his flexed right knee with his ankle plantarflexed. He develops posterolateral knee pain and swelling. Examination reveals a positive posterior sag sign and 8mm of posterior tibial glide on the posterior drawer test at 90 degrees of knee flexion. Which ligament is most likely injured and what is its primary function?","choices":{"A":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which resists anterior translation of the tibia on the femur","B":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which resists posterior translation of the tibia on the femur","C":"Medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus stress and medial compartment widening","D":"Lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus stress and posterolateral corner instability","E":"Popliteofibular ligament, which resists external tibial rotation and posterior subluxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PCL resists posterior tibial translation on the femur. The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall onto a flexed knee with a plantarflexed ankle, or a dashboard injury (anterior force to the proximal tibia with the knee flexed). Positive posterior sag sign and posterior drawer test confirm PCL disruption. The mechanism and findings rule out ACL (which would show anterior drawer/Lachman), MCL (valgus stress), and LCL (varus stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL mnemonic — medial femoral condyle to POSTERIOR tibia. PCL injury: posterior sag + posterior drawer at 90°. MOI: fall on flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle OR dashboard injury (MVA). COMLEX: PCL rarely injured in isolation; assess for posterolateral corner co-injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus","whyNot":{"A":"The mechanism and findings rul"}},{"id":"sdl4_q06","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old football player sustains a valgus blow to the knee. Examination shows medial joint tenderness, laxity of 7mm on valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion, and a firm endpoint. There is no laxity at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. Which MCL injury grade does this represent, and what structures are most likely intact?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1 injury with laxity under 5mm and a solid endpoint, indicating microscopic fiber disruption only","B":"Grade 3 injury with laxity over 10mm and no perceptible endpoint, indicating complete MCL rupture","C":"Grade 4 injury with complete bony avulsion from the medial femoral condyle requiring urgent surgery","D":"Grade 2 injury, 5-9mm laxity with a firm endpoint: a partial MCL tear, deep capsule and ACL intact","E":"Grade 3 injury with laxity at both 0 and 30 degrees, indicating complete MCL and ACL co-disruption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MCL grading: Grade 1 = <5mm laxity, solid endpoint; Grade 2 = 5-9mm laxity, firm endpoint (partial tear); Grade 3 = ≥10mm laxity, no endpoint (complete rupture). Laxity at 30° isolates the superficial MCL. Laxity at 0° indicates deep capsular and ACL involvement as well. This patient has 7mm laxity with a firm endpoint = Grade 2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCL valgus stress test at 30° = superficial MCL; laxity at 0° adds deep MCL and possibly ACL involvement. Grade 2 treated conservatively. Pellegrini-Stieda lesion = chronic MCL calcification on X-ray from prior injury. COMLEX: The course slides make two \"most\" statements: the ACL slide calls the ACL \"the most injured knee ligament\" (SDL 4 p4), and the MCL slide says ligamentous injuries are 40% of all knee injuries and \"MCL is the most common\" (p17; 7.9% of all knee injuries; high-risk sports football, hockey, rugby, wrestling). Moore (Ch 7) says only that ligament sprain is the most common knee injury in contact sports.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL: Lachman/anterior drawer; PCL: posterior sag/posterior drawer; MCL: valgus stress; LCL: varus stress; unhappy triad = ACL + MCL + medial meniscus"},{"id":"sdl4_q07","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old male basketball player twists his planted knee during a game, immediately feeling pain at the medial joint line followed by intermittent locking and giving-out episodes. McMurray test performed with external rotation and valgus force at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and medial pain. Which meniscus is injured and how does the McMurray test confirm this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral meniscus, because internal rotation with varus force stresses the lateral compartment reproducing lateral symptoms","B":"Posterior horn of the lateral meniscus, because external rotation with flexion compresses the posterolateral space","C":"Both menisci equally, because the McMurray test is non-specific and stresses medial and lateral simultaneously","D":"Anterior horn of the medial meniscus, because valgus stress preferentially loads the anterior attachment site","E":"Medial meniscus, because external rotation with valgus force stresses the medial compartment and reproduces medial pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The McMurray test mnemonic: LIME — Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus = Medial meniscus. External rotation stresses the medial compartment; a click or pain with this maneuver implicates the medial meniscus. Medial meniscal tears are more common than lateral (medial > lateral), occurring at 61/100,000 in the general population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: McMurray LIME mnemonic — Internal+varus=Lateral, External+valgus=Medial. Locking = bucket-handle tear. Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving-out) are indications for surgical referral. COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000"},{"id":"sdl4_q08","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following patient profiles is MOST consistent with a degenerative meniscal tear rather than a traumatic meniscal tear?","choices":{"A":"A 16-year-old female soccer player who develops acute swelling and locking after a contact pivot injury","B":"A 55-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis who develops medial pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair","C":"A 22-year-old wrestler who sustains acute medial joint line pain after a takedown with rotational force","D":"A 30-year-old marathon runner who develops sudden lateral pain after a varus twisting fall on a trail","E":"A 19-year-old basketball player with prior ACL reconstruction who develops locking with pivoting drills"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (typically over 40) with pre-existing arthritis, often with minimal or no significant trauma. Risk factors include male gender, age >40, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis. Traumatic tears occur in younger individuals through acute twisting mechanisms. The 55-year-old with OA and minimal-trauma onset fits the degenerative profile exactly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tears = horizontal cleavage pattern, older patients, OA background, minimal trauma. Traumatic tears = vertical/longitudinal (bucket-handle), younger athletes, acute mechanism. COMLEX: OMT is not appropriate for acute bucket-handle tears causing mechanical locking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000","whyNot":{"A":"Traumatic tears occur in young","C":"Traumatic tears occur in young","D":"Traumatic tears occur in young"}},{"id":"sdl4_q09","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old male has been managed conservatively for 8 weeks for a meniscal tear with PRICE therapy and physical therapy. He continues to experience his knee locking during daily activities, cannot fully extend the joint, and reports recurrent giving-out episodes. Which of the following findings BEST justifies surgical referral at this point?","choices":{"A":"A positive Thessaly maneuver in the clinic without any subjective sense of instability or locking during activities","B":"Mild medial joint line tenderness reproducible with deep squatting but no loss of full range of motion","C":"Persistent true locking and giving-out that have not responded to an adequate trial of conservative treatment","D":"MRI showing a Grade 1 signal change within the posterior horn without surface disruption or extension","E":"Occasional clicking with knee flexion beyond 90 degrees without effusion or functional limitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgical referral for meniscal tears is indicated when: (1) mechanical symptoms persist (locking, giving-out), (2) multiligamentous pathology is present, or (3) conservative measures have failed. True locking — inability to fully extend the knee — typically indicates a displaced bucket-handle tear requiring arthroscopic intervention. A positive provocative test without mechanical symptoms alone does not meet the threshold. Joint line tenderness with full range of motion is not a mechanical symptom; the course's referral triggers are mechanical symptoms, multiligamentous pathology and cases that do not settle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications = mechanical symptoms (locking/giving-out), multiligamentous instability, or failure of conservative care. True locking = bucket-handle tear. Initial management = PRICE + PT + avoidance of deep squatting and pivoting. COMLEX: Role of regenerative injections (PRP) is being studied for degenerative meniscal tears.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — McMurray: external rotation + valgus = medial meniscus; internal rotation + varus = lateral meniscus; medial > lateral incidence; 61/100,000","whyNot":{"A":"A positive provocative test wi"}},{"id":"sdl4_q10","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old soccer player sustains a pivoting (internal rotation) injury and develops acute hemarthrosis. AP X-ray shows a small avulsed bone fragment at the anterolateral margin of the proximal tibia, just below the lateral tibial plateau. Which fracture does this represent and what associated injury is strongly implied?","choices":{"A":"Pellegrini-Stieda lesion, a calcification at the medial femoral condyle indicating prior chronic MCL injury","B":"Arcuate sign, a small fibular head avulsion indicating injury to the posterolateral corner structures","C":"Osgood-Schlatter lesion, fragmentation at the tibial tubercle from patellar tendon traction in adolescents","D":"Segond fracture, an anterolateral ligament avulsion from the lateral tibia that strongly implies an ACL tear","E":"Tibial plateau impaction fracture, indicating articular surface depression from axial loading of the lateral condyle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Segond fracture is an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament (ALL) from the lateral tibial plateau. It appears as a small osseous fragment lateral to the tibial plateau on AP X-ray and is highly specific for concurrent ACL tear — so much so that a Segond fracture is considered pathognomonic for ACL disruption until proven otherwise. The arcuate sign is a posterolateral corner fracture of the fibular head. Pellegrini-Stieda = chronic MCL calcification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segond fracture = lateral tibial rim avulsion = ACL tear until proven otherwise. X-ray finding: small bony fragment lateral to proximal tibia on AP view. COMLEX: All traumatic knee injuries require neurovascular assessment distal to the injury before imaging workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation"},{"id":"sdl4_q11","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Knee Rules, which of the following patient presentations mandates knee radiography after acute trauma?","choices":{"A":"Inability to bear weight for four steps, age over 55 years, or isolated tenderness at the fibular head or patella","B":"Any knee pain occurring after a sports injury regardless of the ability to ambulate after the event","C":"Presence of joint effusion or hemarthrosis regardless of weight-bearing status at the time of presentation","D":"Positive Lachman test suggesting ligamentous laxity in the absence of bony point tenderness","E":"Prior history of knee surgery or previous fracture regardless of current mechanism of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ottawa Knee Rules indicate X-ray if: (1) age ≥55, (2) isolated tenderness at the fibular head, (3) isolated patellar tenderness with no other bony tenderness, (4) inability to flex to 90°, or (5) inability to bear weight (4 steps) both immediately and in the ED. Effusion alone, prior surgery, and ligamentous laxity without the above criteria do not mandate imaging per the Ottawa Rules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ottawa Knee Rules — validated decision rule to reduce unnecessary knee X-rays after trauma. COMLEX: These rules apply to adults; pediatric knee trauma generally warrants lower threshold for imaging given growth plate vulnerability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation","whyNot":{"C":"Effusion alone, prior surgery,","E":"Effusion alone, prior surgery,"}},{"id":"sdl4_q12","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male sustains a tibial plateau fracture during a football game. X-ray confirms the fracture but cannot characterize potential soft tissue injuries. Which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for evaluating concurrent meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption, and chondral injury in this setting?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the knee, which provides the best characterization of articular cartilage and synovial tissue","B":"Bone scan, which detects areas of increased metabolic activity corresponding to ligamentous microtrauma","C":"Diagnostic arthroscopy, which is the definitive assessment but carries procedural risk and is not first-line","D":"Weight-bearing X-ray series, which adequately characterizes all soft tissue injuries around the fracture site","E":"MRI, with superior soft tissue contrast for meniscal, ligamentous, and cartilage injury not seen on X-ray"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for evaluating soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligament disruption, and cartilage damage — that accompany knee fractures. CT excels at defining bony architecture and articular depression but is inferior to MRI for soft tissue. Bone scan is not used for acute soft tissue evaluation. Arthroscopy remains definitive but invasive; MRI guides surgical planning first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = study of choice for meniscal tears and ligament injuries. After tibial plateau fracture, MRI changes surgical plan in a high proportion of cases by revealing meniscal and ligamentous co-injuries. COMLEX: Always image before manipulating a post-fracture knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Segond fracture = anterolateral ligament avulsion = strong ACL tear marker; MRI is gold standard for concurrent soft tissue injury evaluation","whyNot":{"B":"Bone scan is not used for acut","C":"Arthroscopy remains definitive"}},{"id":"sdl4_q13","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Patellofemoral syndrome (PFS), commonly called \"runner's knee,\" is one of the most prevalent knee complaints in outpatient practice. Which of the following correctly describes the proportion of all knee problems that PFS represents in a typical outpatient clinic setting?","choices":{"A":"Fewer than 5% of all knee problems, primarily seen in elderly sedentary patients with significant comorbidities","B":"Approximately 8-10% of all knee problems, similar in incidence to lateral meniscal tears in athletes","C":"More than 60% of all knee problems, making it the most common musculoskeletal complaint overall","D":"Between 25 and 40% of all knee problems seen in outpatient clinical settings","E":"Exactly 15% of all knee problems, documented exclusively in female distance runners under age 30"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PFS accounts for 25-40% of all knee problems seen in clinic, making it one of the most common knee complaints in outpatient practice. Its true incidence is unknown due to under-reporting and variable diagnostic criteria. It can be traumatic (fall on flexed knee) or atraumatic (overuse). It is NOT exclusively seen in runners and affects all activity levels and ages.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS is extremely common; also called \"chondromalacia patella.\" Key clinical finding: pain with patellofemoral compression test and single-leg squat. Biomechanical drivers = dynamic valgus, quad weakness, hip weakness. COMLEX: PFS often has a component of somatic dysfunction of the patellofemoral articulation amenable to soft tissue OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver","whyNot":{"E":"It is NOT exclusively seen in "}},{"id":"sdl4_q14","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, prolonged sitting with knees bent, and single-leg squatting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Dynamic valgus is observed on single-leg squat. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the patellofemoral joint to reduce inflammatory-driven cartilage pain","B":"Immediate surgical referral for arthroscopic debridement of patellar articular cartilage surface changes","C":"Complete activity restriction for 3 months with cast immobilization to allow cartilage surface recovery","D":"Oral opioid analgesics as a bridge therapy while awaiting specialist evaluation for surgical management","E":"Physical therapy for quadriceps and posterior hip strengthening and hamstring flexibility to correct maltracking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Physical therapy is the cornerstone first-line treatment for PFS. The program targets: (1) quadriceps strengthening for patellar stabilization, (2) posterior hip strengthening (gluteus medius/maximus) to reduce dynamic valgus, and (3) hamstring flexibility. Dynamic valgus is a key biomechanical driver of patellofemoral maltracking and increased contact pressure. Surgery and injections are not first-line for PFS. Cast immobilization and complete rest are not part of the plan, which is active physical therapy to correct biomechanics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS first-line = PT with quad + hip strengthening. No role for surgery without prior PT failure. Dynamic valgus from hip abductor weakness is the dominant biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellar taping (McConnell technique) may provide short-term symptom relief as an adjunct to PT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery and injections are not","B":"Surgery and injections are not"}},{"id":"sdl4_q15","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old male recreational cyclist develops anterior knee pain reproducible with the patellofemoral compression test and aggravated by prolonged knee flexion (the \"movie sign\"). Which mechanism BEST explains the pathophysiology of pain in patellofemoral syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Synovitis from repetitive friction between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral epicondyle during cycling","B":"Abnormal patellar cartilage contact pressure against the femoral trochlear groove caused by patellar maltracking","C":"Posterior knee capsule inflammation from excessive tibial internal rotation during the power phase of cycling","D":"Tibial stress fracture from repetitive axial load producing referred anterior knee pain from periosteal irritation","E":"Avascular necrosis of the patellar apex from stress-related disruption of blood supply during high-cadence cycling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PFS results from abnormal patellofemoral contact pressure caused by patellar maltracking — the patella does not glide smoothly through the trochlear groove. Increased contact pressure on articular cartilage produces pain, particularly with activities that increase patellofemoral loading (stairs, squatting, prolonged sitting = \"movie sign\"). The other choices describe separate entities: IT band syndrome, popliteal pathology, tibial stress fracture, and AVN.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Movie sign\" = pain after prolonged sitting with knees flexed (patellofemoral contact pressure increases with knee flexion). Dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and amplifies contact pressure. COMLEX: PFS in runners often presents with bilateral symptoms unlike meniscal or ligamentous injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome = 25-40% of knee problems; PT with quad and hip strengthening is first-line; dynamic valgus is key biomechanical driver","whyNot":{"A":"The other choices describe sep","C":"The other choices describe sep","D":"The other choices describe sep","E":"The other choices describe sep"}},{"id":"sdl4_q16","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old tile installer presents with fluctuant, erythematous swelling directly overlying the patella after years of kneeling on hard surfaces. He has low-grade fever. Aspiration reveals cloudy, purulent fluid and the Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which is the MOST appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Compression wrap and elevation alone without aspiration or antibiotics, since bursitis resolves with PRICE therapy","B":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the prepatellar bursa to reduce acute inflammation and prevent recurrence","C":"Colchicine therapy for presumed gout since urate crystals can cause purulent-appearing fluid and elevated WBC","D":"Immediate surgical bursectomy without prior antibiotic therapy given the occupational exposure and purulent aspirate","E":"Aspiration of the bursal fluid combined with systemic antibiotics targeting Staphylococcus aureus for septic prepatellar bursitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Septic prepatellar bursitis (positive Gram stain, fever, purulent aspirate) requires aspiration plus systemic antibiotics. Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus aureus, the most common causative organism. Corticosteroid injection into a septic bursa is contraindicated. Colchicine is for gout (urate crystals on aspirate, no organisms). Bursectomy is reserved for recurrent or refractory cases, not first-line. PRICE alone is insufficient for septic bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepatellar bursitis ddx — septic (fever, erythema, organisms) vs. gout (urate crystals) vs. mechanical (kneeling exposure, no fever). Septic = aspirate + antibiotics. \"Housemaid's knee\" = classic name for prepatellar bursitis from chronic kneeling. COMLEX: Distinguish intra-articular from extra-articular fluid collection before injecting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint","whyNot":{"D":"Bursectomy is reserved for rec"}},{"id":"sdl4_q17","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old female volleyball player sustains her first lateral patellar dislocation after a non-contact planting and pivoting maneuver. Reduction is achieved in the emergency department. MRI confirms disruption of the medial patellofemoral ligament without osteochondral fracture. She has no prior history of patellar instability. What is the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical MPFL reconstruction regardless of dislocation episode history or recurrence risk","B":"Long-leg cast immobilization for 10 weeks before any rehabilitation or weightbearing is permitted","C":"PRICE therapy, physical therapy for quadriceps and hip strengthening, and a patellar stabilizing brace","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce hemarthrosis followed by early return to sport","E":"Lateral retinacular release as the primary surgical intervention for first-episode lateral dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First-episode patellar dislocation without osteochondral fracture or predisposing anatomic factors is managed conservatively with PRICE, PT (quad/hip strengthening), and patellar bracing. Surgical MPFL reconstruction is reserved for recurrent dislocations or when predisposing anatomic factors (trochlear dysplasia, high TT-TG distance) are present. Lateral retinacular release alone does not address the MPFL deficiency and is not standard first-line care. Prolonged cast immobilization and a steroid injection with early return to sport are not part of the course plan of PRICE, physical therapy and bracing, with surgery only if dislocation recurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL = primary medial restraint to lateral patellar displacement. MRI is the key imaging study post-reduction. Surgery is considered for recurrent dislocation or anatomic predisposition. COMLEX: Patellar instability is a CP8 joint dislocation condition; ensure documentation of ligamentous laxity as a predisposing factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint","whyNot":{"B":"Prolonged cast immobilization "}},{"id":"sdl4_q18","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When evaluating a patient with recurrent patellar subluxation using MRI, which structure is the PRIMARY imaging target for assessing the integrity of the medial patellar restraint system?","choices":{"A":"Posterior cruciate ligament, which acts as the primary medial stabilizer of the patellofemoral articulation","B":"Quadriceps tendon, which is assessed for longitudinal splitting and partial tearing in recurrent instability","C":"Lateral collateral ligament, which when torn allows unrestricted medial-to-lateral patellar displacement","D":"Medial patellofemoral ligament, which is the primary passive restraint preventing lateral displacement of the patella","E":"Anterior cruciate ligament, which indirectly stabilizes the patella by controlling tibial rotation at the knee"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary passive restraint against lateral patellar displacement, and the course lists MRI of MPFL integrity as the imaging step for patellar subluxation (SDL 4 slide p57). On MRI, MPFL injury appears as signal change or discontinuity along its course from the medial femoral condyle to the superomedial patella. PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps tendon do not serve as primary medial patellar restraints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MPFL runs from medial femoral epicondyle to superomedial patella. MPFL completely torn in over 50% of patellar dislocations, most often at the femoral attachment (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics). Merchant view (X-ray) = sunrise view for patellofemoral alignment. COMLEX: Ligamentous laxity as a systemic condition predisposes to recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — prepatellar bursitis: septic = aspirate + antibiotics, gout = aspirate + colchicine; patellar subluxation: MPFL is primary lateral restraint","whyNot":{"A":"PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps ","C":"PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps ","E":"PCL, ACL, LCL, and quadriceps "}},{"id":"sdl4_q19","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is best described pathophysiologically as which of the following structures?","choices":{"A":"A true synovial cyst arising from a congenital remnant of the embryonic knee joint capsule in adults","B":"A ganglion cyst forming from periarticular connective tissue without communication to the joint space","C":"A hematoma that organizes into a posterior fluid collection after posterior cruciate ligament rupture","D":"A lipoma within the popliteal fossa that compresses neurovascular structures during knee flexion","E":"A gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis that frequently communicates with the synovial cavity of the knee joint"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A popliteal cyst (Baker's cyst) is a gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis — specifically a fluid collection within the bursa located between the medial gastrocnemius and semimembranosus tendons. In adults it typically communicates with the synovial cavity, allowing joint fluid to accumulate in the bursa when intra-articular pressure increases. This distinguishes it from a true ganglion cyst (no synovial communication) or a hematoma (post-traumatic).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = popliteal fluid in gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa, communicating with synovial space in adults. Causes include OA, RA, and meniscal tears. COMLEX: Rupture of a Baker's cyst can mimic DVT (\"pseudothrombophlebitis\") — ultrasound differentiates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70","whyNot":{"C":"This distinguishes it from a t"}},{"id":"sdl4_q20","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Popliteal cysts demonstrate a bimodal age distribution with two distinct incidence peaks. Which of the following correctly identifies BOTH peak age ranges and their corresponding pathophysiological contexts?","choices":{"A":"Ages 1-3 years in infants due to congenital joint laxity, and ages 50-60 due to post-arthroplasty bursitis","B":"Ages 12-18 in adolescent athletes due to meniscal overuse, and ages 40-55 due to early inflammatory arthritis","C":"Ages 20-35 in young adults from acute knee ligament injuries, and ages 65-80 from post-surgical adhesions","D":"Ages 4-7 in children with non-communicating cysts, and ages 35-70 in adults with degenerative joint disease","E":"Ages 8-12 in school-age children from sports overuse, and ages 60-75 from end-stage knee osteoarthritis only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Popliteal cysts have two incidence peaks: (1) ages 4-7, where the cyst typically does NOT communicate with the joint space (different pathoanatomy than adults) and frequently resolves spontaneously; (2) ages 35-70, where the cyst is associated with underlying intra-articular pathology (OA, RA, meniscal tears) and communicates with the synovial space. Treatment differs significantly between the two groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Children (4-7) — non-communicating, often resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or aspiration. Adults (35-70) — communicating, address underlying pathology. Baker's cyst rupture mimics DVT with calf pseudothrombophlebitis. COMLEX: Ultrasound is useful to guide aspiration and confirm communication with joint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70"},{"id":"sdl4_q21","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old male with advanced knee osteoarthritis presents with a large posterior knee mass and restricted flexion. Ultrasound confirms a popliteal cyst communicating with the synovial space. He has undergone two prior aspirations that resolved the cyst temporarily, but it returned within 3 months each time. Which represents the MOST effective long-term treatment strategy?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying osteoarthritis, since cyst recurrence reflects persistent intra-articular pathology","B":"Surgically excising the cyst wall through a posterior popliteal approach as the definitive long-term treatment","C":"Performing repeated aspiration every 3 months indefinitely to prevent symptomatic recurrence of the cyst","D":"Prescribing custom orthotics to reduce tibial loading and allow the cyst to reabsorb through offloading","E":"Aspirating the cyst with concurrent corticosteroid injection as the definitive non-surgical management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Popliteal cysts in adults recur after aspiration because the underlying cause — excess synovial fluid production from OA, RA, or meniscal tears — is not addressed. The cyst is a consequence, not a primary pathology. Effective treatment requires addressing the root cause. If OA is the driver, management follows OA guidelines (exercise, NSAIDs, IACS, eventually TKA). Aspirating the cyst without treating the source leads to predictable recurrence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baker's cyst = manifestation of intra-articular pathology. Treat the cause (OA, RA, meniscal tear). Aspiration alone = temporizing. COMLEX: This principle mirrors osteopathic philosophy — treat the primary dysfunction, not the compensatory finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Baker's cyst = gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursitis communicating with synovial space; bimodal peaks at ages 4-7 and 35-70","whyNot":{"C":"Aspirating the cyst without tr","E":"Aspirating the cyst without tr"}},{"id":"sdl4_q22","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the simplest clinical diagnostic criteria for knee osteoarthritis, a diagnosis can be established when a patient has persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, age of 45 years or older, and which additional criterion?","choices":{"A":"Elevated ESR above 40 mm/hr with synovial biopsy showing fibrocartilaginous pannus formation","B":"Morning stiffness lasting 30 minutes or less, without features of systemic inflammatory arthritis","C":"Positive rheumatoid factor with symmetric small joint involvement and periarticular osteopenia","D":"Positive ANA titer with malar rash, photosensitivity, and constitutional symptoms","E":"X-ray showing periarticular erosions with uniform joint space loss and cortical destruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The simplest OA diagnostic criteria are: (1) persistent usage-related joint pain in one or a few joints, (2) age ≥45 years, and (3) morning stiffness ≤30 minutes. This triad establishes the clinical diagnosis without requiring imaging or laboratory studies. Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SLE, vasculitis) is distinguished by morning stiffness > 1 hour (FA 2025; Bootcamp gives ~>30 min for RA), positive serology, systemic features, and erosive changes. A high ESR and pannus belong to inflammatory arthritis such as RA, not to OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA is a CLINICAL diagnosis — morning stiffness ≤30 min vs. RA (>1 hour). Usage-related pain = mechanical (worse with activity, better with rest). COMLEX: OA is the most common form of arthritis; CP8 named musculoskeletal condition for COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging","whyNot":{"A":"A high ESR and pannus belong t","C":"Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SL","D":"Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SL","E":"Inflammatory arthritis (RA, SL"}},{"id":"sdl4_q23","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to OARSI guidelines for non-surgical management of knee osteoarthritis, which of the following is classified as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus strength across ALL patient comorbidity profiles?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection as monotherapy without a concurrent structured exercise program","B":"Non-selective oral NSAIDs as the primary first-line treatment across all patients regardless of GI or CV history","C":"COX-2 selective inhibitors combined with intra-articular hyaluronic acid as universal first-line pharmacotherapy","D":"Duloxetine as the first-line agent in patients with bilateral knee OA given its central sensitization mechanism","E":"Structured land-based exercise programs combined with arthritis patient education as the foundation of OA management"},"correct":"E","explanation":"OARSI guidelines designate structured land-based exercise (strengthening, cardio, balance/neuromuscular training, OR mind-body exercise such as Tai Chi or yoga) combined with arthritis education as the CORE recommendation with the highest consensus and strength of evidence. This applies regardless of comorbidities. Pharmacologic options are layered based on comorbidity profile (GI, CV, frailty, widespread pain/depression). Duloxetine appears only as a lower-consensus (Level 2) conditional option for patients with widespread pain or depression, not as the core recommendation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI core = exercise + education. Topical NSAIDs preferred for GI comorbidity. IACS for symptomatic flares. IAHA conditionally recommended. COMLEX: Exercise is the most universally recommended intervention — OMT can complement structured exercise programs for OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging","whyNot":{"D":"Duloxetine appears only as a l"}},{"id":"sdl4_q24","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is considered the GOLD STANDARD for the initial diagnosis and staging of knee osteoarthritis, and which radiographic features are most diagnostically useful?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing X-ray, showing joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and subchondral cysts","B":"MRI of the knee, showing synovial thickening and early cartilage signal changes on T2-weighted fluid-sensitive sequences","C":"Ultrasound of the knee, showing hypoechoic effusion and osteophyte shadowing with dynamic joint assessment","D":"CT arthrogram, showing articular cartilage thinning and full-thickness chondral defects with bony detail","E":"DEXA scan, showing periarticular bone density reduction and cortical thinning in the affected compartments"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging. Key findings: joint space narrowing (most important — reflects cartilage loss), osteophytes (bony growths at joint margins), subchondral sclerosis (increased bone density beneath cartilage), and subchondral cysts. Weight-bearing is essential — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing. MRI is reserved for complex cases or when soft tissue evaluation is needed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA X-ray triad = joint space narrowing + osteophytes + subchondral sclerosis/cysts. Weight-bearing is critical. MRI is NOT first-line for OA. Merchant view assesses patellofemoral compartment alignment. COMLEX: X-ray finding of bone-on-bone OA indicates TKA may be appropriate when conservative measures fail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee OA: clinical diagnosis; OARSI core = structured exercise + arthritis education; weight-bearing X-ray is gold standard imaging","whyNot":{"B":"MRI is reserved for complex ca"}},{"id":"sdl4_q25","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old male presents with chronic knee pain. The physician begins the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow by obtaining a thorough history including pain characteristics, prior knee injuries, and family history. What is the CORRECT next step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow?","choices":{"A":"Immediately ordering MRI to characterize cartilage and synovial pathology before completing physical examination","B":"Performing a directed exam with range of motion, palpation for bony prominences, effusion, tenderness, crepitus","C":"Obtaining synovial fluid analysis to differentiate inflammatory from non-inflammatory arthritis before imaging","D":"Prescribing a therapeutic NSAID trial to confirm an inflammatory pain pattern before further workup","E":"Ordering nuclear medicine bone scan to detect early periarticular metabolic activity before X-ray"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The knee arthritis diagnostic workflow proceeds: history → physical examination → imaging. After history, the next step is physical examination including ROM, palpation for bony prominences and effusion, assessment of tenderness, and crepitus on motion. Only after completing history and exam does imaging follow, beginning with weight-bearing X-ray. MRI is not the first imaging study ordered in the arthritis workflow. An NSAID trial and a bone scan are not steps in the workflow, which moves from history to exam to imaging that starts with X-ray.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow = history → PE → weight-bearing X-ray → MRI (only if complex). Physical exam hallmarks of OA: crepitus, bony enlargement, restricted ROM, effusion. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam can identify somatic dysfunction contributing to painful gait adaptation in OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy","whyNot":{"A":"MRI is not the first imaging s","E":"An NSAID trial and a bone scan"}},{"id":"sdl4_q26","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has medial knee pain on stairs and squatting. A weight-bearing AP view taken with the knee extended shows a preserved medial joint space. The physician suspects that the cartilage loss lies on the posterior femoral condyle and the tibia. Which additional radiograph is most likely to reveal the arthritis?","choices":{"A":"A non-weight-bearing lateral view, because removing load lets the femur drop back and shows posterior condylar wear","B":"A Merchant view, because peering beneath the patella shows wear between the posterior femur and the tibial plateau","C":"A weight-bearing flexed-knee PA Rosenberg view, which rotates the worn posterior condyle opposite the bare tibia","D":"A non-weight-bearing AP view, because the relaxed joint opens and outlines the cartilage defect more clearly than standing","E":"Bilateral oblique views, because angling the beam profiles the posterior condyles that the AP view superimposes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course reading describes a common pattern in which cartilage is lost on the tibia and the posterior femoral condyle while the anterior femur keeps cartilage. On the extended weight-bearing AP the joint space still looks open. The weight-bearing flexed-knee PA view (Rosenberg or notch view) rotates the posterior bald spot opposite the bare tibia and reveals bone-on-bone contact. The lateral view gives the least helpful information of all the views. The Merchant view assesses the patellofemoral joint, not the tibiofemoral contact. A non-weight-bearing AP can make even a bone-on-bone joint look open because the surfaces relax apart. Oblique views belong to the emergency fracture series and are not the view used for this wear pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthritis X-ray series: weight-bearing AP, weight-bearing flexed PA (Rosenberg), Merchant, lateral. Non-weight-bearing films underestimate joint space loss. COMLEX: Order the arthritis series, not the emergency \"knee series,\" for office OA evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 6 — Merchant (sunrise) view for patellofemoral alignment","whyNot":{"A":"The lateral view gives the lea","E":"Oblique views belong to the em"}},{"id":"sdl4_q27","sdlNumber":4,"batch":1,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, in which clinical scenarios is MRI specifically indicated beyond the standard weight-bearing X-ray evaluation?","choices":{"A":"When the case is complex, soft tissue injury must be excluded, or cartilage status will change management","B":"As the routine initial imaging study for all patients over 60 presenting with knee pain and morning stiffness","C":"When the patient has classic OA risk factors and X-ray already demonstrates joint space narrowing bilaterally","D":"For annual monitoring of disease progression in all patients with established knee OA on stable treatment","E":"Only in patients under 40 presenting with unexplained monoarticular arthritis without X-ray changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow, MRI is indicated when: (1) the case is complex and X-ray is insufficient, (2) soft tissue injuries (meniscal, ligamentous) need to be ruled out, or (3) cartilage integrity assessment will change the clinical management plan. MRI is NOT the routine first imaging study — weight-bearing X-ray serves that role. It is also not indicated for routine monitoring of stable OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI is powerful but not cost-effective for routine OA monitoring. Reserve for complexity, pre-surgical planning, or when diagnosis is uncertain. Ultrasound can assess effusion and guide injection but is not the gold standard for OA diagnosis. COMLEX: Imaging should guide — not replace — the clinical examination and history in the OA workflow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 9 — knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-ray; MRI only for complex cases; OA is a clinical diagnosis not requiring biopsy"},{"id":"sdl4_q28","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with BMI of 34 presents with 4 months of right knee pain that is diffuse, worsening with prolonged standing, and associated with morning stiffness lasting 15 minutes. He denies trauma, fever, or radicular leg pain. Neurovascular exam is normal. X-ray shows medial compartment joint space narrowing. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, the physician determines that trauma, dangerous conditions, and nerve pathology have been excluded. Which of the following BEST represents the appropriate next diagnostic conclusion?","choices":{"A":"Peroneal nerve palsy should be evaluated given the occupational exposure to prolonged standing positions","B":"ACL tear should be considered given the diffuse knee pain and positive biomechanical stress on the medial compartment","C":"Osteoarthritis is the most likely cause, so proceed with an OA-directed assessment and OARSI-guided management","D":"Femoral nerve entrapment should be ruled out given the anterior thigh and medial knee pain distribution pattern","E":"Popliteal artery entrapment should be considered given the BMI-related vascular risk and posterior knee symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1), dangerous conditions (step 2), and nerve pathology (step 4), step 3 of the workflow — OA or arthritis — becomes the focus. The clinical presentation (age, BMI, diffuse pain worse with activity, stiffness ≤30 min) combined with X-ray showing medial compartment narrowing is diagnostic of knee OA. Proceeding with OARSI-guided management (exercise, education, pharmacotherapy) is appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The knee pain workflow is a structured clinical heuristic — use it to prevent premature closure. OA is the most common cause of chronic knee pain in patients over 45. COMLEX: Somatic dysfunction often accompanies OA and may be an adjunctive treatment target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/collateral injuries present after trauma; the workflow step 2 dangerous-condition screen must precede advancing to OA assessment in atraumatic chronic knee pain"},{"id":"sdl4_q29","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female marathon runner presents with left anterior knee pain that worsens with downhill running and stairs but resolves with rest. She has no trauma history, no joint line tenderness, no effusion, and no neurological deficits. The physician excludes dangerous conditions and nerve pathology. On single-leg squat, dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Applying the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which anatomic pain generator is identified in step 5?","choices":{"A":"The patellofemoral joint, where dynamic valgus raises contact pressure during downhill impact loading","B":"The medial collateral ligament, which sustains chronic valgus stress from pronation during forefoot strike","C":"The posterior cruciate ligament, which bears eccentric load during downhill deceleration maneuvers","D":"The medial tibial plateau, which accumulates stress fracture from high-mileage cumulative impact loading","E":"The popliteal fossa bursa, which becomes inflamed from repetitive posterior compression during running"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Step 5 of the knee pain workflow identifies the specific pain generator. Dynamic valgus, anterior pain worse with patellofemoral loading (stairs, downhill), and positive patellofemoral compression test localize the pain to the patellofemoral joint. Downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure due to greater knee flexion angle during impact, amplifying pain in PFS. This finding guides targeted PT (hip and quad strengthening to correct valgus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Downhill running = greatest patellofemoral contact pressure (greater knee flexion angle). Single-leg squat reveals dynamic valgus as the biomechanical driver. COMLEX: Patellofemoral syndrome is a named CP8 musculoskeletal condition amenable to structured rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — patellofemoral syndrome identified as pain generator at step 5; downhill running increases patellofemoral contact pressure proportionally with knee flexion angle"},{"id":"sdl4_q30","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old male with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with right knee pain, erythema, and fever for 3 days. He has marked warmth and swelling of the entire joint. CRP is 85 mg/L and WBC is 16,000/mL. He denies trauma. Using the knee pain diagnostic workflow, which step is MOST critical to prioritize immediately in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for osteoarthritis as the cause given his age and the presence of joint swelling","B":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic arthritis","C":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to determine whether OA or bursitis is responsible","D":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma assessment since a missed fall in a diabetic patient could explain findings","E":"Step 2 — determining whether a dangerous medical condition such as septic arthritis is responsible for the presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fever, erythema, elevated inflammatory markers, and monoarticular swelling in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient demand immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous medical condition — specifically septic arthritis, which requires urgent arthrocentesis, culture, and antibiotics. Proceeding to OA or localized pain generator steps without ruling out septic arthritis would be a critical clinical error in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Septic arthritis is a medical emergency — joint destruction occurs within days. Synovial fluid WBC >50,000/mL with >90% PMNs is highly suggestive. Never inject a joint with suspected infection. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major risk factor for septic arthritis and septic bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — septic arthritis is a dangerous condition excluded in step 2 of the workflow before attributing knee pain to OA or structural causes","whyNot":{"A":"Proceeding to OA or localized "}},{"id":"sdl4_q31","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old soccer player has two injuries from separate seasons. A complete (grade III) MCL tear from a valgus tackle healed with 2 weeks in an immobilizer followed by a hinged brace and strengthening. A complete ACL tear from a noncontact cutting injury this season still shows no continuity on MRI after 4 months of rehabilitation, and he has recurrent giving-out. Which feature best explains why the two complete tears behaved differently?","choices":{"A":"The ACL is loaded in tension throughout knee flexion, whereas the MCL is unloaded in full extension, so the MCL stumps can rest and reattach","B":"The MCL is extra-articular with vessels inserting along its length, whereas the ACL spans the joint space with poor blood supply and cannot heal","C":"Synovial fluid contains collagenase that digests torn MCL fibers less than ACL fibers, because the MCL is coated by the medial meniscus","D":"The ACL has a single bundle that retracts fully once ruptured, whereas the MCL has anteromedial and posterolateral bundles that bridge the gap","E":"The MCL receives sensory nerve fibers that trigger protective bracing reflexes, whereas the ACL lacks innervation and is reinjured during healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The practical office orthopedics reading for this SDL explains that the collateral ligaments are extra-articular, surrounded by vascular soft tissue, with blood vessels inserting along their whole length, so nearly all collateral injuries, even complete grade III tears, heal without surgery. The cruciates span the joint space with no vessels inserting along their length, so complete tears do not heal and ligament-deficient, unstable knees usually need graft reconstruction. Constant tension through flexion is not the reason given; the issue is blood supply. Synovial collagenase protecting the MCL is not described, and the MCL is not coated by the meniscus. The anteromedial and posterolateral bundles belong to the ACL, not the MCL. Differences in sensory innervation are not the explanation offered for healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collateral ligaments (MCL, LCL) are extra-articular and well vascularized → heal with bracing; cruciates (ACL, PCL) are intra-articular with poor blood supply → complete tears do not heal and need a graft. COMLEX: ACL deficiency → instability → meniscal tears → early arthritis, which is why active patients are offered reconstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — ACL instability lets the femoral condyles strike the posterior meniscal horns and tear them","whyNot":{"A":"Constant tension through flexi","E":"Differences in sensory innerva"}},{"id":"sdl4_q32","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male rugby player falls onto his flexed knee with ankle plantarflexed during a tackle, developing posterolateral knee pain. Posterior sag sign is positive. Posterior drawer test shows 8mm of posterior glide at 90 degrees. MRI confirms a mid-substance PCL tear without bony avulsion and no associated ligamentous injury. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical PCL repair regardless of grade or associated injury status given the acute hemarthrosis","B":"Arthroscopic PCL reconstruction within 48 hours to prevent posterior instability and tibial sag progression","C":"Lateral collateral ligament reconstruction since LCL co-injury invariably accompanies PCL disruption patterns","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce acute hemarthrosis before initiating surgical planning","E":"Non-operative bracing and physical therapy, given an isolated mid-substance PCL tear without bony avulsion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isolated mid-substance PCL tears (without bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or severe instability >Grade 3) are managed non-operatively with bracing and PT. The literature supports good outcomes with conservative care for isolated PCL tears. Surgical indications include: bony avulsion fragment amenable to fixation, multiligamentous instability (combined with ACL/posterolateral corner), or Grade 3 tears with functional limitations not responding to PT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL isolated tears = non-operative (brace + PT). Surgical indications = bony avulsion, multiligamentous injury, or Grade 3 with functional limitation. PCL grade: Grade 1 (<5mm), Grade 2 (5-10mm), Grade 3 (>10mm). COMLEX: PCL injury is less common than ACL (PCL is the strongest knee ligament).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — PCL avulsion from posterior tibia is the bony avulsion pattern that converts a non-operative PCL tear into a surgical candidate; mid-substance tears without avulsion are managed conservatively"},{"id":"sdl4_q33","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old female presents after a varus stress injury to her right knee during recreational martial arts. Exam shows tenderness along the lateral knee, varus thrust gait, and laxity at 30 degrees of knee flexion with a solid endpoint at 0 degrees. X-ray is negative. MRI reveals a Grade 2 LCL tear without posterolateral corner injury or other ligamentous disruption. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate management for this isolated injury?","choices":{"A":"Conservative care with a hinged knee brace and physical therapy, as indicated for an isolated Grade 2 LCL tear","B":"Immediate surgical LCL reconstruction because all LCL injuries above Grade 1 require operative stabilization","C":"Arthroscopic lateral retinacular release to reduce varus alignment forces on the lateral compartment ligaments","D":"Total knee arthroplasty consultation given the severity of lateral compartment laxity demonstrated at 30 degrees","E":"Aspiration of the lateral joint and corticosteroid injection to reduce inflammation and prevent chronic laxity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated LCL Grade 2 tears (5-10mm laxity, solid endpoint, no posterolateral corner involvement) are managed conservatively with bracing and PT. Surgical referral for LCL injury is indicated for isolated but unstable injuries, mid-portion tears, complete tears, persistent injury at 4-6 weeks, or associated posterolateral corner injuries. Laxity only at 30° with solid endpoint at 0° and no PLC co-injury = conservative management appropriate. Lateral retinacular release is not part of the course's LCL treatment, which is a brace and physical therapy with a surgery consult only for unstable, mid-portion, complete, persistent or combined injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCL is the 2nd least common knee ligament injury (8% of knee injuries); 87% involve multiligamentous co-injuries. LCL surgical indications = isolated unstable, complete, mid-portion, persistent, or associated injuries. LCL runs from lateral femoral epicondyle to fibular head. COMLEX: Varus thrust gait is a key clinical sign of LCL/PLC insufficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — knee dislocation: varus laxity at 0° signals cruciate involvement in a multiligament injury","whyNot":{"B":"Surgical referral for LCL inju","C":"Lateral retinacular release is"}},{"id":"sdl4_q34","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with known knee osteoarthritis develops acute medial joint line pain after minimal twisting while rising from a chair. He describes no significant trauma. MRI reveals a horizontal cleavage tear of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus. Which type of meniscal tear does this most likely represent, and what is the key distinguishing feature?","choices":{"A":"A traumatic bucket-handle tear requiring urgent arthroscopic repair given the mechanical symptoms on exam","B":"A lateral meniscal root tear from rotational stress with tibial external rotation during the rising motion","C":"An osteochondritis dissecans fragment causing a loose body that mimics a meniscal tear on MRI signal","D":"A degenerative tear: horizontal cleavage in an older patient with pre-existing OA and a minimal mechanism","E":"A parameniscal cyst arising from a traumatic vertical meniscal tear in a young athlete with acute pivot injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Degenerative meniscal tears occur in older patients (>40) with pre-existing arthritis, typically presenting with minimal or no significant trauma. The horizontal cleavage pattern on MRI is characteristic of degenerative tears. They differ from traumatic tears (vertical, longitudinal, bucket-handle patterns in younger athletes with acute mechanisms). Risk factors include OA, male gender, and age. Treatment is typically conservative initially unless mechanical symptoms develop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degenerative tear = horizontal cleavage, older patient, OA background. Traumatic tear = vertical/bucket-handle, younger athlete, acute twist. MRI tear grades: 1 = intrasubstance, 2 = reaching surface, 3 = full-thickness. COMLEX: Degenerative tears in OA may not benefit from surgery beyond conservative care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — degenerative meniscal tears occur in the context of OA; risk factors include age >40, male gender, prior ACL injury, and presence of arthritis; minimal trauma is the distinguishing historical feature"},{"id":"sdl4_q35","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old female basketball player twists her planted knee, hears a \"pop,\" and develops lateral joint line pain and swelling. McMurray test performed with internal rotation and a varus force applied at 30 degrees of flexion produces a palpable click and lateral pain. Which meniscus is most likely injured, and what confirms this finding?","choices":{"A":"The medial meniscus, confirmed by the positive external rotation with valgus force reproducing medial pain","B":"The lateral meniscus, confirmed by internal rotation with varus force reproducing lateral click and pain","C":"Both menisci, since internal rotation stresses both compartments equally during the McMurray maneuver","D":"The posterior cruciate ligament, producing pain from posterior capsular impingement during knee flexion","E":"The anterior cruciate ligament, with referred lateral pain from the joint capsule and synovial irritation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"McMurray test mnemonic LIME: Internal rotation + varus force = Lateral meniscus; External rotation + valgus force = Medial meniscus. A click with internal rotation and varus stress implicates the lateral meniscus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common than medial (medial > lateral), but occur with rotational and pivot mechanisms. The lateral meniscus is less firmly attached to the capsule than the medial meniscus, making it more mobile but still susceptible to traumatic tears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LIME mnemonic — lateral meniscus = internal + varus; medial = external + valgus. Lateral meniscal tears are less common but often associated with ACL tears (lateral meniscus is more commonly co-injured with ACL than medial). COMLEX: Meniscal tears are a named COMLEX CP8 knee condition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — lateral meniscus involvement is actually more common than medial when ACL tears occur; the classic unhappy triad teaching (medial meniscus) reflects historical teaching rather than current evidence"},{"id":"sdl4_q36","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of meniscus injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has medial knee pain without locking. MRI is reviewed on T2-weighted sagittal images. The posterior horn of the medial meniscus, normally a solid black triangle, contains a central bright signal that stays inside the meniscus and does not reach its superior or inferior surface. How should this finding be interpreted?","choices":{"A":"Myxoid degenerative change, a normal age-related finding that is not a tear because the bright central signal never reaches the meniscal surface","B":"A horizontal cleavage tear of the posterior horn that needs arthroscopic partial meniscectomy because the signal splits the meniscus into two leaves","C":"A peripheral vertical tear in the vascular red zone that should be repaired with sutures because it lies where the blood supply supports healing","D":"A bucket-handle tear with a displaced inner fragment that explains mechanical symptoms and needs urgent referral before the fragment becomes fixed","E":"Meniscal calcification from calcium pyrophosphate deposition, which appears bright on T2 imaging and points to pseudogout as the pain generator"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On T2-weighted sagittal MRI each meniscal horn is a solid black triangle and joint fluid is white. A tear is called only when the white signal penetrates through the meniscal surface. With age the meniscus normally accumulates central fluid, called intrasubstance myxoid degenerative change, which stays inside the tissue and does not mean the meniscus is torn. A horizontal cleavage tear is a degenerative tear, but by definition its signal reaches the surface. A peripheral vertical tear suitable for repair would also reach the surface and lie at the outer rim, not centrally. A bucket-handle tear shows a displaced fragment, not an intact triangle with central signal. Meniscal calcification is a plain X-ray finding listed in the course, and calcification is dark rather than bright on MRI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meniscal tear on MRI = high signal that reaches an articular surface; intrasubstance signal that does not reach the surface = myxoid degeneration (not a tear). COMLEX: In patients over 70, asymptomatic meniscal tears are common, so an MRI tear may not be the pain generator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — degenerative meniscal change accompanies knee osteoarthritis in older patients","whyNot":{"E":"Meniscal calcification is a pl"}},{"id":"sdl4_q37","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after his knee struck the dashboard in a car crash. The knee is swollen, he cannot bear weight, and the patellar region is tender. Which plain radiograph set is most appropriate for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Weight-bearing AP, flexed-knee PA, and Merchant views, because standing images under load reveal the joint space narrowing that marks injury","B":"A single standing flexed-knee PA notch view, because flexion rotates the posterior femoral condyle into profile where fractures are seen","C":"No radiographs, with MRI as the first study, because MRI is more sensitive for fractures and also shows ligament and meniscal injury","D":"The standard knee series of a non-weight-bearing AP, a lateral, and two oblique views, designed for injured patients who cannot stand","E":"A single weight-bearing AP view of both knees, because comparing the injured side with the opposite knee shows subtle fracture lines"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ottawa criteria are met (inability to bear weight, patellar tenderness), so radiographs are needed. The standard knee series of a non-weight-bearing AP, a lateral, and a pair of obliques was designed for emergency patients who cannot bear weight and have limited motion, and it is well suited to finding fractures. The weight-bearing AP, flexed PA (Rosenberg), and Merchant set is the arthritis series for office patients and cannot be taken in someone who cannot stand. A lone flexed PA view is likewise an arthritis view for posterior condylar wear. MRI picks up fractures and soft-tissue injury, but after significant trauma the course says an X-ray should be taken to rule out a fracture first. A standing bilateral AP is impractical in this patient and is used to judge cartilage space, not fracture lines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute knee trauma → standard (non-weight-bearing) knee series to rule out fracture; suspected arthritis → weight-bearing AP + flexed PA + Merchant. COMLEX: Ottawa knee rules decide who needs films; the type of film depends on whether the question is fracture or cartilage loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — knee fractures: mechanism, imaging, and management","whyNot":{"A":"The weight-bearing AP, flexed ","B":"A lone flexed PA view is likew","C":"MRI picks up fractures and sof","E":"A standing bilateral AP is imp"}},{"id":"sdl4_q38","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old female with severe osteoporosis falls from standing height and presents with acute knee pain and inability to bear weight. X-ray is equivocal. CT confirms a displaced lateral tibial plateau fracture with articular depression. To complete the fracture assessment and guide surgical planning, which additional imaging study is MOST appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear medicine bone scan to detect additional occult lower extremity fractures missed on CT scanning","B":"DEXA scan to quantify bone mineral density and urgently guide osteoporosis pharmacotherapy initiation","C":"MRI of the knee to evaluate concurrent meniscal, ligamentous, and chondral injury that will alter surgery","D":"Fluoroscopic stress views to demonstrate the degree of articular depression under valgus load application","E":"Ultrasound of the popliteal fossa to assess hemarthrosis volume and patellar tendon integrity dynamically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After CT characterizes the tibial plateau fracture bony architecture, MRI is the most appropriate next study to evaluate concurrent soft tissue injuries — meniscal tears, ligamentous disruption (MCL, ACL, PCL), and chondral damage. These injuries frequently accompany tibial plateau fractures (on MRI, 91% have lateral meniscus pathology and 44% have medial meniscal tears, per the SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2) and directly influence surgical approach and outcomes. MRI findings change the operative plan in a high proportion of plateau fractures. CDT bases plateau classification on radiographs and CT and names preoperative MRI for the associated soft-tissue injuries; a DEXA scan measures bone density, and neither stress fluoroscopy nor popliteal ultrasound is the study it names.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau fractures = high association with meniscal (lateral 91%, medial 44% on MRI) and ligamentous co-injuries. CT = bony detail; MRI = soft tissue detail; both needed for complex fractures. COMLEX: Assess neurovascular status (peroneal nerve, popliteal artery) before any imaging in high-energy fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — MRI is indicated when soft tissue injuries need evaluation beyond what X-ray or CT provides; the knee arthritis and fracture workflows share this MRI decision-point principle","whyNot":{"D":"CDT bases plateau classificati","E":"CDT bases plateau classificati"}},{"id":"sdl4_q39","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 18-year-old male basketball player sustains a direct blow to his flexed knee with his ankle in plantarflexion during a fall. He cannot ambulate. X-ray shows a small posterior tibial avulsion fracture. Which structure is responsible for this avulsion pattern, and which clinical test corresponds to disruption of this structure?","choices":{"A":"PCL avulsion from its posterior tibial attachment; a positive posterior drawer test and posterior sag sign confirm the PCL injury","B":"ACL avulsion from the anterior tibial spine; positive Lachman test and anterior drawer confirm anterior cruciate disruption","C":"LCL avulsion from the fibular head; positive varus stress test at 30 degrees confirms lateral collateral ligament disruption","D":"Patellar tendon avulsion from the tibial tubercle; inability to actively extend the knee confirms extensor mechanism disruption","E":"Medial meniscal root avulsion from the tibial plateau; positive McMurray with external rotation confirms medial meniscal disruption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The classic PCL injury mechanism is a fall directly onto a flexed knee with plantarflexed ankle (or MVA dashboard injury). PCL inserts on the posterior tibia; avulsion fractures occur at this insertion. The posterior sag sign (tibia sags posteriorly relative to femur with knee at 90°) and posterior drawer test (posterior tibial glide at 90° flexion) are the diagnostic exam findings. An anterior tibial spine avulsion would implicate the ACL with positive Lachman/anterior drawer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCL avulsion fracture = small bony fragment posterior to the tibia on lateral X-ray. Mechanism: flexed knee + plantarflexed ankle impact (fall or dashboard). PCL = medial femoral condyle to posterior tibia (LAMP mnemonic). COMLEX: Bony avulsion PCL injuries are surgically repaired; mid-substance tears are typically managed non-operatively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — PCL resists posterior tibial translation; the fall-on-flexed-knee with plantarflexed ankle is the prototypical PCL mechanism producing posterior sag and posterior drawer positive findings"},{"id":"sdl4_q40","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old female cross-country runner presents with bilateral anterior knee pain worsening with stairs, single-leg squatting, and prolonged sitting. Patellofemoral compression test is positive bilaterally. There is no effusion or joint line tenderness. Video gait analysis shows dynamic valgus collapse during the stance phase of running. Which biomechanical abnormality MOST directly drives patellofemoral maltracking in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Tibial torsion causing excessive internal tibial rotation during heel strike and increasing patellofemoral compressive force","B":"Weakness of hip abductors and external rotators causing dynamic knee valgus that increases lateral patellar tracking forces","C":"Shortened Achilles tendon reducing ankle dorsiflexion and causing forefoot strike mechanics with knee flexion compensation","D":"Isolated quadriceps atrophy causing reduced extensor mechanism tension and inferior patellar tilt during loading","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint causing lateral compartment loading and increasing lateral tibial plateau stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hip abductor and external rotator weakness is the primary biomechanical driver of dynamic valgus in runners. Weakness of the gluteus medius causes the hip to adduct and internally rotate during single-leg stance, which forces the knee into valgus alignment. This valgus position increases the lateral force on the patella, causing maltracking and elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT targeting posterior hip strengthening directly addresses this mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS biomechanical chain — hip abductor weakness → dynamic valgus → lateral patellar tracking → elevated patellofemoral contact pressure. PT must include gluteus medius and external rotator strengthening, not just quad work. COMLEX: OMT targeting hip external rotator dysfunction can complement PFS rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — hip abductor/external rotator weakness drives dynamic valgus; physical therapy must address hip strengthening as the root cause, not only quadriceps strengthening around the knee"},{"id":"sdl4_q41","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male soccer player is diagnosed with patellofemoral syndrome after 6 weeks of anterior knee pain. His coach suggests he undergo arthroscopic debridement of the patella. Which of the following represents the LEAST appropriate treatment strategy for this patient at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Physical therapy targeting quadriceps strengthening and hamstring flexibility to improve patellar tracking mechanics","B":"Hip strengthening exercises focusing on the gluteus medius and external rotators to reduce dynamic valgus forces","C":"Immediate arthroscopic debridement of the patellar cartilage without first completing supervised physical therapy","D":"Activity modification to reduce high-impact loading while maintaining fitness with low-impact cross-training","E":"Patellar taping and stabilizing bracing techniques to improve patellofemoral alignment during rehabilitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surgery for PFS is not indicated without an adequate prior trial of conservative management (physical therapy, activity modification, bracing). At 6 weeks without PT, the patient has not exhausted first-line treatments. PT targeting quadriceps and hip strengthening with biomechanical correction is the evidence-based first step. Surgical debridement is reserved for cases with documented chondromalacia refractory to extensive conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PFS treatment algorithm = PT first (quad + hip strengthening + biomechanics) → bracing/taping → activity modification → role of injections uncertain → surgery only for refractory documented chondromalacia. COMLEX: Conservative management must be exhausted before surgical referral in PFS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis and knee pain both reinforce conservative-first management before imaging-guided or surgical interventions","whyNot":{"B":"PT targeting quadriceps and hi"}},{"id":"sdl4_q42","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of patellofemoral syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female runner has patellofemoral syndrome confirmed by positive patellofemoral compression test. On single-leg squat assessment, moderate dynamic valgus collapse is observed. Which mechanism BEST explains why single-leg squatting reproducibly worsens her anterior knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendon elongation from eccentric quadriceps loading concentrates stress at the distal patellar pole","B":"Medial meniscal compression from tibial internal rotation during the deep phase of the single-leg squat","C":"Femoral nerve stretch from hip flexion causing referred anterior thigh and anterior knee discomfort","D":"Lateral retinacular tightness pulling the patella medially during varus knee alignment at the squat midpoint","E":"Dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and raises contact pressure against the lateral trochlea"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During single-leg squatting, dynamic valgus collapse — from hip abductor weakness — causes the knee to move medially over the foot, creating a valgus moment that lateralizes the pull on the patella. This increases lateral tracking of the patella against the trochlear groove, amplifying patellofemoral contact pressure. Single-leg squat is the most sensitive clinical movement for detecting dynamic valgus and reproducing PFS symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Single-leg squat = the clinical test of choice for detecting dynamic valgus in PFS. Patellofemoral compression forces are high during eccentric quadriceps contraction on a bent knee (course SDL 4 reading, Practical Office Orthopedics). COMLEX: Clinical video analysis during single-leg squat is a practical screening tool for biomechanical PFS contributors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — dynamic valgus increases lateral patellar tracking and patellofemoral contact pressure; single-leg squat is the functional test that makes this biomechanical driver clinically visible"},{"id":"sdl4_q43","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who works as a carpet layer presents with 2 days of painful swelling directly over the patella. He has a history of recurrent podagra. He is afebrile, and there is no drainage or skin break. Ultrasound shows a distended prepatellar bursa. Which management best fits the course approach?","choices":{"A":"Surgical bursectomy within 48 hours, because crystal-laden bursal tissue will keep reforming the effusion until it is excised","B":"Empiric intravenous vancomycin without aspiration, because any acute prepatellar swelling in a laborer is septic until proven otherwise","C":"Intrabursal corticosteroid injection without aspiration, because steroid settles crystal inflammation and avoids a needle tract infection","D":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration with Gram stain and crystal analysis, then colchicine if urate crystals confirm a gouty bursitis","E":"Allopurinol started during the flare, because lowering serum urate quickly dissolves the crystals and decompresses the bursa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists urate crystals beside Gram stain in the laboratory work-up of prepatellar bursitis, uses ultrasound to guide aspiration, and treats with aspiration plus colchicine when gout is considered (antibiotics when infection is considered), along with PRICE, temporary immobilization, and a compression wrap. A man with recurrent podagra and no fever fits a gouty bursitis, and aspiration both relieves the bursa and separates crystal from infection. Bursectomy is not part of initial management. Empiric vancomycin without fluid analysis skips the step that tells infection from gout. Steroid injection without aspiration is not the course approach and would be unsafe before infection is excluded. Starting urate-lowering therapy during an acute flare does not treat the flare and can prolong it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepatellar bursitis work-up = aspirate → Gram stain + crystals; septic → anti-staph antibiotics; gout → colchicine. Gout = negatively birefringent needle-shaped urate crystals. COMLEX: Kneeling occupations (\"housemaid's knee\") drive prepatellar bursitis; aspiration is diagnostic and therapeutic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects by body system when choosing anti-inflammatory therapy","whyNot":{"A":"Bursectomy is not part of init","B":"Empiric vancomycin without flu","C":"Steroid injection without aspi"}},{"id":"sdl4_q44","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old female basketball player has had three lateral patellar dislocations over 18 months. After the first episode she completed a full course of physical therapy for quadriceps and hip strengthening and has worn a patellar stabilizing brace for sports. MRI after the latest episode shows a torn medial patellofemoral ligament without an osteochondral fragment. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Repeat PRICE therapy and restart the same physical therapy program, because further strengthening will restore the torn ligament","B":"Referral for surgical stabilization, because the instability has recurred despite completed therapy and bracing for the first episode","C":"Long-term knee immobilization in extension for 12 weeks, because rigid protection lets the medial patellofemoral ligament scar in place","D":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection, because inflammation of the medial retinaculum drives repeated lateral patellar translation","E":"A lateral heel wedge orthotic, because shifting ground reaction forces medially reduces the lateral pull of the quadriceps on the patella"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course treats patellar subluxation and dislocation first with PRICE, physical therapy, and bracing, and reserves surgery for recurrent instability. This patient has had three dislocations despite completing that program, so surgical referral is next. Repeating the same failed program does not address recurrence, and physical therapy strengthens muscle rather than restoring a torn ligament. Prolonged immobilization in extension is not part of the course plan and causes stiffness and atrophy. Corticosteroid injection treats inflammation, not mechanical instability. A lateral heel wedge is used for medial knee loading problems and is not a treatment for patellar instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial patellofemoral ligament = primary passive restraint to lateral patellar displacement. First-time dislocation → conservative care; recurrent instability → surgery. COMLEX: Weak quadriceps and ligamentous laxity are listed mechanisms; adolescents have the highest incidence (29/100,000).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 6 — patellar dislocation presentation and reduction","whyNot":{"C":"Prolonged immobilization in ex"}},{"id":"sdl4_q45","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of prepatellar bursitis and patellar subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the evaluation of a patient with suspected recurrent patellar subluxation, which plain radiographic view is MOST appropriate for assessing patellofemoral alignment and lateral patellar tilt?","choices":{"A":"Merchant (sunrise) view, imaging the patellofemoral joint tangentially to show patellar tilt and alignment","B":"Weight-bearing AP view, which demonstrates tibiofemoral joint space and is the gold standard for OA staging","C":"Oblique lateral view, which demonstrates the arcuate sign indicating posterolateral corner injury pattern","D":"Standard lateral view, which shows the Pellegrini-Stieda lesion at the medial femoral condyle attachment","E":"Tunnel view (notch view), which is used to evaluate the intercondylar notch for osteochondritis dissecans"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Merchant view (sunrise or subpatellar view) is the dedicated radiographic view for patellofemoral assessment. The X-ray beam is directed inferosuperiorly along the axis of the femur, providing a tangential view of the patellofemoral joint. It demonstrates patellar tilt, trochlear morphology, and patellar subluxation. The course reading (Practical Office Orthopedics) uses this view to detect patellofemoral (bone-on-bone) arthritis. The AP and lateral views do not adequately assess the patellofemoral compartment in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Merchant view = sunrise view = subpatellar view. Used for: patellar subluxation, PFS assessment, patellofemoral OA (bone-on-bone on Merchant = patellofemoral arthritis). COMLEX: X-ray alignment views guide surgical planning for tibial tubercle osteotomy in recurrent patellar instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 9 — Merchant view is part of the knee arthritis imaging protocol for assessing the patellofemoral compartment; the knee arthritis workflow includes standard AP, lateral, and Merchant views","whyNot":{"B":"The AP and lateral views do no","D":"The AP and lateral views do no"}},{"id":"sdl4_q46","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing knee osteoarthritis has a large posterior knee mass confirmed on ultrasound as a popliteal cyst. Over the past month she has developed numbness of the sole of the foot and difficulty rising onto her toes. Dorsiflexion and sensation over the dorsum of the foot are normal. Which nerve is the cyst most likely compressing?","choices":{"A":"The common fibular nerve, which wraps the fibular neck and supplies foot dorsiflexion, eversion, and sensation over the dorsum of the foot","B":"The femoral nerve, which runs through the posterior knee to supply the quadriceps and sensation over the anteromedial leg and medial foot","C":"The saphenous nerve, which passes behind the medial femoral condyle and supplies the plantar skin and the long toe flexors of the foot","D":"The tibial nerve, which runs through the popliteal fossa to supply the plantarflexors, the long toe flexors, and the skin of the sole","E":"The obturator nerve, which descends behind the knee with the popliteal artery to supply the calf muscles and the skin of the heel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A popliteal (Baker) cyst sits in the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa in the popliteal fossa. First Aid lists a Baker cyst as a proximal cause of tibial nerve injury: the tibial nerve supplies the triceps surae and foot flexors and the skin of the sole, so the patient cannot stand on her toes and has plantar numbness. The common fibular nerve is lateral at the fibular neck and would cause foot drop and dorsal foot numbness, which she does not have. The femoral nerve is an anterior thigh nerve that does not run through the popliteal fossa. The saphenous nerve is sensory to the medial leg and has no plantar or motor role. The obturator nerve supplies the thigh adductors and does not descend behind the knee.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial nerve (L4-S3): plantarflexion, inversion, toe flexion, sole sensation; proximal lesion from knee trauma or Baker cyst; distal lesion = tarsal tunnel. \"Tibial Inverts and Plantarflexes; can't stand on TIPtoes.\" COMLEX: Baker cysts can also rupture and mimic DVT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — lower extremity nerve entrapments of the leg and foot","whyNot":{"B":"The femoral nerve is an anteri"}},{"id":"sdl4_q47","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is incidentally found to have a 2cm popliteal cyst on knee ultrasound during evaluation of mild knee stiffness after a minor fall. He has no constitutional symptoms, no fever, and no prior knee pathology. Ultrasound shows no communication with the synovial space. Which is the MOST appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical excision because pediatric popliteal cysts always carry risk of malignant transformation","B":"Aspiration combined with corticosteroid injection to permanently collapse the cyst in young children","C":"Watchful waiting or aspiration, since pediatric cysts rarely communicate and often resolve on their own","D":"MRI with gadolinium contrast to rule out a vascular malformation or synovial sarcoma in this age group","E":"Total knee arthroscopy to assess the posterior compartment and cauterize the cyst at its point of origin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pediatric popliteal cysts (peak incidence ages 4-7) have different pathoanatomy from adult cysts — they typically do NOT communicate with the synovial space, arise from the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa independently, and frequently resolve spontaneously with watchful waiting or simple aspiration. They are not associated with intra-articular pathology the way adult cysts are, and there is no indication for aggressive surgical or injection treatment in the absence of concerning features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric (4-7) vs. adult (35-70) Baker's cysts have different pathoanatomy and treatment. Pediatric = non-communicating, benign, often resolve spontaneously. Adult = communicating, treat underlying joint pathology. COMLEX: Watchful waiting is appropriate for asymptomatic pediatric popliteal cysts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — pediatric popliteal cysts arise from different pathoanatomy than adult cysts; they typically lack synovial communication and resolve spontaneously, in contrast to adult cysts driven by intra-articular disease","whyNot":{"A":"They are not associated with i","B":"They are not associated with i","E":"They are not associated with i"}},{"id":"sdl4_q48","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, histopathology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of popliteal cyst.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis develops acute calf pain and swelling mimicking a deep vein thrombosis. Doppler ultrasound shows no venous thrombus but reveals a ruptured popliteal cyst with free fluid tracking into the posterior calf. Which term BEST describes this complication and its underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"True DVT, because popliteal cyst compression of the popliteal vein causes secondary venous thrombosis formation","B":"Gastrocnemius muscle avulsion, caused by sudden knee hyperextension during the rupture event itself","C":"Compartment syndrome, caused by arterial embolization from synovial shedding producing distal calf ischemia","D":"Septic thrombophlebitis, caused by bacterial seeding from the ruptured cyst resulting in polymicrobial cellulitis","E":"Pseudothrombophlebitis, in which a ruptured cyst releases synovial fluid into the calf muscles and mimics DVT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rupture of a popliteal cyst causes \"pseudothrombophlebitis\" — synovial fluid tracks inferiorly into the calf, producing acute pain, swelling, and erythema that mimics DVT. Doppler ultrasound demonstrates the cyst rupture and absence of venous thrombus, distinguishing it from true DVT. This is a recognized complication of Baker's cysts in patients with RA or OA who have large fluid collections. Management involves treating the underlying joint disease rather than anticoagulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ruptured Baker's cyst = pseudothrombophlebitis. Ultrasound is the diagnostic test of choice — shows free fluid tracking into calf with no venous thrombus. D-dimer may be falsely elevated. COMLEX: RA and OA are the most common underlying conditions driving large popliteal cysts that can rupture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — popliteal cyst rupture causes pseudothrombophlebitis; ultrasound differentiates this from true DVT and guides the decision against anticoagulation in this setting"},{"id":"sdl4_q49","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with symptomatic knee osteoarthritis had a myocardial infarction last year and has heart failure. He does structured land-based exercise and has used a topical NSAID for 2 months with inadequate relief. Using the OARSI comorbidity columns, which pharmacologic option is listed as a conditional Level 1B choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection of the knee","B":"Oral celecoxib at the lowest effective daily dose","C":"Oral naproxen combined with a proton pump inhibitor","D":"Oral ibuprofen taken with food for short pain flares","E":"Oral duloxetine titrated to a daily maintenance dose"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OARSI sorts knee OA recommendations by comorbidity profile. Arthritis education and structured land-based exercise are core for everyone, and topical NSAIDs are Level 1A for patients with no comorbidity, GI comorbidity, or cardiovascular comorbidity. In the cardiovascular column, the conditional Level 1B pharmacologic options are intra-articular corticosteroid (IACS) and intra-articular hyaluronic acid (IAHA); oral NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors are not listed for this profile. Celecoxib appears in the no-comorbidity, GI, and widespread pain columns, but not in the cardiovascular column. Naproxen with a PPI is a choice for patients without comorbidity, and the PPI protects the stomach, not the heart. Ibuprofen is a non-selective oral NSAID, also absent from the cardiovascular column. Duloxetine is listed only for the widespread pain or depression profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oral NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors raise cardiovascular risk (COX-2 → less prostacyclin, more thromboxane). OARSI cardiovascular profile: topical NSAIDs (1A), IACS or IAHA (1B). COMLEX: Match the OA drug to the comorbidity column before prescribing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 5 — COX-2 selective inhibitor cardiovascular adverse effects","whyNot":{"B":"Celecoxib appears in the no-co","C":"Naproxen with a PPI is a choic","D":"Ibuprofen is a non-selective o","E":"Duloxetine is listed only for "}},{"id":"sdl4_q50","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with knee osteoarthritis and a history of peptic ulcer disease controlled on PPI therapy requires pharmacologic treatment for moderate pain. He also has stable hypertension. According to OARSI guidelines, which pharmacologic option is MOST appropriate as the preferred initial choice for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Non-selective systemic NSAIDs as monotherapy given the anti-inflammatory benefit and cost-effectiveness","B":"COX-2 selective inhibitors alone without gastroprotection, given their lower GI ulcer risk than non-selective NSAIDs","C":"Oral opioid analgesics as first-line therapy given the contraindication to both selective and non-selective NSAIDs","D":"Topical NSAIDs, the preferred initial agent for patients with GI comorbidities such as peptic ulcer disease","E":"Aspirin monotherapy given its combined anti-inflammatory and cardiovascular protective dual mechanism of action"},"correct":"D","explanation":"OARSI guidelines specifically recommend topical NSAIDs as the preferred pharmacologic option for patients with GI comorbidities (peptic ulcer disease). Topical NSAIDs provide local anti-inflammatory effect with minimal systemic absorption, significantly reducing GI risk compared to oral NSAIDs. Systemic NSAIDs (selective or non-selective) carry GI bleeding risk even with PPI use. Opioids are not first-line for OA per OARSI. Aspirin is not recommended as primary OA pharmacotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OARSI GI comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first. CV comorbidity → topical NSAIDs first (avoid systemic non-selective). Topical NSAIDs: diclofenac gel/patch has strong evidence for knee OA. COMLEX: Avoid systemic NSAIDs in patients with peptic ulcer disease even with PPI coverage when topical options are available.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — OARSI pharmacologic stratification by comorbidity: GI comorbidity = topical NSAIDs preferred; this reflects the risk-benefit calculation of minimizing systemic drug exposure in high-risk patients","whyNot":{"A":"Systemic NSAIDs (selective or ","B":"Systemic NSAIDs (selective or ","E":"Aspirin is not recommended as "}},{"id":"sdl4_q51","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of knee osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician wants to document baseline physical function in a 64-year-old woman newly diagnosed with knee osteoarthritis, so that response to an exercise program can be tracked. Which assessment is one of the performance-based tests OARSI recommends?","choices":{"A":"Kellgren-Lawrence grading of a weight-bearing AP radiograph","B":"A Lachman test repeated at each visit to track stability","C":"A 30-second chair stand test counting full sit-to-stands","D":"Serial knee joint aspiration to measure synovial fluid volume","E":"Goniometry of passive knee flexion with the patient supine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"OARSI recommends a set of performance-based functional tests for hip and knee osteoarthritis and publishes demonstration videos: the 30-second chair stand test, the 40-meter fast-paced walk test, a stair climb test, the timed up and go test, and the 6-minute walk test. The chair stand test measures what the patient can do and can be repeated to track response to exercise. Kellgren-Lawrence grading is a radiographic structure score, not a functional test. The Lachman test assesses the ACL, not osteoarthritis function. Serial aspiration is invasive and is not a functional measure. Passive goniometry measures range of motion, not functional performance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA is a clinical diagnosis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, morning stiffness ≤30 minutes); OARSI functional tests: 30-second chair stand, 40-m fast walk, stair climb, timed up and go, 6-minute walk. COMLEX: Objective function testing guides exercise prescription and documents improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — goals and measurable outcomes in a physical therapy prescription"},{"id":"sdl4_q52","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has 8 weeks of medial joint line pain. Medial joint line tenderness, medial pain with deep flexion, and a medial McMurray test are all positive. He asks to go straight to arthroscopy for a suspected meniscal tear. According to the course reading, what should be obtained before arthroscopy is considered?","choices":{"A":"Serum ESR and CRP, because meniscal pain in men over 50 is usually an early sign of inflammatory arthritis rather than a tear","B":"An MRI with gadolinium, because contrast is needed to show the tear before any arthroscopy and must precede plain films","C":"A diagnostic aspiration, because blood in the joint fluid confirms a peripheral tear in the vascular zone that can be repaired","D":"A weight-bearing arthritis series of X-rays, because medial bone-on-bone arthritis can produce all three positive meniscal signs","E":"A bone scan, because increased uptake in the medial tibial plateau separates a meniscal tear from a stress fracture of the tibia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The reading recommends the weight-bearing arthritis X-ray series in any patient over 40 with suspected meniscal tear. Medial bone-on-bone arthritis gives medial joint line pain, tenderness, pain with deep flexion, and a positive McMurray test, so history and exam alone can mislabel arthritis as a meniscal tear. Arthroscopy has little utility in arthritis and gives poor results next to exposed bone, so arthritis must be ruled out first. ESR and CRP do not separate a tear from osteoarthritis. MRI is not required before arthroscopy in a classic case and does not need gadolinium for meniscal tears. Aspiration is not part of this work-up. A bone scan is used for stress fractures and is not the step described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee arthritis workflow: history → exam → weight-bearing X-rays (gold standard); MRI only for complex cases or cartilage questions. COMLEX: Rule out arthritis in at-risk patients (over 40) before meniscal surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — degenerative versus traumatic meniscal tears","whyNot":{"C":"Aspiration is not part of this"}},{"id":"sdl4_q53","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic knee pain, swelling, and stiffness. The physician notes that these complaints are common to many knee conditions. When she lies supine and squeezes her legs together, her ankles touch well before her knees. Which interpretation of this physical finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A severe varus deformity, the exam finding most specific for knee arthritis, from medial compartment cartilage wear","B":"A severe valgus deformity from lateral compartment wear, which brings the knees together before the ankles","C":"A normal finding, since mild angular deformities are common and do not point to cartilage loss in any compartment","D":"Medial collateral ligament laxity, which lets the tibia drift away from the midline when the legs are adducted","E":"Patellofemoral maltracking, which rotates the tibia outward and separates the knees during adduction of the hips"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course reading notes that swelling, stiffness, tenderness, and warmth are not specific for arthritis; the one exam finding that is very specific for knee arthritis is a severe angular deformity. In the supine squeeze test, normal knees and ankles touch at about the same time. If the ankles touch first, the knees are bowed (varus), which comes from medial compartment wear; if the knees touch first, the deformity is valgus from lateral wear. Valgus is the opposite pattern. Mild deformities can be normal, but a clear gap at the knees with the ankles touching is a severe varus pattern. MCL laxity would open the medial joint under valgus stress, not create a fixed bowleg. Patellofemoral maltracking does not cause a coronal-plane bowleg deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial compartment OA → varus (bowleg); lateral compartment OA → valgus (knock-knee). Severe angular deformity is the most specific exam sign of knee arthritis. COMLEX: Supine leg-squeeze test is a quick office screen before weight-bearing X-rays.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee osteoarthritis clinical criteria and OARSI management","whyNot":{"C":"Mild deformities can be normal","D":"MCL laxity would open the medi"}},{"id":"sdl4_q54","sdlNumber":4,"batch":2,"objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for knee arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician completing the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow reaches the imaging step. The patient is a 65-year-old male with classic clinical OA features and pain that is moderate in severity. According to the knee arthritis workflow, which imaging modality is the GOLD STANDARD for initial evaluation of knee arthritis?","choices":{"A":"MRI, because it provides the most sensitive evaluation of articular cartilage and synovial tissue integrity","B":"Ultrasound, because it provides real-time dynamic assessment of effusion and allows immediate guided aspiration","C":"Weight-bearing X-ray, which shows joint space narrowing under physiological load and is the OA gold standard","D":"CT arthrogram, because it provides superior cartilage mapping and bony architectural detail for OA staging","E":"Radionuclide bone scan, because it identifies early periarticular metabolic changes preceding X-ray abnormalities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for initial knee OA imaging. It is essential that imaging be performed weight-bearing — non-weight-bearing X-rays can miss joint space narrowing by allowing the femur and tibia to separate. Standard knee OA X-ray series includes AP weight-bearing (for tibiofemoral joint space), lateral, and Merchant view (for patellofemoral compartment). MRI is not the gold standard for OA; it is reserved for complex presentations. Ultrasound is listed only for joint effusion, and neither CT arthrography nor a bone scan is in the workflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weight-bearing X-ray = OA gold standard. Always specify \"weight-bearing\" when ordering — non-weight-bearing AP is inadequate for OA assessment. Merchant view adds patellofemoral compartment evaluation. COMLEX: Appropriate imaging selection is a core clinical reasoning skill tested in COMLEX Level 1 and Level 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — weight-bearing X-ray is the gold standard for knee OA diagnosis and staging; it is the definitive imaging step in the knee arthritis diagnostic workflow before MRI or other advanced imaging is considered","whyNot":{"A":"MRI is not the gold standard f","B":"Ultrasound is listed only for ","D":"Ultrasound is listed only for ","E":"Ultrasound is listed only for "}}],"5":[{"id":"sdl5_q01","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluating a patient with hip pain applies the 5-step diagnostic workflow. After confirming no trauma in step 1, what does step 2 direct the physician to assess?","choices":{"A":"Whether osteoarthritis or another inflammatory arthritis is the most likely cause of the pain","B":"Whether nerve pathology such as radiculopathy or the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is responsible","C":"Whether the pain is localized and, if so, identifying the specific anatomic hip pain generator","D":"Whether a dangerous condition such as infection, malignancy, or fracture is the underlying cause","E":"Whether the pain is related to overuse activities such as running or repetitive hip flexion exercises"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hip pain diagnostic workflow mirrors the knee pain framework: (1) Is pain trauma-related? (2) Is a dangerous medical condition the cause? (3) Is OA or arthritis responsible? (4) Is nerve pathology such as radiculopathy responsible? (5) Is pain localized — what is the pain generator? Step 2 always screens for dangerous conditions — septic hip, fracture, psoas abscess, or malignancy — before advancing to the arthritis or nerve steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain diagnostic workflow = same 5-step structure as knee pain. Critical point: hip X-rays are almost always indicated to augment H&P for hip pain. COMLEX: In children presenting with knee pain, always include hip X-ray — hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip) commonly refers to the knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain","whyNot":{"A":"(3) Is OA or arthritis respons"}},{"id":"sdl5_q02","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old male presents with medial knee pain and a slight limp but no knee trauma. The physician completes the knee pain diagnostic workflow and finds no knee pathology. Which additional imaging step is MOST critical before concluding the evaluation?","choices":{"A":"Ordering knee MRI to rule out occult growth plate injury in the medial femoral condyle region","B":"Ordering a bone scan to rule out malignancy at the distal femoral epiphysis and proximal tibia","C":"Obtaining hip X-rays, since hip pathology in children often refers pain to the thigh and knee","D":"Performing nerve conduction studies to evaluate for saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee","E":"Ordering bilateral knee X-rays with comparison views to assess for physeal growth plate abnormality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hip pain workflow explicitly states that hip X-rays should almost always be used to augment history and physical, including in children presenting with knee pain. Hip pathology (Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, SCFE, septic hip) frequently manifests as ipsilateral thigh or knee pain rather than hip pain in children, making this a critical clinical pearl. Missing hip pathology by focusing only on the knee is a recognized diagnostic error. The workflow calls for hip X-rays in a child with knee pain, not a bone scan for malignancy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic boards trap — child with knee pain without knee pathology = always X-ray the hip. SCFE and LCP both present with knee or thigh pain. SCFE: obese adolescent, \"ice cream scoop\" X-ray appearance. LCP: age 4-9, insidious limp. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a CP8 named musculoskeletal presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q03","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which of the following BEST distinguishes the approach to hip pain from that of knee pain?","choices":{"A":"Hip X-rays almost always augment the history and physical, even when the pain does not localize to the hip","B":"The hip pain workflow uses only 3 steps, while the knee pain workflow requires a 5-step sequential process","C":"Nerve pathology is assessed before dangerous conditions in the hip workflow, unlike the knee workflow order","D":"The hip workflow begins with imaging before the history because X-rays are always required for hip pain","E":"Arthritis is evaluated in the final step of the hip workflow, whereas it is assessed in step 3 for knee pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hip pain workflow uses the same 5-step sequential framework as the knee pain workflow. The key distinguishing feature is that hip X-rays are almost always used to augment the history and physical in hip evaluations — a stronger imaging recommendation than for knee pain. This is because hip pathology (fracture, OA, AVN, SCFE, septic joint) frequently requires radiographic confirmation even when the history and exam are incomplete or atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both knee and hip workflows use the same 5-step structure. Hip-specific addition: X-ray is almost always ordered as a routine augment — not just when Ottawa-like criteria are met. COMLEX: Osteopathic structural exam complements the hip diagnostic workflow by identifying somatic dysfunction patterns consistent with specific hip pathologies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip pain workflow: same 5-step framework as knee; X-ray almost always used to augment H&P; include hip X-ray in children presenting with knee pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q04","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 65-year-old male presents with a deep aching pain around the hip socket, worsening with both active and passive motion, and progressive restriction of internal rotation followed by external rotation. He has no radicular pain. Which of the following risk factors is MOST associated with hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Male gender over age 40, NSAID use, and sedentary lifestyle are the primary risk factors for hip OA","B":"Autoimmune disease, positive rheumatoid factor, and prolonged corticosteroid use are the key risk factors","C":"Prior hip trauma, high-impact activity, obesity, and a family history of arthritis are all risk factors","D":"Tobacco use, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia are the most strongly associated risk factors for hip OA","E":"History of gout, hyperuricemia, and metabolic syndrome are the dominant risk factors for hip OA development"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hip OA risk factors are multifactorial and include: prior trauma or fracture or dislocation, history of high-impact activities, obesity, family history, and metabolic conditions. The clinical presentation features a deep aching pain around the hip socket, stiffness, and restricted range of motion — internal rotation is lost first, followed by external rotation in end-stage disease. Both active and passive motion reproduce pain, distinguishing hip OA from extra-articular causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA = deep ache around socket; IR lost first (then ER). Active and passive motion both painful = intra-articular source. Hip OA can refer to thigh or knee without hip pain. COMLEX: Hip OA is a named COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal condition; OMT can address somatic dysfunction of the hip as an adjunct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q05","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old female with hip osteoarthritis presents with predominantly thigh and knee pain with no groin complaint. Knee examination is normal. Which of the following BEST explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Hip pathology frequently refers pain to the thigh and knee without groin pain, so the hip must be evaluated","B":"The knee pain is from a separate degenerative meniscal tear that has developed simultaneously with the hip OA","C":"The thigh pain represents L2-L3 radiculopathy, which should be investigated with lumbar MRI before hip imaging","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment is the most likely cause of medial thigh pain in patients with hip osteoarthritis","E":"The patient has patellofemoral syndrome bilaterally causing knee pain secondary to the altered hip mechanics"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hip OA can present with thigh and/or knee pain without actual hip or groin pain — a clinically important pitfall. The hip joint refers pain through the obturator nerve and capsular innervation to the medial thigh and knee, which can mislead clinicians into focusing on the knee rather than the hip. This is why the hip diagnostic workflow recommends X-raying the hip in children with knee pain, and why a normal knee exam should prompt hip evaluation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain pattern = medial thigh and knee. Clinical pearl: knee pain with normal knee exam = X-ray the hip. COMLEX: Hip OA is a common cause of knee pain misdiagnosis; osteopathic structural exam should include hip mobility assessment in all patients with knee complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain"},{"id":"sdl5_q06","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old male with hip OA is found to have progressive loss of range of motion. Which pattern of motion restriction is MOST characteristic of advanced hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"External rotation is the first motion restricted, followed by internal rotation loss as a late finding in OA","B":"Internal rotation is lost first with progression, and external rotation restriction follows in end stage","C":"Flexion is the first and predominant motion loss, while rotation is preserved until the very late stages","D":"Abduction is preferentially restricted early and is always the first clinical sign of hip OA progression","E":"Extension loss occurs earliest and is the most reliable indicator of early hip OA on clinical examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hip OA, internal rotation (IR) is characteristically the first motion restricted as the joint capsule and surrounding tissues contract. As disease progresses, external rotation (ER) also becomes restricted, particularly in end-stage OA. Both active and passive hip motion provoke pain in intra-articular pathology — this distinguishes hip OA from extra-articular causes such as greater trochanteric tendinopathy, where passive motion is usually preserved. Extension loss is not the pattern the course gives; restriction falls especially on internal rotation, with external rotation lost at end stage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA motion loss pattern: IR first → then ER (end stage). Active AND passive motion painful = intra-articular. Passive motion preserved = extra-articular. COMLEX: Restricted hip IR on passive exam is a somatic dysfunction finding amenable to OMT (muscle energy, articulatory technique).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — hip OA: deep ache around socket, restricted IR then ER, usually unilateral; active and passive motion painful; can refer to thigh or knee without groin pain","whyNot":{"E":"Extension loss is not the patt"}},{"id":"sdl5_q07","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female marathon runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter, worsening with single-leg squat and associated with dynamic knee valgus. She was referred with a diagnosis of \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" What does current evidence suggest is the ACTUAL underlying pathology in most cases?","choices":{"A":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy is the primary pathology in most cases of so-called greater trochanteric bursitis","B":"Iliotibial band friction syndrome is the primary cause of lateral hip pain in runners, not tendinopathy","C":"Hip labral tear is the underlying cause of lateral hip pain in runners who present with dynamic valgus","D":"Snapping hip syndrome from iliopsoas impingement is the correct diagnosis in most cases of lateral hip pain","E":"True bursitis of the trochanteric bursa from direct pressure is the diagnosis in the majority of runners"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Current evidence demonstrates that \"greater trochanteric bursitis\" is a misnomer — the primary pathology is gluteus medius tendinopathy in the vast majority of cases. The presentation is lateral greater trochanter pain worsening with single-leg squat, running, and activities requiring repetitive hip flexion/extension. Dynamic knee valgus (from gluteus medius weakness) is both a biomechanical driver and a clinical finding. Treatment targets the tendinopathy and underlying biomechanics, not bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Greater trochanteric bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy. Lateral GT pain + single-leg squat pain + dynamic valgus = tendinopathy pattern. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness causes Trendelenburg gait and is a key somatic dysfunction pattern in the hip-pelvis region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q08","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female runner with confirmed gluteus medius tendinopathy fails conservative treatment with rest and physical therapy. Which of the following represents a RECOGNIZED escalation treatment option for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Surgical bursectomy is always the next step after physical therapy fails in any form of hip tendinopathy","B":"Oral opioid analgesics combined with long-leg immobilization is the recommended escalation for tendinopathy","C":"Total hip arthroplasty is the only definitive treatment for refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy at the hip","D":"Corticosteroid injection, tenotomy, regenerative injection (PRP), or nitroglycerin patches are all options","E":"Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the hip joint capsule is the preferred escalation for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After first-line biomechanical adjustment (PT, gait retraining, strengthening), refractory gluteus medius tendinopathy can be treated with: corticosteroid injection (for acute flare), tenotomy (for recalcitrant cases), regenerative injection (PRP), or nitroglycerin patches (shown to improve tendinopathy healing). These options address the tendinopathic tissue directly. Surgical bursectomy is not indicated when the pathology is tendinopathic rather than true bursitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinopathy treatment ladder = biomechanics → CSI → regeneratives/NTG patches → tenotomy. NTG patches (nitroglycerin) have evidence for tendinopathy healing via nitric oxide signaling. COMLEX: OMT addressing hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction can reduce the biomechanical load driving tendinopathy recurrence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line"},{"id":"sdl5_q09","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old female dancer describes an audible \"snapping\" sensation at the hip with certain movements. She has no pain with the snapping at rest but mild discomfort during repetitive activity. Which is the MOST common anatomic source of snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Avulsion of the gluteus medius from the greater trochanter producing an audible click during hip rotation","B":"Femoral head subluxation from a torn hip labrum producing a reproducible snap during hip flexion maneuvers","C":"Greater trochanteric bursitis producing a palpable fluid wave as the bursa shifts over the trochanteric ridge","D":"Osteochondral loose body impinging within the joint space producing a mechanical snap during hip extension","E":"The iliotibial band over the trochanter (external) or iliopsoas over the iliopectineal eminence (internal)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans) has two main anatomic sources: (1) external snapping — the iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter with hip flexion/extension; (2) internal snapping — the iliopsoas tendon snapping over the iliopectineal eminence with hip flexion. A third form involves intra-articular loose bodies, but this is less common. External snapping is the most common and is often painless; treatment focuses on IT band and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Snapping hip = coxa saltans. External = IT band over greater trochanter. Internal = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence. Most snapping is benign and painless. COMLEX: Snapping hip from IT band tightness responds to hip-pelvis OMT addressing somatic dysfunction of the iliotibial band insertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" is usually gluteus medius tendinopathy; lateral GT pain, single-leg squat pain, dynamic valgus; biomechanics correction is first-line","whyNot":{"D":"A third form involves intra-ar"}},{"id":"sdl5_q10","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 78-year-old female falls from standing height and presents with right groin pain, inability to bear weight, and the leg appearing shortened and externally rotated. Which hip fracture pattern is MOST consistent with this presentation, and what blood supply concern is associated with it?","choices":{"A":"Femoral neck fracture, which carries the highest AVN risk from disruption of the medial circumflex femoral artery","B":"Intertrochanteric fracture, which carries the highest AVN risk due to complete disruption of the femoral neck vasculature","C":"Subtrochanteric fracture, which is most commonly seen in elderly women and carries a low AVN risk overall","D":"Acetabular fracture, which disrupts the joint capsule but rarely affects blood supply to the femoral head","E":"Femoral shaft fracture, which presents with internal rotation and shortening but carries minimal AVN risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Femoral neck fractures carry the highest risk of AVN because the blood supply to the femoral head — primarily via the medial circumflex femoral artery — traverses the femoral neck and is disrupted by the fracture. Intertrochanteric fractures have better blood flow (below the neck) but greater displacement risk. The course slides list groin pain, decreased ROM, an internally rotated leg, and possible shortening as the presentation of hip fracture, while the course orthopedics reading (CDT Orthopedics, femoral neck fractures) Bootcamp and Moore (\"fractures of the femoral neck cause lateral rotation of the lower limb\") describe the femoral neck fracture leg as shortened and externally rotated, so the majority of sources support external rotation; a shortened, internally rotated leg is the classic picture of posterior hip dislocation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Femoral neck = poor blood supply → high AVN risk. Medial circumflex femoral artery = primary blood supply to femoral head. 1/3 of hip fractures progress to THA. FA: hip fractures can lead to AVN and nonunion (femoral neck). COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly female = high COMLEX yield with osteoporosis management component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q11","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old male motorcyclist presents after a high-speed MVC with the right hip flexed and internally rotated, with the femoral head palpable posteriorly. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST common, and which nerve is MOST at risk?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation is most common; the femoral nerve is most at risk causing quadriceps weakness and knee extension deficit","B":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the obturator nerve is most at risk causing medial thigh adductor weakness","C":"Central fracture-dislocation is most common; the pudendal nerve is most at risk from acetabular fragment displacement","D":"Superior hip dislocation is most common; the superior gluteal nerve is most at risk causing abductor weakness and Trendelenburg gait","E":"Posterior hip dislocation is most common; the sciatic nerve is most at risk and can cause foot drop and posterior thigh sensory loss if injured"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation is the most common type (85% of hip dislocations per the course reading, SDL 25 CDT Orthopedics; about 90% per Bootcamp), typically caused by high-energy trauma (MVA dashboard mechanism). The femoral head dislocates posteriorly and superiorly, putting the sciatic nerve at direct risk of injury. Sciatic nerve injury causes foot drop (peroneal division) and hamstring weakness, along with posterior thigh and below-knee sensory loss. Urgent reduction is essential to reduce sciatic nerve and femoral head ischemia risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation — leg flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop = peroneal division). FA: inferior gluteal nerve injury = posterior hip dislocation = difficulty rising from seated/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Posterior dislocation = CP8 named joint dislocation; requires urgent reduction, preferably within the first few hours, to minimize AVN risk (SDL 25 reading).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery","whyNot":{"B":"The femoral head dislocates po"}},{"id":"sdl5_q12","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Regarding hip fracture epidemiology, which of the following correctly identifies the fracture type MOST common in women aged 65 and older, and the fracture subtype with a BIMODAL age distribution?","choices":{"A":"Femoral shaft fractures are most common in women over 65; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","B":"Acetabular fractures are most common in elderly women; intertrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution","C":"Femoral neck fractures are most common in elderly women; acetabular fractures have a bimodal distribution","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures are most common in elderly women; femoral neck fractures have a bimodal distribution","E":"Intertrochanteric and femoral neck are most common in elderly women; subtrochanteric fractures are bimodal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In women aged 65 and older, intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common hip fracture types. Femoral neck fractures occur 3x more commonly in women than men. Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution — a younger peak at ages 20-40 (high-energy trauma) and an older peak above age 60 (low-energy osteoporotic fractures). Overall hip fracture incidence is 100-315 per 100,000 population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture types by age — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck. Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. Risk factors: age, female, osteoporosis, low BMI <22, endocrine disease. COMLEX: Hip fracture prevention via osteoporosis screening and fall prevention is a key preventive medicine objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — femoral neck: poor blood flow, high AVN risk; intertrochanteric: better flow; presentation = groin pain, shortened and internally rotated leg; most require surgery"},{"id":"sdl5_q13","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male with SLE who has been on long-term corticosteroids presents with left groin pain at rest and with weight bearing. X-ray is normal. MRI shows characteristic signal changes in the femoral head. Which of the following BEST describes the ARCO stage classification for this patient?","choices":{"A":"ARCO Stage II — subchondral fracture with crescent sign visible on plain X-ray indicating early collapse","B":"ARCO Stage III — femoral head depression greater than 2mm on X-ray representing established collapse","C":"ARCO Stage IV — joint space narrowing and degenerative acetabular changes consistent with end-stage OA","D":"ARCO Stage I — normal radiograph with abnormal MRI findings, a pre-collapse stage visible only on MRI","E":"ARCO Stage II — sclerosis and osteolysis visible on X-ray without crescent sign or femoral head collapse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ARCO staging for AVN: Stage I = normal X-ray with abnormal MRI (bone marrow edema, early ischemic signal); Stage II = no crescent sign, X-ray shows sclerosis/osteolysis/osteoporosis; Stage III = subchondral fracture with crescent sign and femoral head flattening (IIIA ≤2mm, IIIB >2mm depression); Stage IV = OA changes with joint space narrowing and acetabular degeneration. This patient has normal X-ray + abnormal MRI = Stage I.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI sensitivity for AVN = 100% (Stage I detectable on MRI when X-ray is normal). ARCO Stage I = pre-collapse = opportunity for joint-preserving treatment. FA: AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS. Most common site = femoral head (watershed from medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: AVN is a CP8 named hip condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q14","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly lists the risk factors associated with avascular necrosis of the femoral head using the First Aid mnemonic?","choices":{"A":"Calcium deficiency, Anemia, Smoking, Tuberculosis, Statin use, Bisphosphonate use, Lupus, Exercise excess","B":"Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, the Bends, Legg-Calvé-Perthes, Gaucher, and SCFE (CASTS Bend LEGS)","C":"Crohn disease, ACE inhibitors, Sepsis, Thrombus, Spondylosis, Bariatric surgery, Long-term NSAID use","D":"Corticosteroids, Aspirin, Septic arthritis, Thyroid disease, Systemic sclerosis, Bisphosphonate use, Lactose intolerance, Edema","E":"Calcium oxalate, Alcohol, Smoking, Thrombocytopenia, Septicemia, Beta-blocker use, Liver cirrhosis, Embolism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The First Aid mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS captures the key AVN associations: glucoCorticoids (most common drug-related cause), chronic Alcohol overuse, Sickle cell disease, Trauma, SLE, \"the Bends\" (caisson/decompression disease), Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (idiopathic childhood AVN), Gaucher disease, and SCFE (slipped capital femoral epiphysis). Corticosteroid use and alcohol are the two most common reversible risk factors in adults. Calcium deficiency, anemia, smoking, tuberculosis and exercise excess are not in the mnemonic, and the course lists bisphosphonates and statins among possible AVN treatments, not causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CASTS Bend LEGS = canonical AVN mnemonic (First Aid). Corticosteroids + alcohol = most common drug-related causes. Sickle cell = AVN in multiple sites. Medial circumflex femoral artery = blood supply at risk. COMLEX: AVN occurs in all joints but femoral head is most clinically tested for boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV"},{"id":"sdl5_q15","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old male with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with bilateral groin pain that is worse at night and at rest. X-ray shows flattening of the right femoral head and subchondral fracture with a crescent sign. Left femoral head shows sclerosis without collapse. Which imaging modality has 100% sensitivity for detecting early AVN before X-ray changes appear?","choices":{"A":"CT scan of the pelvis, which is superior to MRI for detecting early bone marrow ischemia in AVN staging","B":"Bone scan with technetium, which is the gold standard imaging for early AVN detection before collapse occurs","C":"Ultrasound of the hip joint, which shows early joint effusion and subchondral signal change in pre-collapse AVN","D":"Plain X-ray with comparison views, which is reliable for detecting Stage I and Stage II AVN with high sensitivity","E":"MRI, which has near 100% sensitivity for detecting femoral head AVN even at Stage I when plain X-rays are completely normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MRI has near 100% sensitivity for detecting AVN even in Stage I, when X-rays are completely normal. The characteristic finding is a \"double line sign\" on T2-weighted images — a low-signal outer band and high-signal inner band at the necrotic zone interface. CT is better than X-ray but inferior to MRI. Plain X-ray only becomes positive at Stage II or later. Early detection is critical because joint-preserving interventions are most effective before collapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for early AVN (Stage I — normal X-ray). X-ray: crescent sign = subchondral fracture = Stage III. ARCO Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. FA: AVN of femoral head = watershed area (medial circumflex femoral artery). COMLEX: Early detection enables core decompression before Stage III collapse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — AVN mnemonic CASTS Bend LEGS (Corticosteroids, Alcohol, Sickle cell, Trauma, SLE, Bends, LCP, SCFE); MRI sensitivity 100%; ARCO staging I-IV","whyNot":{"A":"CT is better than X-ray but in","D":"Plain X-ray only becomes posit"}},{"id":"sdl5_q16","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male with Crohn disease presents with right hip pain, fever, and pain that worsens with passive hip extension. Lab shows WBC 15,000 and ESR 88. CT reveals a focal hypodense lesion within the iliopsoas muscle plane. Which organism is MOST commonly implicated in psoas abscess?","choices":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, particularly in primary (hematogenous) cases","B":"Escherichia coli is the most common organism since psoas abscess is predominantly a GI-origin infection","C":"Candida albicans is the most common organism in immunocompromised patients with psoas abscess overall","D":"Streptococcus pyogenes is the most common organism in psoas abscess from Crohn disease-related spread","E":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most common organism causing psoas abscess in all geographic settings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated organism in psoas abscess, especially in primary (hematogenous) cases. Secondary psoas abscess from Crohn disease may also involve S. aureus or mixed flora (including gram-negative organisms from the GI tract). Tuberculosis causes psoas abscess in endemic regions (Pott disease spreading from vertebral osteomyelitis), but is not the global leading cause. Key diagnostic features: psoas sign — pain with passive hip extension — is a classic finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess = infection in iliopsoas compartment; S. aureus most common. FA: also associated with vertebral osteomyelitis (Pott disease), Crohn disease, pyelonephritis. Psoas sign = hip extension exacerbates lower abdominal/hip pain. CT = 90% sensitive (lower if <6 days of symptoms). TX: drainage + IV antibiotics. COMLEX: Psoas abscess is a CP8 dangerous hip condition that mimics hip OA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)","whyNot":{"E":"Tuberculosis causes psoas absc"}},{"id":"sdl5_q17","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with fever of 38.9°C, refusal to bear weight on the left leg, ESR of 52 mm/h, and WBC of 13,500. CRP is 2.8 mg/dL. Using the modified Kocher criteria, what is the estimated probability of septic hip arthritis?","choices":{"A":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 40% probability, which requires watchful waiting with repeat labs","B":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 3% probability, which is managed with outpatient antibiotics alone","C":"A score of 4 criteria gives approximately 93% probability, requiring urgent surgical lavage consultation","D":"A score of 4 criteria, plus an elevated CRP >2.0 mg/dL, gives approximately 99.6% probability of septic hip","E":"A score of 3 criteria gives approximately 93% probability since CRP is not included in the Kocher criteria"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Modified Kocher criteria include: (1) non-weight bearing, (2) ESR >40 mm/h, (3) temperature >101.3°F (38.5°C), (4) WBC >12,000, plus CRP >2.0 mg/dL as an add-on criterion. Risk stratification: 0 criteria = <0.2%, 1 = 3%, 2 = 40%, 3 = 93%, 4 = 99.6%. This patient meets all four classic criteria (non-weight bearing, fever 38.9°C, ESR 52, WBC 13,500) and also has CRP 2.8 mg/dL; a score of 4 = 99.6% probability (course slide). Urgent surgical aspiration/lavage and IV antibiotics are required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modified Kocher criteria = 4 classic criteria (+ CRP >2.0 mg/dL add-on); score 4 = 99.6% septic arthritis risk. Treatment = surgical aspiration/lavage + IV antibiotics. Key pitfall: distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal ESR/WBC, post-viral). COMLEX: Septic hip is a medical emergency in children — delay in treatment leads to femoral head destruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)","whyNot":{"A":"This patient meets all four cl","B":"This patient meets all four cl"}},{"id":"sdl5_q18","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl presents with a 3-day history of left hip pain and reluctance to walk. Temperature is 37.1°C (afebrile), ESR is 22 mm/h, WBC is 8,200. She had a URI 2 weeks ago. Hip ultrasound shows a small effusion. Modified Kocher score is 0. Which diagnosis is MOST likely and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Septic arthritis, which must always be excluded with surgical aspiration regardless of the Kocher score result","B":"Transient synovitis, managed with NSAIDs and activity restriction since a Kocher score of 0 is <0.2% risk","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, which presents acutely with fever and joint effusion in children after a viral illness","D":"Psoas abscess, which should be excluded with CT imaging given the hip pain and recent URI history in a child","E":"SCFE, which presents acutely with effusion and restricted hip motion in young children after viral illness onset"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in children, often preceded by an upper respiratory infection a few days to 2 weeks earlier (SDL 9 p3 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). It is distinguished from septic arthritis by the absence of fever, normal or mildly elevated inflammatory markers, and a low Kocher score. Kocher score 0 = <0.2% probability of septic arthritis. Management is conservative: NSAIDs + activity restriction + close follow-up. The effusion is resorbed within 3 to 7 days, with no long-term sequelae (same reading).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transient synovitis vs. septic hip — key differentiator = Kocher criteria. Transient synovitis: afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, post-viral. Septic hip: fever, elevated inflammatory markers, ill-appearing child. Both can cause limp and hip effusion. COMLEX: Transient synovitis is the most common cause of acute hip pain in school-age children.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — psoas abscess: Staph aureus most common, psoas sign; septic hip: modified Kocher 4 criteria = 99.6% risk; distinguish from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal labs)","whyNot":{"A":"It is distinguished from septi"}},{"id":"sdl5_q19","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is compressed in meralgia paresthetica, and what is the characteristic distribution of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"The femoral nerve is compressed, producing anterior thigh pain with weakness of knee extension and absent patellar reflex","B":"The obturator nerve is compressed, producing medial thigh pain and weakness of hip adduction bilaterally","C":"The sciatic nerve is compressed, producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and diminished Achilles reflex","D":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN) is compressed, producing pain, tingling, and burning paresthesias over the anterolateral thigh","E":"The ilioinguinal nerve is compressed, producing medial inguinal and scrotal pain without any thigh paresthesias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica is caused by compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN), a purely sensory nerve that exits the pelvis near the anterior superior iliac spine and passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament. It produces tingling, numbness, and burning pain in the anterolateral thigh — no motor deficit (it is purely sensory). The LFCN does not supply the medial thigh, groin, or below the knee, distinguishing it from femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN compression = anterolateral thigh paresthesias only, NO motor deficit. FA: causes = tight clothing, obesity, pregnancy; tingling/numbness/burning in anterolateral thigh. Median age = 50. COMLEX: LFCN runs beneath/through inguinal ligament near ASIS; palpation at this point may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts","whyNot":{"A":"The LFCN does not supply the m","C":"The LFCN does not supply the m"}},{"id":"sdl5_q20","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male with BMI of 38 and poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with burning and tingling over the anterior and lateral right thigh for 6 weeks. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness. Sensation is diminished over the anterolateral thigh only. Deep tendon reflexes are intact bilaterally. Which risk factor MOST significantly increases his risk for meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Male gender is the primary risk factor since meralgia paresthetica predominantly affects males over age 45","B":"Diabetes mellitus, which increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics","C":"Hypertension is the most significant risk factor through microvascular disease affecting nerve conduction velocity","D":"Hyperlipidemia is the primary risk factor through nerve sheath lipid deposition causing external compression","E":"Chronic NSAID use is the most significant risk factor through peripheral nerve demyelination over time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus increases the risk of meralgia paresthetica approximately 7-fold compared to non-diabetics — making it the most significant listed risk factor in this patient. The mechanism involves peripheral nerve susceptibility to compression from metabolic neuropathy. Other risk factors include obesity, older age (median age 50), pregnancy (increased abdominal pressure), ascites, tight belts/waistbands, prolonged pressure, and long-distance activities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica risk factors = obesity, older age (median 50), diabetes (7x risk), pregnancy, ascites, tight clothing. Clinical diagnosis — no EMG required unless diagnosis is uncertain. COMLEX: Anterior ASIS-area palpation may reproduce LFCN symptoms; OMT can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to nerve compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q21","sdlNumber":5,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester develops burning pain and numbness over the anterolateral right thigh. Exam confirms LFCN distribution sensory loss without motor deficit. Which is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Treating the underlying cause: reassurance that symptoms typically resolve postpartum once the pressure eases","B":"Prescribing anticonvulsants such as gabapentin or pregabalin as the primary first-line pharmacologic treatment","C":"Referring to anesthesia for nerve block injection at the anterior superior iliac spine immediately","D":"Ordering EMG and nerve conduction studies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment","E":"Placing the patient in a corset brace to decompress the inguinal ligament and reduce LFCN compression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica treatment begins by addressing the underlying cause. In pregnancy, increased abdominal pressure compresses the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. Symptoms typically resolve spontaneously after delivery as pressure decreases — expectant management and reassurance are appropriate first-line treatment. For recalcitrant cases (e.g., non-pregnant patients not responding to conservative measures), anticonvulsants (gabapentin, pregabalin) or anesthesia consultation for nerve block are escalation options.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat the cause first (weight loss, pregnancy delivery, remove tight clothing). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants or anesthesia consult. Clinical diagnosis — treatment does not require imaging or EMG. COMLEX: Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica resolves postpartum; OMT is safe in pregnancy and may address contributing pelvic somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica: LFCN compression at inguinal ligament; anterolateral thigh paresthesias/pain; risk factors = obesity, diabetes, pregnancy, tight belts"},{"id":"sdl5_q22","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female with type 2 diabetes presents with 3 days of right hip pain, fever of 38.7°C, and inability to bear weight. She appears ill. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step should be prioritized FIRST and what immediate action follows?","choices":{"A":"Step 3 — evaluating for hip osteoarthritis, since elderly diabetic patients with hip pain most likely have OA","B":"Step 5 — identifying the localized pain generator to differentiate greater trochanteric tendinopathy from joint disease","C":"Step 4 — evaluating for nerve pathology since diabetic peripheral neuropathy can mimic septic joint in elderly patients","D":"Step 2 — ruling out a dangerous condition with CBC, ESR, CRP, hip X-ray, and aspiration if an effusion is found","E":"Step 1 — returning to the trauma screen since a fall that was not reported is the most common cause of hip pain in elderly diabetics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, inability to bear weight, and an ill appearance in an immunocompromised (diabetic) patient mandate immediate step 2 evaluation for a dangerous condition — specifically septic hip arthritis. Labs (CBC, ESR, CRP, blood cultures) and hip imaging are obtained, and the hip is aspirated urgently, because fluid analysis is the key to diagnosis. The hip diagnostic workflow explicitly requires dangerous conditions to be excluded before proceeding to OA or nerve pathology steps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip pain + fever + non-weight-bearing + elevated labs = septic hip until proven otherwise. The modified Kocher criteria were derived in children; at any age, aspiration and fluid analysis make the diagnosis. COMLEX: Diabetes is a major immunocompromising risk factor for septic arthritis; septic hip requires surgical lavage + IV antibiotics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — septic hip must be ruled out in step 2 of the hip pain workflow; modified Kocher criteria guide the urgency of aspiration and surgical consultation in febrile patients with hip pain","whyNot":{"A":"The hip diagnostic workflow ex","C":"The hip diagnostic workflow ex"}},{"id":"sdl5_q23","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male presents with his mother reporting medial knee pain and a limp for 2 weeks. He denies trauma and has a normal knee examination. Which step in the hip pain diagnostic workflow is MOST critical to apply for this patient, and what imaging must be obtained?","choices":{"A":"The hip workflow applies; hip X-rays are essential because LCP and septic hip often refer pain to the thigh and knee","B":"The knee pain workflow should be completed first with MRI of the knee before considering any hip evaluation","C":"Lumbar spine MRI should be ordered first since L2-L3 radiculopathy is the most common cause of knee pain in children","D":"No additional imaging is needed since a normal knee exam in a child confirms there is no significant pathology","E":"Bilateral knee X-rays should be obtained to compare growth plates before expanding the evaluation to the hip"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A child with knee pain and a normal knee examination demands hip evaluation — this is a high-yield clinical pearl explicitly stated in the course hip pain workflow. Hip pathology (LCP, SCFE, septic hip, hip OA) frequently refers pain to the medial thigh and knee in children via obturator nerve distribution. Hip X-rays are almost always indicated. Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (ages 4-9, more common in males) and SCFE (obese adolescents) must both be excluded.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Child with knee pain + normal knee exam = ALWAYS X-ray the hip. LCP = age 4-9, male predominance 4:1, insidious limp. SCFE = obese adolescent, ice cream scoop appearance on frog-leg lateral X-ray. Both are CP8 named COMLEX pediatric hip conditions. COMLEX: Hip disease in children is a classic board trap with knee as the presenting complaint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fractures and pediatric hip pathologies (LCP, SCFE) frequently refer pain to the knee; the hip workflow's emphasis on hip X-ray in children with knee pain is driven by this referral pattern","whyNot":{"D":"Hip X-rays are almost always i"}},{"id":"sdl5_q24","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for hip pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male presents with right hip pain for 4 months. History reveals no trauma. He denies fever. Neurological exam is normal. Hip X-ray shows mild joint space narrowing and osteophyte formation. Applying the hip pain diagnostic workflow, which step has the physician reached in determining the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Step 5 — the specific pain generator has been confirmed as the hip labrum based on the X-ray findings","B":"Step 3 — arthritis is the most likely cause, after trauma and dangerous conditions were excluded","C":"Step 4 — nerve pathology must now be ruled out before the OA diagnosis can be confirmed on X-ray","D":"Step 2 — a dangerous condition such as septic arthritis must be ruled out before X-ray findings are interpreted","E":"Step 1 — the history of possible missed trauma must be re-explored before attributing the findings to OA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After excluding trauma (step 1) and dangerous conditions (step 2), and with a normal neurological exam excluding nerve pathology (step 4), finding classic X-ray features of OA — joint space narrowing and osteophytes — places this physician at step 3 of the hip pain workflow: determining that OA is the most likely cause. Importantly, hip X-ray is almost always used to augment the H&P in the hip workflow, as it was here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip workflow integrates X-ray as a routine diagnostic augment — not just ordered after all H&P steps are complete. OA X-ray findings: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis. COMLEX: The hip pain workflow is identical in structure to the knee pain workflow; both follow the 5-step framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — hip OA presenting with joint space narrowing and osteophytes represents step 3 of the workflow; X-ray is almost always used to augment the hip pain diagnostic evaluation at this stage"},{"id":"sdl5_q25","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old female presents with progressive right hip pain that she describes as a \"deep ache\" around the hip socket. She also reports right medial knee aching over the past year without trauma. Hip examination reveals markedly restricted internal rotation and moderate restriction of external rotation. Knee exam is entirely normal. What BEST explains the knee pain in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Concurrent medial compartment knee OA, which commonly develops simultaneously with ipsilateral hip OA in older women","B":"Degenerative medial meniscal tear, which is a predictable secondary complication of altered gait from hip OA","C":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee from altered biomechanics secondary to hip abductor dysfunction","D":"Hip OA referring pain to the medial thigh and knee through obturator nerve distribution, without intrinsic knee pathology","E":"Patellar subluxation from hip external rotator tightness causing lateral patellar tracking during ambulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hip OA can cause thigh and knee pain without any hip or groin pain — a recognized clinical pitfall. The hip joint's capsular innervation includes the obturator nerve (L2-L4), which also supplies the medial knee, explaining the referral pattern. The findings of restricted IR (first lost) and restricted ER (more advanced) confirm intra-articular hip pathology. A completely normal knee exam in the context of progressive hip OA strongly supports referred pain rather than intrinsic knee disease. Lateral patellar tracking would show on the knee exam, which is normal; the course notes that hip OA can present with thigh or knee pain alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip OA referred pain = thigh and medial knee via obturator nerve. Always evaluate the hip when knee exam is normal in older patients with knee pain. COMLEX: In osteopathic practice, restricted hip IR on structural exam is a key finding that guides hip OMT and differentiates hip from knee pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — the hip pain workflow specifically flags knee pain in children and thigh pain as potential hip referral patterns; this applies to adults with hip OA referring through obturator nerve distribution","whyNot":{"A":"A completely normal knee exam ","B":"A completely normal knee exam "}},{"id":"sdl5_q26","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man reports 8 months of left \"hip pain.\" When asked to point with one finger, he indicates the groin. The pain is worse when he walks or turns in bed but is not worse when he lies on his left side, and the lateral thigh is not tender. Which feature of the history most supports hip osteoarthritis over greater trochanteric pain?","choices":{"A":"Pain located over the lateral upper thigh that worsens when he lies on the affected side, because the trochanter is compressed at night","B":"Pain spanning the joint and muscle along the whole limb, because hip arthritis spreads along the tensor fascia lata to the lateral knee","C":"Pain felt in the groin and worsened by moving the joint, with no increase when lying on that side, because true hip pain localizes there","D":"Pain in the buttock that radiates down the posterior thigh, because hip capsule distension irritates the sciatic nerve as it exits the pelvis","E":"Pain that is burning and brought on by light touch of the anterior thigh, because the capsule shares its supply with the lateral cutaneous nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The history chapter assigned for this SDL notes that pain from true hip pathology is felt in the groin, and that hip osteoarthritis usually radiates to the groin and is worsened by moving the joint. Patients often call lateral thigh pain \"hip\" pain, but lateral pain that is worse when lying on the affected side at night points to trochanteric pain (now understood mostly as gluteus medius tendinopathy). Pain along a whole limb spanning joint and muscle suggests a vascular or nerve root process, not osteoarthritis. Buttock pain radiating down the posterior thigh suggests sciatica or piriformis syndrome. Burning pain provoked by light touch of the anterolateral thigh is neuropathic, as in meralgia paresthetica.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip joint pain → groin (and may refer to thigh/knee via obturator nerve); lateral thigh pain worse lying on it → greater trochanteric pain syndrome. COMLEX: Have the patient point to the pain; location sorts articular from soft-tissue and neuropathic causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — gluteus medius tendinopathy behind so-called trochanteric bursitis"},{"id":"sdl5_q27","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old retired warehouse worker has right hip pain. Which pattern of modifying factors most supports osteoarthritis rather than rheumatoid arthritis as the cause of his hip pain?","choices":{"A":"Pain greatest on waking with prolonged morning stiffness that eases as the day goes on","B":"Pain brought on by movement and worse after prolonged use of the joint, easing with rest","C":"Pain present at rest and through the night with a warm, swollen joint and early erosions","D":"Pain triggered by light touch of the thigh with burning that does not change with motion","E":"Pain that improves with continued use and warm-up, then recurs after the activity ends"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The history chapter for this SDL sorts hip pain by modifying factors: joint pain present with movement suggests the effusion of osteoarthritis, and pain worse with prolonged use of the joint is listed under osteoarthritis. It also notes that repeated minor trauma, such as jumping off trucks and platforms at work, contributes to hip osteoarthritis. Pain greatest in the morning with stiffness, and pain present at rest, point to inflammatory disease such as rheumatoid arthritis, where synovial destruction can happen early. Burning pain provoked by light touch suggests neuropathic pain such as an entrapment neuropathy. Pain that improves with use and recurs after activity is the pattern listed for a hip adductor strain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = pain with use, relieved by rest, brief stiffness (≤30 min); RA = morning stiffness >1 hour, pain at rest, symmetric synovitis. COMLEX: Occupational history (repetitive impact loading) is a hip OA risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — clinical criteria for osteoarthritis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, stiffness ≤30 min)"},{"id":"sdl5_q28","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female long-distance runner presents with lateral hip pain over the greater trochanter for 3 months, worsening with running, single-leg squat, and stair climbing. Passive hip range of motion is fully preserved and pain-free. MRI shows increased signal at the gluteus medius tendon insertion on the greater trochanter. What is the MOST accurate diagnosis and first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"True greater trochanteric bursitis; first-line treatment is intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection for bursitis","B":"Hip labral tear; first-line treatment is arthroscopic labral repair given the lateral hip pain in a runner","C":"Gluteus medius tendinopathy; first-line treatment is gait retraining and hip abductor strengthening to correct the load","D":"IT band friction syndrome; first-line treatment is foam rolling and stretching of the iliotibial band insertionally","E":"Hip OA; first-line treatment follows OARSI guidelines with structured land-based exercise and education as core care"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MRI showing signal change at the gluteus medius tendon insertion — not the bursa — confirms gluteus medius tendinopathy, the actual pathology underlying most cases of so-called \"greater trochanteric bursitis.\" Preserved passive hip ROM distinguishes this extra-articular tendinopathy from intra-articular hip OA (which restricts passive motion). First-line treatment is biomechanical correction: gait retraining (reduce hip adduction during stance), hip abductor strengthening, and load management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater trochanteric \"bursitis\" = gluteus medius tendinopathy in most cases. MRI confirms tendon pathology. Passive ROM preserved = extra-articular. Treatment: biomechanics first; escalation options = CSI, regenerative injection (PRP), NTG patches, tenotomy. COMLEX: Gluteus medius weakness = Trendelenburg pattern; OMT addresses hip-pelvis somatic dysfunction driving tendinopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — lateral hip pain with preserved passive ROM represents step 5 of the hip pain workflow: the localized pain generator is the gluteus medius tendon, not the intra-articular hip joint","whyNot":{"E":"Preserved passive hip ROM dist"}},{"id":"sdl5_q29","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has lateral upper thigh pain that radiates down the lateral thigh and is worse when she lies on that side at night. The pain does not reach the groin. She reports that one leg is noticeably longer than the other, and years ago similar pain went away after she added a lift to the shoe of the shorter leg. Which intervention is most directly suggested by her history?","choices":{"A":"Total hip arthroplasty, because lateral thigh pain at night in an older woman marks end-stage osteoarthritis of the joint","B":"Core decompression of the femoral head, because night pain with a limb length change signals femoral head collapse","C":"Open reduction and internal fixation, because limb shortening with lateral pain points to an occult femoral neck fracture","D":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve block, because lateral thigh pain worse at night is from nerve compression at the ligament","E":"Correcting the leg length discrepancy with a shoe lift, because the discrepancy can drive her greater trochanteric pain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral upper thigh pain that radiates down the thigh, spares the groin, and worsens when lying on the affected side is the classic trochanteric presentation described in the history chapter's case. That case notes trochanteric pain may be caused by a leg length discrepancy and improve when the discrepancy is corrected, which fits her earlier response to a shoe lift. True hip osteoarthritis radiates to the groin and worsens with joint motion, so arthroplasty is not indicated. Core decompression treats pre-collapse avascular necrosis, and she has no AVN risk factors or groin pain. A femoral neck fracture presents with groin pain and inability to bear weight after trauma. Meralgia paresthetica causes burning anterolateral thigh paresthesias, not pain over the trochanter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greater trochanteric pain syndrome is mostly gluteus medius tendinopathy; first-line care is biomechanical correction (gait retraining, abductor strengthening, leg length correction). COMLEX: Leg length discrepancy and pelvic obliquity are treatable biomechanical contributors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — prescribing footwear and orthotic changes based on biomechanics","whyNot":{"D":"Meralgia paresthetica causes b"}},{"id":"sdl5_q30","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip overuse injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female ballet dancer describes an audible, painless snap at the left hip that occurs reproducibly when she swings her leg forward during pliés. She has no pain at rest and no mechanical locking. Which structure MOST likely causes this external snapping hip syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The iliotibial band snapping over the greater trochanter as the hip moves from extension to flexion, producing an external audible snap","B":"The iliopsoas tendon flipping over the iliopectineal eminence, producing an internal deep groin snap during hip flexion","C":"A hip labral tear causing intermittent femoral head subluxation with an audible click during hip rotation maneuvers","D":"A loose osteochondral body within the hip joint impinging during range of motion with reproducible snapping","E":"Greater trochanteric bursitis causing fluid displacement over the trochanteric prominence with hip motion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"External snapping hip syndrome occurs when the iliotibial band (or posterior edge of the gluteus maximus aponeurosis) snaps over the greater trochanter with hip flexion and extension. It produces a reproducible lateral snap or click that is audible and often palpable over the greater trochanter. Internal snapping hip involves the iliopsoas tendon over the iliopectineal eminence (anterior/deep groin snap). External snapping is the most common form; it is usually painless and benign, managed conservatively with IT band stretching and hip flexor flexibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: External snapping hip = IT band over greater trochanter = lateral audible snap. Internal snapping hip = iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence = anterior deep groin snap. Intra-articular snapping = loose body or labral tear = painful mechanical click. COMLEX: Painless snapping hip without impingement is managed conservatively; OMT can address IT band and hip flexor somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — snapping hip (coxa saltans) is classified as external (IT band over greater trochanter) or internal (iliopsoas over iliopectineal eminence); the lateral location of snapping distinguishes external from internal types"},{"id":"sdl5_q31","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A primary care physician is reviewing hip fracture prevention for an 80-year-old woman with osteoporosis. Besides her age, sex, and osteoporosis, which additional patient factor is listed in the course as a risk factor for hip fracture?","choices":{"A":"A body mass index above 30, because a heavier body raises the energy of a fall","B":"Male sex after age 65, because men have a smaller femoral neck cross section","C":"A low body mass index below 22, with little soft tissue padding over the hip","D":"Regular moderate exercise, because more time upright exposes her to falls","E":"Hypothyroidism on replacement, because thyroxine slows osteoblast activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists increasing age, female sex, osteoporosis, and low BMI (under 22) as hip fracture risk factors, with associations including endocrine disease (hyperthyroidism, diabetes), cardiovascular disease, and low socioeconomic status. A thin patient has less padding over the trochanter and often lower bone mass. A high BMI is not the listed risk factor. Female, not male, sex is the listed risk; femoral neck fractures are three times more common in women. Regular exercise is not a listed risk factor. The endocrine association named is hyperthyroidism (excess thyroid hormone accelerates bone turnover), not treated hypothyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture risk = age, female sex, osteoporosis, low BMI (<22); associations include hyperthyroidism, diabetes, CV disease. COMLEX: About one third of hip fractures go on to total hip arthroplasty; fall prevention and bone health are core primary care interventions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — hormones of bone remodeling, including thyroid hormone effects on bone turnover","whyNot":{"A":"A high BMI is not the listed r","D":"Regular exercise is not a list","E":"The endocrine association name"}},{"id":"sdl5_q32","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old male motorcycle rider is brought to the ED after a collision. He is found to have his right hip held in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Neurovascular assessment shows diminished sensation over the posterior thigh and lateral leg. Which type of hip dislocation is MOST likely, and what is the urgency of reduction?","choices":{"A":"Anterior hip dislocation with femoral nerve injury; reduction can be delayed up to 24 hours safely without AVN risk","B":"Central fracture-dislocation with pudendal nerve injury; emergent CT must be completed before any reduction attempt","C":"Superior dislocation with superior gluteal nerve injury; urgent arthroscopic reduction is the preferred technique","D":"Posterior hip dislocation with peroneal nerve injury only; sciatic involvement is not possible in posterior dislocations","E":"Posterior hip dislocation with sciatic nerve injury; reduce urgently, as soon as possible, to limit AVN and ischemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Posterior hip dislocation — the most common type — presents with the hip in flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. The sciatic nerve lies posterior to the joint and is at risk of stretch injury, causing posterior thigh sensory loss (semitendinosus, semimembranosus territory) and lateral leg sensory loss (common peroneal component producing risk of foot drop). Reduction should be achieved as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours after injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics), to minimize both AVN risk (from femoral head ischemia) and sciatic nerve stretch injury. Emergent closed reduction under sedation is standard.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior hip dislocation = hip flexed, adducted, internally rotated. Sciatic nerve at risk (foot drop from peroneal division). Reduce as soon as possible, preferably within the first few hours, to limit AVN. FA: inferior gluteal nerve at risk in posterior dislocation = difficulty rising from chair/climbing stairs. COMLEX: Hip dislocation is a CP8 named joint dislocation requiring urgent reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — posterior hip dislocation is a traumatic cause of femoral head AVN; urgency of reduction (within 6 hours) is driven by the same vascular disruption mechanism as femoral neck fracture-related AVN"},{"id":"sdl5_q33","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of hip fracture and dislocation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male with a high-velocity skiing injury presents with right hip pain and X-ray showing a subtrochanteric femur fracture. Which of the following BEST describes the epidemiology of subtrochanteric fractures?","choices":{"A":"Subtrochanteric fractures are the most common hip fracture type in women over 65 and have no bimodal distribution","B":"Subtrochanteric fractures are seen exclusively in elderly women over age 75 and are always associated with osteoporosis","C":"Subtrochanteric fractures are bimodal, peaking at ages 20-40 (high-energy trauma) and over 60 (osteoporotic falls)","D":"Subtrochanteric fractures occur predominantly in male athletes aged 15-25 from high-impact sports participation","E":"Subtrochanteric fractures are a pediatric injury seen exclusively in Salter-Harris physeal injury patterns under age 15"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal age distribution: a younger peak at ages 20-40 caused by high-energy trauma (MVA, falls from height, sports injuries) and an older peak above age 60 caused by osteoporotic low-energy falls. This is in contrast to intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures, which predominantly occur in elderly women. Intertrochanteric and femoral neck fractures are the most common types in women over 65, with femoral neck occurring 3x more often in women than men.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hip fracture epidemiology by type — elderly women: intertrochanteric + femoral neck (most common). Subtrochanteric: bimodal (20-40 trauma, >60 osteoporosis). Femoral neck: 3x more common in women. COMLEX: Hip fracture in elderly = CP8 fracture; prevention requires fall risk assessment and osteoporosis treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — subtrochanteric fractures have a bimodal distribution reflecting two distinct injury mechanisms: high-energy trauma in young adults and osteoporotic low-energy falls in the elderly population"},{"id":"sdl5_q34","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl who plays competitive soccer has 2 months of pain under the ball of the foot that is worse with running. There is tenderness over the head of the second metatarsal. Radiographs show flattening and sclerosis of the second metatarsal head. The course groups this condition with other forms of avascular necrosis in children. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Köhler disease, an osteochondrosis of the tarsal navicular that most often affects children aged 4 to 7","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, an osteochondrosis of the femoral head that most often affects ages 4 to 9","C":"Freiberg infarction, an osteochondrosis of the 2nd, 3rd, or 4th metatarsal, especially at 11 to 17","D":"Sever disease, a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus at the Achilles insertion in active children","E":"Morton neuroma, a perineural fibrosis of the third common digital nerve between the metatarsal heads"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course defines osteochondrosis as AVN in children and lists three types: Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease of the hip (ages 4 to 9), Köhler disease of the tarsal navicular (ages 4 to 7), and Freiberg infarction of the second, third, or fourth metatarsal (any age, especially 11 to 17). A teenager with second metatarsal head pain, flattening, and sclerosis has Freiberg infarction. Köhler disease affects the navicular in younger children. Legg-Calvé-Perthes involves the femoral head and presents with a limp and hip, thigh, or knee pain. Sever disease is a traction apophysitis at the heel, not osteonecrosis of a metatarsal. Morton neuroma is a nerve lesion with burning forefoot pain and normal bone on radiographs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteochondroses (pediatric AVN): Legg-Calvé-Perthes = femoral head (4–9 y), Köhler = navicular (4–7 y), Freiberg = 2nd–4th metatarsal head (11–17 y). COMLEX: AVN is ischemic death of marrow and bone; preserving the joint is the treatment goal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, the hip osteochondrosis of childhood","whyNot":{"B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes involves th"}},{"id":"sdl5_q35","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old male with sickle cell disease presents with severe bilateral groin pain, worse at night and with weight bearing. X-ray shows sclerosis in the right femoral head without crescent sign and a crescent sign with 3mm femoral head depression in the left. Which ARCO stages correspond to each hip, and which hip requires more urgent surgical consultation?","choices":{"A":"Right hip = Stage I (normal X-ray); left hip = Stage II (sclerosis); neither requires urgent surgical evaluation","B":"Right hip = Stage III (crescent sign); left hip = Stage IV (OA changes); both require total hip arthroplasty now","C":"Right hip = Stage IV (OA changes); left hip = Stage IIIA (depression ≤2mm); right hip is more urgent for arthroplasty","D":"Right hip = Stage II (sclerosis); left hip = Stage I (MRI only); surgical consultation is not yet warranted for either hip","E":"Right hip = Stage II (sclerosis); left hip = Stage IIIB (depression >2mm); the collapsed left hip is more urgent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ARCO staging: Stage II = X-ray shows sclerosis, osteolysis, or osteoporosis without crescent sign or collapse (right hip here); Stage IIIA = femoral head depression ≤2mm; Stage IIIB = depression >2mm (left hip with 3mm depression). The left hip at Stage IIIB has established structural collapse and requires urgent surgical evaluation for core decompression, osteotomy, or arthroplasty depending on extent. The right hip at Stage II is pre-collapse and eligible for joint-preserving procedures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARCO staging key cutpoints — Stage II = X-ray changes without crescent/collapse. Stage IIIA = ≤2mm depression. Stage IIIB = >2mm depression. Stage IV = OA + joint space narrowing. Sickle cell disease is a major cause of bilateral AVN. COMLEX: Pain at rest and nocturnal pain are characteristic of AVN, distinguishing it from OA (activity-related pain).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — ARCO staging guides treatment decisions in AVN; Stage I-II = joint-preserving (core decompression); Stage III-IV = reconstruction/arthroplasty; sickle cell disease causes bilateral AVN through vascular sludging in the femoral head watershed","whyNot":{"A":"The left hip at Stage IIIB has","B":"The right hip at Stage II is p","D":"The left hip at Stage IIIB has"}},{"id":"sdl5_q36","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of avascular necrosis of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old female is diagnosed with ARCO Stage II AVN of the femoral head secondary to chronic alcohol use. No femoral head collapse has occurred. Which of the following represents the MOST appropriate conservative management options for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Immediate total hip arthroplasty since Stage II AVN always progresses to collapse and arthroplasty is the only cure","B":"Activity reduction, analgesics or NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, and physical therapy to delay femoral head collapse","C":"Systemic corticosteroid therapy to reduce inflammation in the femoral head and halt ischemic progression","D":"Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection into the hip as the primary treatment for Stage II femoral head AVN","E":"Complete hip immobilization with non-weight-bearing cast for 12 weeks as the definitive conservative treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conservative management of AVN at Stage II (pre-collapse) focuses on: (1) activity reduction to minimize femoral head loading, (2) analgesics/NSAIDs for pain, (3) bisphosphonates (reduce osteoclast activity and may slow progression), and (4) physical therapy to maintain function. No clearly superior treatment plan exists, but the goal is joint preservation. Investigational options include anticoagulants, statins, ESWT, and hyperbaric oxygen. Surgery (core decompression, osteotomy, arthroplasty) is reserved for progressive or end-stage disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVN conservative management = activity reduction + NSAIDs + bisphosphonates + PT. No treatment reverses established necrosis — goal is joint preservation. Core decompression = surgical option for Stage I-II (pre-collapse). Stage III-IV = osteotomy or arthroplasty. COMLEX: Alcohol use disorder is a major modifiable AVN risk factor; counseling and cessation are part of management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hip fracture and dislocation are traumatic causes of femoral head AVN; alcohol-induced AVN shares the same endpoint of progressive femoral head ischemia and collapse requiring staged joint-preserving or reconstructive interventions","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery (core decompression, o"}},{"id":"sdl5_q37","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has 3 days of right flank and anterior hip pain, fever, a limp, and pain with resisted hip flexion. WBC and CRP are elevated, and blood cultures are drawn. A contrast CT of the abdomen and pelvis shows no collection. Why should a psoas abscess still not be excluded?","choices":{"A":"CT cannot image the retroperitoneum, so the iliopsoas compartment must instead be examined with plain radiographs of the pelvis","B":"CT detects secondary abscesses from bowel disease, so a hematogenous abscess in a diabetic patient requires MRI","C":"Contrast enhancement masks fluid in the iliopsoas, so the CT must be repeated without contrast to show the abscess cavity","D":"Psoas abscess is a clinical diagnosis, so imaging has no role and drainage is planned from the examination findings","E":"CT is about 90% sensitive overall but falls to about 33% sensitive when symptoms have lasted less than 6 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course gives CT a sensitivity of about 90% for psoas abscess, but only about 33% when symptoms have lasted less than 6 days, so an early negative CT does not exclude the diagnosis in a patient with fever, flank and hip pain, a limp, and pain with resisted hip flexion. Diabetes is a listed risk factor for primary (hematogenous) abscess. CT does image the retroperitoneum, and plain films are not the test for an abscess. CT is not limited to secondary abscesses. Contrast does not mask the collection; contrast CT is the standard study. Imaging plays a central role; the course pairs CBC, ESR, CRP, LFTs, and blood cultures with CT, and treatment is incision and drainage with IV antibiotics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoas abscess: fever, flank/hip pain, limp, pain with resisted hip flexion (psoas sign); S. aureus most common; CT ~90% sensitive (≈33% if <6 days). COMLEX: Primary risk factors include diabetes, renal failure, HIV/immunosuppression, IV drug use, epidural anesthesia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — red-flag back pain from spinal infection","whyNot":{"B":"CT is not limited to secondary","C":"Contrast does not mask the col"}},{"id":"sdl5_q38","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 4 days of right hip pain, fever of 39.1°C, and refusal to bear weight. ESR is 65 mm/h, WBC is 14,200, and CRP is 3.2 mg/dL. He is ill-appearing. Modified Kocher score is 4-5. Which of the following is the MOST appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Outpatient NSAID therapy and repeat Kocher score assessment in 48 hours to monitor for improvement or decline","B":"Urgent surgical aspiration and lavage of the hip joint with intravenous antibiotics for septic arthritis","C":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration for fluid analysis without lavage, followed by oral antibiotics if WBC >100,000","D":"Watchful waiting with IV hydration and bed rest while awaiting blood culture results before starting antibiotics","E":"MRI of the hip to confirm effusion before any aspiration or antibiotic therapy is initiated in this child"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Modified Kocher score 4-5 = 99.6% probability of septic hip arthritis. This is a surgical emergency — delay in treatment leads to rapid femoral head cartilage destruction and permanent disability. Management requires: (1) surgical aspiration/lavage of the joint, and (2) IV antibiotics (anti-staphylococcal coverage first-line). Delayed aspiration or conservative treatment alone is inappropriate at this Kocher score. The key pitfall is distinguishing this from transient synovitis (afebrile, normal WBC/ESR, Kocher score 0-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kocher score 4 = 99.6% septic arthritis = surgical emergency. Treatment = surgical lavage + IV antibiotics. Contrast with transient synovitis (Kocher score 0 = <0.2% risk). COMLEX: Septic hip is a named CP8 pediatric musculoskeletal emergency; joint destruction occurs within days without surgical intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 6 — modified Kocher criteria are the validated decision tool for septic hip vs. transient synovitis in children; score of 4-5 mandates urgent surgical aspiration/lavage regardless of imaging findings","whyNot":{"A":"Delayed aspiration or conserva","D":"Delayed aspiration or conserva"}},{"id":"sdl5_q39","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of a psoas abscess and septic hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin presents with fever, right flank pain, and a limp. He has no gastrointestinal, urinary, spinal, or prior hip surgery history. CT shows a collection within the right iliopsoas compartment. How is this abscess classified, and why is this compartment prone to infection?","choices":{"A":"Secondary abscess from contiguous spread, because injected drug enters the psoas directly from a nearby femoral vein","B":"Secondary abscess from vertebral osteomyelitis, because injection drug use seeds the disc before the muscle is involved","C":"Primary abscess of hematogenous origin, because the muscle has rich blood flow and lies near retroperitoneal lymphatics","D":"Primary abscess from Crohn disease, because occult transmural ileitis is the usual source in young patients without history","E":"Tuberculous cold abscess, because injection drug use classically reactivates spinal tuberculosis that tracks along the psoas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides psoas abscess into primary, which is hematogenous or lymphatic in origin, and secondary, from direct spread from an adjacent structure (vertebrae, infected hip arthroplasty, GI tract especially Crohn disease, GU tract, aorta). IV drug use is a listed primary risk factor, along with renal failure, HIV or immunosuppression, diabetes, epidural anesthesia, and trauma with hematoma. The compartment is vulnerable because of its very rich blood flow and its closeness to retroperitoneal lymphatics. Spread from a femoral injection site or from vertebral osteomyelitis would be contiguous, and he has no spinal or adjacent source. Crohn disease causes secondary, not primary, abscesses. Tuberculous cold abscess is not the course classification and is not tied to injection drug use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary psoas abscess = hematogenous/lymphatic (IVDU, HIV, diabetes, renal failure); secondary = contiguous spread (Crohn, vertebral osteomyelitis, GU, infected THA, aorta). Most common organism S. aureus. COMLEX: Treatment = incision/drainage + IV antibiotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — dangerous conditions (infection) screened in step 2 of the hip pain workflow","whyNot":{"A":"Spread from a femoral injectio","D":"Crohn disease causes secondary"}},{"id":"sdl5_q40","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old male with BMI of 42 and type 2 diabetes presents with a 3-month history of burning pain and numbness over the right anterior and lateral thigh. He works as a long-haul truck driver and wears a tight tool belt. Neurological exam shows no motor weakness, intact patellar reflex, and decreased sensation over the anterolateral thigh only. Which diagnosis BEST explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"L3 radiculopathy from lumbar disc herniation, producing anterior thigh pain with quadriceps weakness and reduced patellar reflex","B":"Meralgia paresthetica from LFCN compression by the tool belt and obesity, with purely sensory symptoms","C":"Femoral neuropathy from diabetic amyotrophy, producing proximal leg pain and weakness with reduced patellar reflex","D":"Obturator nerve entrapment producing medial thigh adductor weakness and medial thigh sensory loss","E":"Sciatic nerve compression producing posterior thigh pain with foot drop and reduced Achilles tendon reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meralgia paresthetica (LFCN compression) presents with pure sensory symptoms — burning, tingling, numbness over the anterolateral thigh — without any motor deficit or reflex change. The LFCN is a purely sensory nerve. Key distinguishing features: no motor weakness (eliminates radiculopathy and femoral/obturator neuropathy), intact reflexes, and sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh. Risk factors include obesity, DM (7x risk), tight belts, and prolonged sitting/pressure — all present here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica = LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensory only, NO motor, intact reflexes. Distinguish from L3 radiculopathy (quadriceps weakness, reduced patellar reflex), femoral neuropathy (motor deficit + reflex loss). FA: LFCN = anterolateral thigh sensation; causes = obesity, pregnancy, tight clothing. COMLEX: LFCN passes under/through inguinal ligament at ASIS; local palpation at ASIS may reproduce symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — meralgia paresthetica is differentiated from lumbar radiculopathy by the absence of motor deficit and preserved deep tendon reflexes; sensory loss confined to the anterolateral thigh confirms LFCN rather than L2-L3 root involvement","whyNot":{"A":"Key distinguishing features: n"}},{"id":"sdl5_q41","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old female in her 32nd week of pregnancy develops burning and tingling over the right anterolateral thigh that began 3 weeks ago. Motor exam is normal. Patellar reflex is intact. She finds the symptoms most bothersome at night. Which is the MOST accurate explanation for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"L3-L4 disc herniation from pregnancy-related lumbar hyperlordosis causing radiculopathy into the anterior thigh","B":"Femoral nerve stretch from increased pelvic tilt during pregnancy causing quadriceps weakness and anterior thigh pain","C":"Obturator nerve entrapment from pelvic widening during pregnancy causing medial thigh burning and adductor weakness","D":"Piriformis syndrome from pelvic floor changes during pregnancy compressing the sciatic nerve in the gluteal region","E":"Gravid uterus pressure compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament (meralgia paresthetica), resolving postpartum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-associated meralgia paresthetica results from increased abdominal pressure (gravid uterus) and altered pelvic mechanics compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament. It presents with burning and tingling in the anterolateral thigh without motor deficit — characteristic of pure LFCN sensory compression. It typically resolves spontaneously within weeks to months after delivery as abdominal pressure decreases. Management is expectant with reassurance; positioning changes (avoiding prolonged sitting) may help.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy = major risk factor for meralgia paresthetica via increased abdominal pressure. Pure sensory (anterolateral thigh), NO motor deficit. Management = reassurance + symptom modification; resolves postpartum. COMLEX: OMT is safe in pregnancy and can address iliac/inguinal somatic dysfunction contributing to LFCN compression during pregnancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — pregnancy causes meralgia paresthetica through increased abdominal pressure compressing the LFCN at the inguinal ligament; this is the same mechanism as obesity and ascites, both of which increase intra-abdominal pressure"},{"id":"sdl5_q42","sdlNumber":5,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment of meralgia paresthetica.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with obesity-related meralgia paresthetica fails to improve after weight loss counseling over 3 months and lifestyle modification. He has persistent 7/10 burning pain over the anterolateral thigh limiting work and sleep. Which is the MOST appropriate escalation of treatment for refractory meralgia paresthetica?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar epidural steroid injection targeting the L2-L3 level to reduce radicular inflammation around the LFCN origin","B":"Intra-articular hip joint corticosteroid injection to reduce local inflammation compressing the LFCN at the joint margin","C":"Anticonvulsants such as gabapentin or pregabalin, or anesthesia referral for an LFCN block at the inguinal ligament","D":"Surgical hip arthroplasty to decompress the LFCN by relieving hip joint capsule tension on the surrounding nerves","E":"Systemic oral corticosteroid taper for 6 weeks to reduce perineural inflammation around the compressed LFCN"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For refractory meralgia paresthetica (failed conservative management including treating the underlying cause), escalation options include: (1) anticonvulsants — gabapentin or pregabalin — for neuropathic pain management, and (2) anesthesia consultation for nerve block injection at the inguinal ligament level (LFCN block). Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rarely needed. Lumbar epidural and hip injections target the wrong anatomic structure. The course slides explicitly list anticonvulsants and anesthesia consultation as the treatment options for recalcitrant cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meralgia paresthetica treatment = treat cause first (weight loss, remove compression). Recalcitrant = anticonvulsants (gabapentin/pregabalin) or anesthesia consult for LFCN block. Surgical decompression or neurectomy is rare. COMLEX: LFCN block at ASIS is a targeted intervention that confirms diagnosis and provides therapeutic benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — anticonvulsant medications (gabapentin, pregabalin) are the recognized pharmacologic escalation for refractory meralgia paresthetica when treating the underlying cause fails to resolve the neuropathic pain","whyNot":{"B":"Lumbar epidural and hip inject"}}],"7":[{"id":"sdl7_q01","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which structure is the primary site of pathology in medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS)?","choices":{"A":"Posteromedial tibial periosteum, subjected to repetitive traction from posterior compartment muscles including the tibialis posterior, soleus, and flexor digitorum longus","B":"Anterior tibial cortex, stressed by axial compressive loading from repetitive heel strike impact forces during high-mileage training programs in competitive distance runners","C":"Lateral tibial cortex, subjected to shear stress from excessive ankle pronation and supination cycling forces during push-off in runners with biomechanical malalignment","D":"Medial tibial endosteum, injured by elevated intramedullary pressure from increased bone marrow vascularity during rapid progression of aerobic training load in athletes","E":"Posterior tibial diaphysis, compressed by increased quadriceps and hamstring co-activation forces generating axial tibial stress during high-intensity interval training"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The primary pathological site in MTSS is the posteromedial tibial periosteum, which is subjected to repetitive traction from the origins of the posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL). The anterior tibial cortex is more associated with anterior tibial stress fractures, while endosteal pathology suggests deeper marrow injury. Identifying the periosteum as the site explains why overpronation — which increases traction on the posteromedial periosteum — is the strongest modifiable risk factor.","boardPrep":"MTSS = traction periostitis at origins of posterior compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, soleus, FDL) on posteromedial tibia. Steps: repetitive activity → posterior compartment overuse → traction periostitis → bone remodeling → fracture if no rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor","whyNot":{"B":"The anterior tibial cortex is ","D":"The anterior tibial cortex is "}},{"id":"sdl7_q02","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone scan finding best differentiates medial tibial stress syndrome from a tibial stress fracture?","choices":{"A":"Focal, intense, well-demarcated area of increased uptake at a single point on the tibial cortex, indicating a stress fracture at that specific discrete cortical location","B":"Completely normal bone scan bilaterally with no areas of increased uptake despite significant clinical symptoms and prolonged posteromedial tibial tenderness on examination","C":"Patchy, diffuse areas of increased uptake spanning several centimeters along the posteromedial tibial cortex without any single focal hot spot on the delayed-phase images","D":"Increased uptake in the anterior tibial compartment bilaterally reflecting tibialis anterior muscle inflammation from overuse training in a distance runner during peak mileage","E":"Focal uptake at the tibial metaphysis bilaterally suggesting growth plate stress injury rather than periosteal or cortical bone involvement in the skeletally immature athlete"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key differentiator between MTSS and tibial stress fracture on bone scan is the distribution of tracer uptake: MTSS produces patchy, diffuse uptake spanning centimeters along the posteromedial cortex, while a stress fracture shows a focal, intense single-point hot spot at the fracture site. Both conditions can be bilateral, so symmetry is not the distinguishing feature. A normal scan does not separate the two: the bone scan is often negative in MTSS, so only the uptake pattern (diffuse vs focal) distinguishes them.","boardPrep":"MTSS bone scan: patchy diffuse uptake. Stress fracture: focal intense hot spot at one site. MRI: periosteal fluid, bone marrow signal in MTSS. X-ray often negative in MTSS; may show periosteal scalloping or cortical thickening late.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor","whyNot":{"B":"A normal scan does not separat"}},{"id":"sdl7_q03","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for medial tibial stress syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Increased external hip rotation beyond 15 degrees measured during gait analysis in female distance runners with a history of recurrent lower extremity overuse injuries","B":"Decreased lumbar spine bone mineral density measured by DEXA scan in collegiate athletes with amenorrhea from relative energy deficiency in sport during competitive season","C":"Use of minimalist footwear with a heel-to-toe drop below 4mm in athletes who transition abruptly from traditional cushioned running shoes during high-mileage training","D":"Increased tibial torsion angle above 20 degrees measured on CT scanogram of the lower extremity in military recruits during the first weeks of intensive basic training","E":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial tibial periosteum from the posterior compartment muscles during the loading phase of gait"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Overpronation of the subtalar joint is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, increasing traction forces on the posteromedial periosteum from posterior compartment muscle origins during the loading phase of gait. External hip rotation, bone mineral density, footwear style, and tibial torsion are proposed risk factors but have weaker or less consistent evidence than subtalar overpronation in the published literature on MTSS.","boardPrep":"MTSS: the only confirmed ('Yes') risk factor on the course slide is overpronation; training errors, footwear, surface, fatigue, flexibility, low BMD and hypermobile subtalar joint are 'maybe'. Male military recruits: ↑ hip internal and external rotation, less calf girth. Females 3× more likely to get MTSS than males.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — MTSS: diffuse posteromedial tibial periostitis from posterior compartment traction; X-ray often negative; overpronation is the confirmed risk factor","whyNot":{"A":"External hip rotation, bone mi","B":"External hip rotation, bone mi","C":"External hip rotation, bone mi","D":"External hip rotation, bone mi"}},{"id":"sdl7_q04","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of ankle sprains?","choices":{"A":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral sprain cases seen clinically","B":"Medial ankle sprains are the most common type, occurring at 2.7 per 1000 athlete exposures and frequently requiring surgical repair of the deltoid ligament complex","C":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprains account for 40-60% of all ankle injuries and most cases require orthopedic surgical fixation to restore distal tibiofibular joint stability","D":"Ankle sprains affect males more than females with an overall incidence of 7 per 1000 person-years in male athletes compared to female counterparts in all age groups","E":"Lateral ankle sprains carry a 45% recurrence rate and invariably progress to chronic ankle instability requiring surgical ligament reconstruction within 2 years of injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprains account for 70-90% of all ankle sprains, with the ATFL injured in isolation in approximately 73% of lateral cases. Medial sprains are rare (0.06/1000 athlete exposures), not the most common type. Syndesmotic sprains account for only 0.38/1000 — not 40-60%. Ankle sprains occur more frequently in females than males, and the recurrence rate is approximately 11.9%, not 45%.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprains: 2 million/year in US. Lateral 70-90%; ATFL injured in 73% of lateral cases. Females > males. Children/adolescents > adults. Medial: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures. Syndesmotic: 0.38/1000. Recurrence rate 11.9%.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Ottawa rules separate a sprain from a fracture that needs radiographs","whyNot":{"D":"Ankle sprains occur more frequ"}},{"id":"sdl7_q05","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces a lateral ankle sprain with isolated ATFL injury?","choices":{"A":"Dorsiflexion combined with internal rotation of the tibia over a fixed foot, stressing the anterior talofibular ligament during a rapid deceleration maneuver in sport","B":"Eversion combined with external rotation of the ankle while planting the foot, stressing the calcaneofibular and deltoid ligament complex in a multiligamentous injury","C":"Plantarflexion combined with tibial external rotation stressing the anterior tibiofibular ligament and the interosseous membrane of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis","D":"Inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, placing the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and making it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading","E":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation stressing the deltoid ligament medially and the anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis simultaneously"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateral ankle sprain most commonly occurs with inversion while the foot is in plantarflexion, which orients the ATFL perpendicular to the fibula and makes it the first lateral structure to fail under tensile loading. Dorsiflexion with external rotation stresses the syndesmosis; dorsiflexion with internal rotation is not the lateral-sprain mechanism (the ATFL is lax in dorsiflexion). Eversion with external rotation injures the deltoid complex, not the lateral ligaments.","boardPrep":"Lateral ankle sprain mechanism: inversion with plantarflexion. Order of injury: ATFL first (73% isolated) → CFL → PTFL. Medial sprain: eversion and dorsiflexion. Syndesmotic: dorsiflexion + external rotation. Anterior drawer tests ATFL; talar tilt tests CFL; squeeze and external rotation tests syndesmosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Ottawa rules separate a sprain from a fracture that needs radiographs","whyNot":{"A":"Dorsiflexion with external rot"}},{"id":"sdl7_q06","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Ottawa Ankle Rules, which criterion mandates ankle radiograph in a patient presenting with ankle pain?","choices":{"A":"Anterior ankle tenderness at the ATFL with visible swelling and ecchymosis, regardless of whether the patient can bear weight on the injured extremity at time of evaluation","B":"Midfoot tenderness at the cuboid or base of the 5th metatarsal with inability to bear weight, which triggers foot radiographs rather than standard ankle view series","C":"Posterior medial malleolus tenderness over the distal 6cm, posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps immediately after injury","D":"Lateral ankle tenderness anterior to the ATFL with a positive anterior drawer test indicating ligament laxity but no point tenderness over the malleoli on palpation","E":"Visible ankle swelling appearing within 2 hours of injury with complete inability to complete active range of motion in plantarflexion or dorsiflexion upon examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ottawa Ankle Rules mandate radiographs when there is posterior medial malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), posterior lateral malleolus tenderness, or inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Anterior ATFL tenderness and visible swelling alone do NOT trigger ankle X-rays under the Ottawa rules. Midfoot tenderness at the navicular or 5th metatarsal base triggers foot radiographs, not ankle views. Positive anterior drawer without malleolar tenderness does not mandate imaging.","boardPrep":"Ottawa Ankle Rules — ankle X-ray if: (1) posterior lateral malleolus tenderness (distal 6cm), (2) posterior medial malleolus tenderness, OR (3) inability to bear weight for 4 steps. Foot X-ray if: navicular tenderness OR 5th metatarsal base tenderness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Ottawa rules separate a sprain from a fracture that needs radiographs"},{"id":"sdl7_q07","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury most commonly produces an acute gastrocnemius strain at the medial musculotendinous junction?","choices":{"A":"Sustained plantarflexion against resistance during repetitive uphill running causing concentric overload and progressive degenerative change at the medial gastrocnemius origin","B":"Rapid deceleration with the knee extended causing simultaneous hamstring and gastrocnemius strain with diffuse posterior knee pain in middle-aged recreational athletes","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading during prolonged downhill running causing gradual degenerative microtrauma at the Achilles tendon myotendinous junction proximal to insertion","D":"Sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, causing eccentric overstretch of the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous unit during push-off","E":"Direct blunt contusion to the posterior calf from contact during a team sport causing a localized intramuscular hematoma within the lateral gastrocnemius muscle belly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gastrocnemius medial head strain (tennis leg) classically occurs with sudden acceleration from a stationary position with the ankle in dorsiflexion, producing eccentric overstretch at the musculotendinous junction during push-off. Sustained plantarflexion against resistance causes concentric, not eccentric, overload. Rapid deceleration with the knee extended more commonly stresses the hamstrings. Direct blunt contusion causes intramuscular hematoma at the point of impact, not at the medial MTJ.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain (tennis leg): sudden acceleration from stationary position with ankle dorsiflexed (lunging in tennis or squash). Eccentric overstretch; injury at transition from eccentric to isometric contraction. Medial head most commonly injured. Classic: sudden pop, \"hit in the calf\" sensation, tenderness at medial MTJ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q08","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical finding distinguishes a Grade III gastrocnemius strain from a Grade II injury?","choices":{"A":"Severe posterior calf pain during passive dorsiflexion that prevents the patient from completing full range of motion assessment during the clinical physical examination","B":"Positive Thompson test with a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction indicating complete rupture of the medial gastrocnemius with surgical repair required","C":"Significant ecchymosis tracking distally to the heel within 24 hours of injury due to gravitational spread of blood along the posterior leg fascial planes after trauma","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg calf raise on the affected side within the first 72 hours of the injury due to pain and weakness of the posterior calf compartment","E":"Loss of active plantarflexion strength measured against gravity with the ankle held at 20 degrees of dorsiflexion, distinguishing Grade II from Grade I partial tear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Grade III gastrocnemius strain is defined by complete rupture of the medial head, producing a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the musculotendinous junction. Grade II has active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM but Thompson is negative and no palpable defect exists. Ecchymosis and inability to calf raise occur in both Grade II and III and therefore do not distinguish the grades from each other.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grades: Grade I — sharp pain, can calf raise, returns in 10-12 days. Grade II — unable to continue activity, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, returns in 16-21 days. Grade III — immediate severe pain, positive Thompson test, palpable defect at MTJ, surgical repair, returns in 6 months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test","whyNot":{"C":"Ecchymosis and inability to ca"}},{"id":"sdl7_q09","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which progressive rehabilitation sequence is most appropriate within the first 24 hours of a gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Begin concentric calf strengthening exercises, progressing to weighted calf raises and then eccentric calf lowering exercises as pain allows for full functional restoration","B":"Apply a full leg cast in equinus for 6 weeks with strict non-weight bearing to protect the myotendinous junction from any mechanical loading during the acute healing phase","C":"Administer a corticosteroid injection directly into the musculotendinous junction under ultrasound guidance to reduce the acute inflammatory response within 24 hours","D":"Proceed with surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius head regardless of clinical grade determination on initial physical examination findings","E":"Apply deep tissue massage and myofascial release techniques directly over the site of the muscle tear within the first 24 hours to limit early scar tissue formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation begins within 24 hours with concentric calf strengthening, then progressive loading with weight, then eccentric calf lowering as pain allows. Full equinus casting is reserved only for Grade III complete rupture after surgical repair. Corticosteroid injection and surgery are not indicated for Grade I or Grade II strains. The progression from concentric to eccentric loading is the established rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain rehabilitation: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching. Strengthening within 24h: (1) concentric, (2) add weight, (3) eccentric calf lowering. Low-impact cardio maintained throughout. Myofascial techniques when pain-free. Surgery only for Grade III complete rupture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: medial belly injury from eccentric overstretch; dorsiflexion reproduces pain; Grade III has palpable defect and positive Thompson test","whyNot":{"B":"Full equinus casting is reserv","C":"Corticosteroid injection and s","D":"Corticosteroid injection and s"}},{"id":"sdl7_q10","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of clinical findings is most consistent with Grade 2 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Normal medial longitudinal arch with pain and swelling of the tendon; patient is able to complete a single-leg heel raise without significant difficulty or pain on examination","B":"Fixed subtalar joint deformity with rigid pes planus that cannot be passively corrected to neutral; requires orthopedic referral for surgical bony reconstruction procedure","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial tendon swelling with preserved normal arch height; patient completes heel raise without weakness or pain on repeat standardized physical exam","D":"Inability to perform a single-leg heel raise with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction deformity that is passively correctable to neutral subtalar and hindfoot alignment","E":"Complete tendon rupture confirmed on MRI with total collapse of medial arch requiring urgent orthopedic consultation for posterior tibial tendon surgical reconstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Grade 2 PTTD is defined by the inability to perform a single-leg heel raise combined with flexible pes planus and forefoot abduction that is passively correctable to neutral hindfoot alignment. Grade 1 has tendon pain and swelling but preserved heel raise ability. Grade 3 has a fixed subtalar deformity that cannot be passively corrected to neutral. Grade 4 involves valgus tibiotalar tilt within the mortise, requiring surgical reconstruction.","boardPrep":"PTTD grading: Grade 1 — pain/swelling, able to single-leg heel raise, no deformity, conservative care. Grade 2 — CANNOT single-leg heel raise, flexible pes planus + forefoot abduction, same conservative treatment as Grade 1. Grade 3 — Grade 2 + fixed subtalar joint → ortho referral for reconstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint","whyNot":{"A":"Grade 1 has tendon pain and sw","C":"Grade 1 has tendon pain and sw"}},{"id":"sdl7_q11","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test is the hallmark clinical assessment for posterior tibial tendon dysfunction across all grades?","choices":{"A":"Weakness or inability to perform a single-leg heel raise on the affected side, indicating insufficient posterior tibial tendon function to supinate the hindfoot during rise","B":"Positive anterior drawer sign at the ankle indicating concurrent anterior talofibular ligament laxity contributing to chronic lateral ankle instability in the affected patient","C":"Tenderness over the lateral calcaneus worsening with inversion stress testing, indicating peroneal tendon subluxation or coexisting lateral ankle ligament pathology","D":"Decreased passive subtalar inversion range of motion below 10 degrees indicating early degenerative arthritic changes in the subtalar joint complex on examination","E":"Medial ankle ecchymosis with a palpable gap at the posterior tibial musculotendinous junction indicating acute complete rupture of the posterior tibial tendon recently"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The single-leg heel raise is the hallmark clinical test for PTTD across all grades, directly assessing the posterior tibial tendon ability to supinate the hindfoot and maintain the medial arch during heel rise. The anterior drawer tests the ATFL, not the posterior tibial tendon. Subtalar inversion ROM reflects joint mobility, not tendon function specifically. A palpable gap with medial ecchymosis would indicate acute complete tendon rupture, not the chronic progressive course of typical PTTD.","boardPrep":"PTTD hallmark: single-leg heel raise failure. The posterior tibial tendon inverts the hindfoot and maintains the medial arch during heel rise. Too-many-toes sign (hindfoot valgus, forefoot abduction viewed from behind) is classic for Grade 2+. Prevalence 3.3-10%; increases after age 40; usually unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint","whyNot":{"B":"The anterior drawer tests the ","D":"Subtalar inversion ROM reflect"}},{"id":"sdl7_q12","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which management strategy is most appropriate as the primary initial treatment for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Platelet-rich plasma injection directly into the posterior tibial tendon sheath under ultrasound guidance as the preferred first-line regenerative intervention for Grade 1","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the posterior tibial tendon sheath as the primary evidence-based first-line management approach for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction","C":"Nitroglycerin patch applied over the medial ankle as the first-line adjunctive treatment for all grades of posterior tibial tendinopathy based on strong evidence","D":"Extracorporeal shockwave therapy targeting the tendon insertion as the evidence-based preferred non-surgical option for Grade 1 posterior tibial tendon dysfunction management","E":"Activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics for pain control, and supervised physical therapy with eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grade 1 PTTD is managed conservatively with activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, and supervised physical therapy including eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening. PRP and corticosteroid injections are adjunctive options with limited evidence and are not first-line for Grade 1. Casting is reserved for more advanced cases. Nitroglycerin patches have limited and inconsistent evidence and are not the primary recommended treatment.","boardPrep":"PTTD management: Grade 1 and Grade 2 — activity modification, medial arch support orthotics, analgesics, physical therapy. Grade 3 — orthopedic referral for surgical reconstruction. Adjunctive options: corticosteroid injections (caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections, nitroglycerin patches (all limited evidence). Eccentric PT is cornerstone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — PTTD: hallmark is failed single-leg heel raise; Grade 1 normal arch, Grade 2 flexible pes planus, Grade 3 fixed subtalar joint","whyNot":{"A":"PRP and corticosteroid injecti"}},{"id":"sdl7_q13","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes insertional from mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy based on anatomic location?","choices":{"A":"Mid-portion tendinopathy involves the paratenon sheath exclusively and responds to eccentric loading programs regardless of the stage or chronicity of symptoms in the patient","B":"Insertional tendinopathy occurs exclusively in older sedentary patients and is entirely unrelated to activity level, sport participation, or mechanical loading forces","C":"Insertional tendinopathy affects the tendon-bone junction at the calcaneal enthesis, while mid-portion disease occurs in the hypovascular tendon body above the insertion","D":"Mid-portion tendinopathy causes calcaneal bone spurring at the insertion requiring surgical excision, while insertional disease has no associated bony calcaneal changes","E":"Insertional tendinopathy exclusively affects competitive athletes while mid-portion tendinopathy is limited to recreational runners with poor foot and ankle biomechanics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy occurs at the calcaneal enthesis where the tendon attaches to bone, while mid-portion tendinopathy occurs in the hypovascular tendon body proximal to the insertion — the most common site of tendinopathy. The mid-portion involves the tendon substance and paratenon, not the paratenon exclusively. Insertional tendinopathy is not limited to older sedentary patients, and bone spurring (Haglund deformity) is associated with the insertional type, not mid-portion disease.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy: insertional = at calcaneal enthesis (tendon-bone junction), associated with bone spur or Haglund deformity. Mid-portion = tendon body proximal to the insertion (hypovascular zone) (most common site). Both worsen with increased activity. Tendinopathy umbrella includes tendinitis, tendinosis, paratendinitis, enthesopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation","whyNot":{"A":"The mid-portion involves the t"}},{"id":"sdl7_q14","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of factors is confirmed as independently associated with increased risk of Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Female sex with estrogen-related collagen laxity, vegetarian diet with reduced collagen precursors, and hyperpronation of the subtalar joint as the primary risk factors","B":"High weekly mileage above 50 miles per week, running exclusively on hard concrete surfaces, and consuming a high-fat diet as the primary confirmed risk factors identified","C":"Reduced ankle dorsiflexion ROM below 10 degrees measured non-weight bearing as the single most important confirmed primary risk factor for Achilles tendinopathy in athletes","D":"Male sex, increasing age, prior history of tendinopathy, overweight status, and poor running mechanics with excessive supination, each confirmed as an independent risk factor","E":"Leg-length discrepancy above 2cm measured by scanogram combined with arch height index below 0.30 as the most strongly confirmed combination of risk factors for tendinopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Confirmed risk factors for Achilles tendinopathy include male sex, increasing age, prior tendinopathy history, overweight status, and poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion). Female sex and estrogen-related laxity are not the primary confirmed risk factors — males have higher incidence. High mileage and hard surfaces are proposed risk factors but are less consistently confirmed than the biomechanical and demographic factors listed.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy risk factors: prior tendinopathy, poor running mechanics (excessive supination, inadequate dorsiflexion), worn footwear, weak plantarflexion, leg-length discrepancy, increased age, male sex, overweight. Incidence: competitive athletes 24%, recreational runners 6-17% of injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation","whyNot":{"B":"High mileage and hard surfaces"}},{"id":"sdl7_q15","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical therapy intervention is considered the first-line rehabilitation for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Concentric calf strengthening only in a completely pain-free range with no eccentric component permitted until 12 full weeks after symptom onset has been reached","B":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading protocol with progressive resistance increase, establishing controlled tendon loading to promote collagen remodeling and pain reduction","C":"Isokinetic strengthening using a dynamometer at 60 degrees per second as the preferred physical therapy approach validated for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy treatment","D":"Ultrasound-guided corticosteroid injection into the paratenon sheath followed by 6 weeks of complete relative rest and passive gentle Achilles tendon stretching exercises","E":"Static gastrosoleus stretching for 30 seconds three times daily combined with daily nitroglycerin patch application as the current evidence-based standard protocol for tendinopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line PT for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy, applying controlled tensile load that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction and promotes collagen remodeling. Concentric-only exercises lack the specific mechanical stimulus that drives matrix remodeling in tendinopathy. Corticosteroid injection carries significant tendon rupture risk and has uncertain long-term benefit. Static stretching and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive at best and are not first-line evidence-based treatments.","boardPrep":"Achilles tendinopathy PT: eccentric calf-muscle loading (Alfredson protocol) is first-line. Perform heel drop off a step: rise on two, lower on one. Gastrosoleus mobilization and activity modification are complementary. Corticosteroid injection: uncertain benefit, rupture risk. Percutaneous tenotomy and nitroglycerin patches are adjunctive options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional at calcaneal enthesis vs mid-portion 2-6cm proximal; eccentric calf loading is first-line PT rehabilitation","whyNot":{"A":"Concentric-only exercises lack","D":"Corticosteroid injection carri"}},{"id":"sdl7_q16","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomic description correctly identifies the location of the retrocalcaneal bursa?","choices":{"A":"The superficial subcutaneous bursa located posterior to the Achilles tendon between the skin and the tendon at the heel level, irritated by shoe counter pressure on activity","B":"The medial calcaneal bursa located at the plantar-medial calcaneal prominence adjacent to the plantar fascia attachment on the inferior calcaneal surface with weight bearing","C":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion","D":"The subtalar bursa located within the tarsal sinus between the talus and calcaneus adjacent to the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament on the lateral aspect of the foot","E":"The tibiotalar bursa located anteriorly between the tibial plafond and the talar dome within the ankle mortise, irritated by repetitive dorsiflexion impingement in athletes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The retrocalcaneal bursa is positioned between the posterior calcaneus and the deep anterior surface of the Achilles tendon, just proximal to the calcaneal insertion. The superficial calcaneal bursa lies between the Achilles and overlying skin and is irritated by shoe counter pressure. The medial calcaneal and subtalar bursae are distinct structures at different anatomic locations. The tibiotalar bursa is anterior within the ankle mortise and is not the retrocalcaneal bursa.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursa: between the calcaneus and deep surface of Achilles tendon. Distinct from superficial calcaneal bursa (between Achilles and skin). Often coexists with Achilles tendinosis and calcaneal enthesopathy. One of 3 most common bursitis locations. More common in adults; males = females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy","whyNot":{"B":"The medial calcaneal and subta","D":"The medial calcaneal and subta"}},{"id":"sdl7_q17","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding is most characteristic of retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Pain with resisted plantarflexion and maximum tenderness at the mid-portion of the Achilles tendon 4cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion on direct palpation of the tendon","B":"Posterior heel pain with tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon insertion, worsening with passive ankle dorsiflexion examination","C":"Inferomedial heel pain that is most severe with the first morning steps and improves significantly after walking for approximately 10 minutes of activity on the affected foot","D":"Lateral heel tenderness with swelling that worsens with ankle inversion stress testing and resolves completely when the ankle is held in passive dorsiflexion by the examiner","E":"Diffuse posterior ankle swelling with pain on forced plantarflexion at end range indicating posterior impingement between the calcaneus and the posterior tibial plafond"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis characteristically produces posterior calcaneal tenderness at the posterosuperior calcaneus just deep to the Achilles tendon, worsened by passive dorsiflexion which compresses the bursa. Mid-tendon tenderness 4cm proximal suggests mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy. Inferomedial heel pain worst with first morning steps is the classic presentation of plantar fasciitis, not bursitis. Lateral heel pain with inversion stress indicates lateral ankle ligament or peroneal tendon pathology.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: posterior calcaneal pain deep to Achilles, worse with dorsiflexion (bursa compressed). Often confused with plantar fasciitis (inferior heel, worse first steps). Highly associated with Achilles tendinosis. Treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching, corticosteroid injection, surgery rarely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy"},{"id":"sdl7_q18","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions is most appropriate as the initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis?","choices":{"A":"Cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain control, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure, and Achilles tendon stretching program","B":"Immediate corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa under ultrasound guidance followed by 2 weeks of strict non-weight bearing on crutches for the patient","C":"Surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as the primary treatment given the poor long-term outcomes reported with conservative management for retrocalcaneal bursitis","D":"Platelet-rich plasma injection combined with prolotherapy injections into the bursa and surrounding tendon as the initial regenerative treatment approach at first visit","E":"Oral corticosteroid taper for 10 days combined with iontophoresis applied directly over the posterior heel as the standard evidence-based initial management protocol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Initial conservative treatment for retrocalcaneal bursitis includes cold compresses to reduce swelling, analgesics for pain, footwear modification to reduce heel counter pressure on the bursa, and Achilles tendon stretching. Immediate corticosteroid injection is not first-line — it is reserved for cases refractory to conservative care. Surgery is rarely needed. PRP and prolotherapy have insufficient evidence to serve as initial treatment. Oral corticosteroid taper is not standard management for localized bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis treatment: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification (reduce heel counter pressure), Achilles stretching. If refractory: corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not into Achilles tendon). Surgery rarely indicated. Distinguish from superficial calcaneal bursitis (between skin and Achilles — caused by shoe rubbing).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: bursa between calcaneus and Achilles tendon; worse with dorsiflexion; highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy","whyNot":{"B":"Immediate corticosteroid injec","C":"Surgery is rarely needed.","D":"PRP and prolotherapy have insu"}},{"id":"sdl7_q19","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease affects females at age 8 years and males at age 6 years, with unilateral involvement accounting for approximately 90% of all affected pediatric patients","B":"The incidence is 10 per 1000 pediatric patients, and unilateral presentation occurs in approximately 90% of all cases presenting in skeletally immature young athletes","C":"Sever disease predominantly affects adolescents over age 14 years and resolves spontaneously within 6 weeks without any specific treatment or activity restriction","D":"Males and females are equally affected with a peak onset between ages 12 and 14 years and predominantly unilateral involvement in the dominant extremity in most cases","E":"Males develop at a peak age of 8 years, females at 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately two-thirds of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease peaks in males at age 8 years and females at age 6 years, with bilateral involvement in approximately 2/3 of cases and an incidence of 3.7 per 1000 patients. Females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation, not later. The incidence is not 10/1000. Sever disease does NOT predominantly affect adolescents over 14 — it resolves as the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity, which occurs by the early teenage years.","boardPrep":"Sever disease: males peak at 8yr, females peak at 6yr (females develop earlier due to earlier skeletal maturation). Males > females. Bilateral in 2/3. Incidence 3.7/1000. Duration: 3-4 years until skeletal maturity. Posteroinferior calcaneus growth plate at Achilles insertion is the site of injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years","whyNot":{"A":"Females develop earlier due to","B":"The incidence is not 10/1000."}},{"id":"sdl7_q20","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pathophysiologic mechanism is responsible for calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)?","choices":{"A":"Avascular necrosis of the calcaneal epiphysis from disruption of the nutrient artery blood supply to the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate cartilage during athletic activity","B":"Repetitive traction stress from the Achilles tendon on the calcaneal apophysis growth plate during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes during development","C":"Acute complete fracture through the calcaneal apophyseal growth plate from a single episode of forceful ground impact during competitive athletic activity in youth athletes","D":"Inflammatory bursitis of the retrocalcaneal bursa causing secondary irritation of the adjacent calcaneal growth plate cartilage in young skeletally immature pediatric athletes","E":"Repetitive compressive stress fracture of the calcaneal body from excessive impact loading during running causing subchondral bone insufficiency injury in growing athletes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sever disease is caused by repetitive traction from the Achilles tendon on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate (apophysis) during running and jumping in skeletally immature athletes. It is NOT avascular necrosis — that term applies to Legg-Calve-Perthes disease of the femoral head. It is not an acute fracture through the apophysis, nor isolated bursitis. The diagnosis is traction apophysitis, mechanically analogous to Osgood-Schlatter disease at the tibial tubercle but occurring at the calcaneal apophysis.","boardPrep":"Sever disease pathophysiology: calcaneal apophysis is the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate at the Achilles insertion. Repetitive Achilles traction on the immature apophysis during running and jumping → apophysitis (traction injury, NOT avascular necrosis — that is Legg-Calve-Perthes). Clinical diagnosis; X-ray not required but may show fragmentation of apophysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years","whyNot":{"C":"It is not an acute fracture th","D":"It is not an acute fracture th"}},{"id":"sdl7_q21","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of interventions represents appropriate initial management of Sever disease in a skeletally immature athlete?","choices":{"A":"Activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain control, and footwear modification with adequate heel support","B":"Immediate casting in equinus position for 6 weeks followed by a gradual return to sport over 3 months under supervised physical therapist rehabilitation program","C":"Surgical removal of the apophyseal ossification center when conservative measures fail to improve symptoms within 2 weeks of initial conservative treatment initiation","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the calcaneal apophysis under ultrasound guidance combined with complete cessation of all athletic activity for 3 months minimum","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the apophyseal growth plate combined with extracorporeal shockwave therapy as the preferred first-line treatment for all cases"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sever disease management begins with activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles tendon stretching, NSAIDs for acute pain, and footwear modification. Surgery is NEVER indicated for Sever disease, which is self-limited. Casting in equinus is not the initial approach. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at a pediatric growth plate. PRP is not an appropriate first-line therapy in pediatric growth plate conditions. The goal is symptom control with maintenance of activity as tolerated.","boardPrep":"Sever disease treatment: activity reduction (not complete cessation), heel cushioning inserts, Achilles stretching, cold compresses, NSAIDs, PT. Recalcitrant cases: short-term immobilization. Self-limited 3-4 years. Surgery NOT indicated. Corticosteroid injection NOT appropriate in pediatric growth plate conditions. Return to sport as symptoms allow with activity modification.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — Sever disease: calcaneal apophysitis from Achilles traction on growth plate; males at 8yr, females at 6yr; bilateral in 2/3; self-limited 3-4 years","whyNot":{"B":"Casting in equinus is not the ","C":"Surgery is NEVER indicated for","D":"Corticosteroid injection is co","E":"PRP is not an appropriate firs"}},{"id":"sdl7_q22","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which clinical presentation is most specific for common peroneal nerve injury at the level of the fibular head?","choices":{"A":"Burning and shooting pain radiating from the lateral knee to the dorsum of the foot and toe web spaces, with possible ankle dorsiflexion weakness or foot drop","B":"Decreased sensation over the dorsolateral foot and sinus tarsi with tenderness to percussion at the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus without any weakness","C":"Burning superficial pain with decreased sensation at the anterolateral shin and a palpable fascial defect at the crural fascia just lateral to the fibula distally","D":"Medial knee pain with numbness extending to the medial calf without any foot weakness, from saphenous nerve compression at the medial knee fascial level on examination","E":"Burning pain along the medial calf with numbness at the medial knee and no identifiable motor deficits, consistent with saphenous nerve entrapment at the fascia level"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head produces burning and shooting pain from the lateral knee to the dorsal foot and web spaces, with possible foot drop from motor involvement (ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension weakness). Saphenous nerve entrapment causes medial knee and calf pain without any foot weakness. Superficial peroneal entrapment at the crural fascia produces only sensory symptoms (dorsolateral foot numbness) without motor deficit. Deep peroneal compression causes first web space numbness only.","boardPrep":"Common peroneal nerve: wraps around fibular neck at the fibular head. Injury from: cycling (hip flexion pressure), leg crossing, braces, casts, direct trauma. Motor: foot drop (ankle dorsiflexion, toe extension). Sensory: dorsum of foot, first through fourth web spaces, lateral lower leg. Tinel at fibular head. Most common cause of foot drop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)","whyNot":{"C":"Superficial peroneal entrapmen","E":"Saphenous nerve entrapment cau"}},{"id":"sdl7_q23","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Where is the superficial peroneal nerve most commonly entrapped, and which symptom pattern does it produce?","choices":{"A":"At the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus where the nerve exits the fascia, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot","B":"At the fibular head where the nerve wraps around the lateral fibular neck, producing foot drop and toe web space numbness from direct pressure at the lateral knee level","C":"At the medial knee where the nerve passes through the fascia between the sartorius and gracilis tendons, producing medial lower leg paresthesias and medial knee pain","D":"At the tarsal tunnel posterior to the medial malleolus beneath the flexor retinaculum, producing plantar foot burning, numbness, and arch pain with prolonged standing","E":"At the anterior ankle beneath the extensor retinaculum, producing first web space numbness and pain with active dorsiflexion testing of the anterior compartment musculature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superficial peroneal nerve exits the crural fascia proximal to the lateral malleolus and is most commonly entrapped at this fascial exit point, producing burning pain and decreased sensation at the sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. It does NOT wrap around the fibular head — that is the common peroneal nerve. Superficial peroneal entrapment is PURELY SENSORY distally, distinguishing it from common peroneal injury which causes foot drop. Tarsal tunnel involves the tibial nerve posterior to the medial malleolus.","boardPrep":"Superficial peroneal nerve: entrapment at crural fascia proximal to lateral malleolus. Sports: bodybuilding, horse racing, running, soccer, tennis. Symptoms: burning superficial pain, decreased sensation at sinus tarsi and dorsolateral foot. Signs: Tinel at fascia, palpable fascial defect. Purely sensory distally — no foot drop (distinguishes from common peroneal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)"},{"id":"sdl7_q24","sdlNumber":7,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which diagnostic study is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis and localize the level and severity of lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the lower extremity with dedicated nerve protocol to evaluate for intraneural ganglion cysts or perineural fibrosis mechanically compressing the nerve trunk along its course","B":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to localize the level of nerve dysfunction and quantify whether the injury is primarily axonal or demyelinating in nature","C":"Ultrasound-guided injection of local anesthetic adjacent to the suspected nerve as the gold standard confirmatory test for definitively confirming peripheral nerve entrapment","D":"CT myelogram of the lumbar spine to exclude a radicular origin at the L4-S1 nerve roots as the source of lower extremity sensory and motor symptoms and changes","E":"Plain radiograph of the fibula to identify bony cortical abnormality at the fibular head contributing to common peroneal nerve compression and foot drop in the patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nerve conduction studies and electromyography (NCS/EMG) are the definitive tests for peripheral nerve entrapment, localizing the level of dysfunction and characterizing whether injury is primarily axonal (amplitude reduction) or demyelinating (conduction velocity slowing). MRI with nerve protocol detects structural causes but does not provide electrophysiological characterization. Ultrasound-guided local anesthetic injection is diagnostic-therapeutic but not the gold standard for nerve injury characterization. CT myelogram evaluates the spinal canal for radicular pathology.","boardPrep":"Lower extremity nerve injuries: confirm with NCS and EMG to localize level and characterize injury (axonal vs demyelinating). Saphenous nerve: purely sensory — pain/numbness medial knee and calf, NO motor deficits. Common peroneal: motor (foot drop) + sensory. Superficial peroneal: mainly sensory (dorsolateral foot). Conservative: gait analysis, neuromodulators, TENS, PT, anti-inflammatories. Invasive: steroid injection, hydrodissection, surgical release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal (fibular head, foot drop), superficial peroneal (crural fascia, dorsolateral foot), saphenous (medial knee, no motor deficit)","whyNot":{"A":"MRI with nerve protocol detect","D":"CT myelogram evaluates the spi"}},{"id":"sdl7_q25","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female cross-country runner has 3 weeks of bilateral lower leg pain. Pain is diffuse along the posteromedial tibia, worsens with activity, and is present the following morning. She increased mileage from 30 to 50 miles/week over 2 weeks. Exam shows diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness without focal point tenderness. X-rays are negative. Which diagnosis is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral tibial stress fractures from acute mileage increase, requiring immediate non-weight bearing and orthopedic referral for management with focal point tenderness expected","B":"Bilateral exertional compartment syndrome causing elevated posterior compartment pressures with exercise, requiring compartment pressure measurement at rest and with exertion","C":"Bilateral posterior tibial vein deep venous thrombosis presenting as bilateral lower extremity pain in a young female athlete with increased training demands and activity level","D":"Bilateral periosteal hematomas from direct contact injuries during team cross-country training sessions causing localized posteromedial tibial tenderness and soft tissue swelling","E":"Medial tibial stress syndrome from repetitive posterior compartment traction periostitis triggered by rapid mileage increase and likely subtalar overpronation mechanics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This clinical picture — diffuse posteromedial tibial pain bilaterally, worsening with activity and present the following morning, triggered by rapid mileage increase in a young female runner with negative X-rays — is classic MTSS. Bilateral tibial stress fractures would show focal point tenderness and require imaging confirmation. Exertional compartment syndrome symptoms occur during exercise and require compartment pressure measurement at rest and post-exercise. DVT and periosteal hematoma from contact do not fit this activity-related pattern.","boardPrep":"MTSS hallmark: diffuse (NOT focal) posteromedial tibial pain at junction of proximal 2/3 to distal 1/3. Pain decreases with warm-up, athlete can complete session, recurs after exercise and the following morning. Key differentiator from stress fracture: diffuse vs focal tenderness. Bone scan differentiates: MTSS = patchy diffuse vs stress fracture = focal hot spot.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hip overuse injuries: repetitive mechanical loading exceeding tissue adaptation capacity is the shared pathophysiologic basis of all overuse syndromes including MTSS; apply the 10% rule to prevent all overuse injuries","whyNot":{"C":"DVT and periosteal hematoma fr","D":"DVT and periosteal hematoma fr"}},{"id":"sdl7_q26","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old volleyball player has a grade 2 lateral ankle sprain. After PRICE therapy and a few days on crutches, her physician fits an ankle stirrup brace rather than a cast and starts early motion exercises. Which statement best explains why selective motion in a stirrup brace is preferred over rigid casting for healing the torn ligament?","choices":{"A":"The brace allows flexion and extension but blocks inversion and eversion, and ligaments healed under this motion lay down collagen parallel to their long axis","B":"The brace blocks flexion and extension but allows inversion and eversion, so the torn lateral ligaments are moved through their normal arc and stretched back to full length","C":"The brace locks the ankle in plantarflexion, which shortens the anterior talofibular ligament and brings the torn ends together faster than a neutral cast can achieve","D":"The brace compresses the joint capsule, which raises intra-articular pressure and drives synovial fluid into the ligament to nourish its avascular midsubstance","E":"The brace is cheaper and easier to remove for bathing, but cast immobilization produces stronger healed tissue and remains the preferred option when cost is not a concern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading for this SDL explains that the stirrup brace allows ankle flexion and extension, which prevents stiffness, while blocking inversion and eversion, which would disturb healing of the torn lateral and medial ligaments. In the animal work it cites, ligaments allowed flexion-extension motion healed with collagen fibers parallel to the long axis of the ligament and were stronger and less likely to rerupture, whereas cast-immobilized ligaments laid down randomly oriented collagen. A brace that blocks flexion-extension and allows inversion-eversion reverses the design and would stress the injured ligaments. Locking the ankle in plantarflexion places the ATFL in its most vulnerable position, the position in which it tears. Raising intra-articular pressure to nourish the ligament is not the mechanism described. Casting is reserved for patients with severe pain and does not produce stronger healed tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Healing ligament under controlled tensile load → collagen aligns along the line of stress; rigid immobilization → disorganized collagen. Lateral sprain: grade 2 = brief non-weight bearing, brief immobilization, rehabilitation. COMLEX: Early proprioceptive and balance exercises reduce recurrent sprains.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — ligament composition as dense regular collagen aligned with tensile load","whyNot":{"B":"A brace that blocks flexion-ex","C":"Locking the ankle in plantarfl"}},{"id":"sdl7_q27","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial tibial stress syndrome (MTSS).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old female marathon runner recovers from MTSS after activity modification. Bilateral subtalar overpronation is confirmed on exam. She asks which modification most urgently reduces recurrence risk given the evidence base for MTSS risk factors. Which counseling point is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Transition entirely to barefoot running to eliminate heel strike and normalize posterior compartment loading mechanics during all future training and competition activities","B":"Increase training cadence above 180 steps per minute as the single most effective evidence-based intervention for reducing the risk of MTSS recurrence in distance runners","C":"Obtain custom orthotics proven by a randomized controlled trial to reduce MTSS recurrence by 60% in female runners with confirmed subtalar overpronation on physical exam","D":"Address subtalar overpronation with appropriate motion-control footwear or custom orthotics, as overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS","E":"Permanently reduce weekly mileage by 50% below the level at which symptoms developed, as mileage reduction is the only proven strategy for preventing future MTSS episodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Overpronation is the most consistently confirmed modifiable risk factor for MTSS, so addressing it with motion-control footwear or custom orthotics is the best-supported prevention counseling given this runner's confirmed subtalar overpronation. Barefoot running is not evidence-based for MTSS prevention and may worsen the condition. Increased cadence has theoretical benefit but is not confirmed as the most effective intervention. Permanent 50% mileage reduction is unsupported — the 10% weekly increase rule is the appropriate training modification.","boardPrep":"MTSS prevention: address overpronation (strongest confirmed evidence). Apply 10% rule for mileage increase. Footwear modification for gait abnormalities. Cross training maintains fitness during recovery. Return to play: 2 weeks to 4 months with 10% weekly mileage rule. Surgery (superficial posterior compartment release): 70% success rate for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — MTSS: overpronation is the strongest confirmed modifiable risk factor; training errors such as rapid mileage increase and changes in terrain are probable contributors with less consistent evidence than biomechanical factors","whyNot":{"A":"Barefoot running is not eviden","B":"Increased cadence has theoreti"}},{"id":"sdl7_q28","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male basketball player rolls his right ankle during practice. He has lateral ankle pain, ecchymosis, and is able to bear weight with pain. Anterior drawer test is mildly positive; talar tilt is negative. Ottawa ankle rules are negative. Which sprain grade and initial management plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain with incomplete ATFL tear; treat with PRICE therapy, brief non-weight bearing, short immobilization period, and early progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 1 lateral ankle sprain with microtears only; treat with PRICE and immediate full activity return without any immobilization or bracing because no instability is present","C":"Grade 3 lateral ankle sprain with complete ligament rupture; treat with surgery and 6 weeks of non-weight bearing in a full cast before initiating any rehabilitation program","D":"Syndesmotic ankle sprain requiring orthopedic referral for syndesmotic fixation and 8 weeks of non-weight bearing based on mechanism regardless of examination findings","E":"Medial ankle sprain of the deltoid complex requiring MRI confirmation and non-weight bearing before initiating any weight-bearing activity or rehabilitation for this patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A mildly positive anterior drawer (ATFL partially torn) with negative talar tilt (CFL intact), ecchymosis, and ability to bear weight with pain best fits Grade 2 lateral ankle sprain. Grade 1 has no instability on anterior drawer. Grade 3 has complete ligament rupture with strongly positive stress testing and often inability to bear weight. Ottawa rules were negative, excluding clinically significant fracture. Syndesmotic or medial sprains do not match the mechanism or this exam pattern.","boardPrep":"Ankle sprain grading: Grade 1 — stretching/microtears, minimal pain, no instability, no immobilization, early rehab. Grade 2 — incomplete tear, moderate swelling/ecchymosis, mild anterior drawer, brief NWB + immobilization, rehab. Grade 3 — complete tear, marked swelling in 1-2h, cannot bear weight, positive stress test, immobilization + rehab.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 7 — Ankle joint dislocations: severe ankle ligament disruption with dislocation requires immediate orthopedic evaluation; ankle sprains without dislocation are graded 1-3 based on ligament integrity and functional instability","whyNot":{"B":"Grade 1 has no instability on ","D":"Syndesmotic or medial sprains ","E":"Syndesmotic or medial sprains "}},{"id":"sdl7_q29","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female soccer player sustains an ankle injury when her foot is planted and her leg externally rotates while the ankle dorsiflexes. Pain is above the ankle at the distal tibiofibular junction. The squeeze test is positive. No lateral ankle ligament tenderness is present. Which structure is most likely injured?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament and calcaneofibular ligament complex from classic inversion with plantarflexion mechanism causing the most common lateral ankle sprain pattern","B":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis, injured by dorsiflexion and external rotation mechanism producing a high ankle sprain above the ankle mortise","C":"Deltoid ligament complex from eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism causing medial ankle pain, often associated with avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus in athletes","D":"Posterior talofibular ligament from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior ankle pain and positive posterior drawer sign on physical examination testing","E":"Calcaneofibular ligament in isolation from pure inversion mechanism without plantarflexion, producing lateral ankle pain and a positive talar tilt test on examination"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dorsiflexion combined with external rotation of the leg over a planted foot, producing pain at the distal tibiofibular junction with a positive squeeze test but no lateral ligament tenderness, is pathognomonic of a syndesmotic (high) ankle sprain of the anterior tibiofibular ligament. Lateral ligament injuries (ATFL/CFL) occur with inversion-plantarflexion. Deltoid injury occurs with eversion. PTFL injury from severe inversion causes posterior ankle pain, not proximal tibiofibular pain. Always palpate the proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve.","boardPrep":"Syndesmotic high ankle sprain: mechanism = dorsiflexion + external rotation. Structures: anterior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL), posterior tibiofibular ligament (PITFL), interosseous membrane. Tests: squeeze test (compress fibula at mid-calf, pain at distal tibiofibular joint), external rotation test. Harder to diagnose, longer recovery than lateral sprain. Always palpate proximal fibula to exclude Maisonneuve fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: Maisonneuve fracture = syndesmotic disruption + proximal fibula fracture from external rotation; always palpate proximal fibula when syndesmotic sprain is suspected on examination after ankle injury","whyNot":{"A":"Lateral ligament injuries (ATF","C":"Deltoid injury occurs with eve","E":"Lateral ligament injuries (ATF"}},{"id":"sdl7_q30","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ankle sprain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female gymnast has medial ankle pain after landing from a dismount with her foot in eversion. X-ray shows an avulsion fracture of the medial malleolus. Which ligament injury is most likely responsible for the avulsion fracture mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Anterior talofibular ligament tear from inversion mechanism causing lateral ligament disruption and lateral ankle instability without medial ankle or malleolus involvement","B":"Calcaneofibular ligament rupture from combined inversion and plantarflexion during the landing, producing lateral ankle swelling and a positive talar tilt test on examination","C":"Anterior tibiofibular ligament of the syndesmosis torn from dorsiflexion and external rotation producing proximal pain above the ankle mortise after the gymnastics dismount","D":"Deltoid ligament complex injury from an eversion and dorsiflexion mechanism, with traction from the deltoid ligament avulsing the tip of the medial malleolus at its attachment","E":"Posterior talofibular ligament avulsion from severe inversion at terminal plantarflexion causing posterior lateral ankle pain and chronic instability after the gymnastics landing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eversion combined with dorsiflexion stresses the deltoid ligament complex, which can avulse the medial malleolus at its bony attachment. The deltoid is the medial stabilizer of the ankle and resists eversion forces. ATFL injuries from inversion and CFL injuries from inversion-plantarflexion do not produce medial malleolus avulsion. Syndesmotic injury from external rotation produces proximal pain at the tibiofibular junction, not medial malleolus avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Medial ankle sprain: mechanism = eversion and dorsiflexion. Deltoid complex injury: often with multiligamentous injury or medial malleolus avulsion fracture. Medial sprain incidence: 0.06/1000 athlete exposures (much rarer than lateral 0.93/1000). Treatment: PRICE, immobilization, non-weight bearing, orthopedic consult. Ottawa: medial malleolus posterior tenderness = X-ray required.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 8 — Ankle fractures: medial malleolus fracture from deltoid traction is a component of bimalleolar or trimalleolar fracture patterns in ankle injuries with eversion mechanism; Ottawa rules mandate imaging when medial malleolus tenderness is present","whyNot":{"A":"ATFL injuries from inversion a","B":"ATFL injuries from inversion a"}},{"id":"sdl7_q31","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male tennis player feels a sudden pop in his posterior left calf while lunging for a ball. He reports feeling \"hit in the leg.\" Tenderness is at the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous junction. Dorsiflexion reproduces pain. No palpable defect is found. Thompson test is negative. He could not continue playing. Active plantarflexion against resistance is painful, and passive dorsiflexion is reduced compared with the other side. Which grade and expected return-to-sport timeline is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Grade 2 gastrocnemius strain with incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion ROM, and return to sport in 16 to 21 days with progressive rehabilitation","B":"Grade 3 gastrocnemius strain with complete rupture confirmed by Thompson positivity and palpable defect, requiring surgical repair with return 6 months after operative intervention","C":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain with microtears causing mild calf pain on unilateral calf raise only, with expected return to sport in 10 to 12 days with conservative management","D":"Achilles tendon rupture with positive Thompson sign and palpable gap at the tendon, requiring urgent orthopedic evaluation for either surgical repair or conservative casting","E":"Deep venous thrombosis of the posterior tibial vein mimicking gastrocnemius strain, requiring Doppler ultrasound and therapeutic anticoagulation rather than sport rehabilitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Negative Thompson test and absent palpable defect with active plantarflexion pain and loss of dorsiflexion ROM confirm Grade 2 (incomplete tear), with expected return to sport in 16-21 days. Grade 3 requires a positive Thompson test and a palpable defect at the MTJ. Grade 1 produces only pain with single-leg calf raise with no loss of dorsiflexion. Achilles tendon rupture has a gap at the tendon proper (not the MTJ) with a positive Thompson test. DVT should be excluded but has a distinct clinical course.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain grade 2 vs 3: Grade 2 = incomplete tear, active plantarflexion pain, loss of dorsiflexion, Thompson negative, no palpable defect, RTS 16-21 days. Grade 3 = complete rupture, Thompson positive, palpable defect at MTJ, surgery, RTS 6 months. Achilles rupture: gap at tendon itself (not MTJ), Thompson positive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Thompson test distinguishes Achilles rupture (positive = no plantarflexion squeeze response) from gastrocnemius strain (Thompson negative); palpable defect location at the MTJ (gastrocnemius) vs tendon itself (Achilles) is the key anatomic distinguishing feature","whyNot":{"B":"Grade 3 requires a positive Th","E":"DVT should be excluded but has"}},{"id":"sdl7_q32","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female masters runner has a sudden tearing sensation in her right calf while accelerating from a stop. Ultrasound shows a 2cm partial tear of the medial gastrocnemius head at the musculotendinous junction. She has pain with weight bearing and reduced dorsiflexion. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical exploration and primary repair of the medial gastrocnemius given the 2cm partial tear size confirmed on musculoskeletal ultrasound imaging assessment","B":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the partial tear under ultrasound guidance as the current standard first-line treatment based on strong randomized controlled trial evidence","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the tear site under ultrasound guidance to reduce acute inflammation and facilitate earlier return to running activity in this masters athlete","D":"Equinus casting for 4 weeks in full plantarflexion to protect the healing musculotendinous junction from mechanical stretch forces during the acute tissue repair phase","E":"Ice, crutches for weight bearing assistance, heel lift to offload the calf, and progressive rehabilitation beginning with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening program"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grade 2 (incomplete) gastrocnemius strain from a 2cm partial tear on ultrasound is managed conservatively with ice, crutches for weight-bearing assistance, heel lift, and progressive rehabilitation starting with concentric then eccentric calf strengthening. Surgery is not indicated for partial tears — only Grade III complete rupture warrants surgical exploration. Corticosteroid injection is contraindicated at an acute muscle tear site. Equinus casting is reserved for post-surgical Grade III repair management.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain Grade II conservative management: ice, TENS, crutches if needed, heel lift, gentle stretching, strengthening within 24h (concentric → add weight → eccentric calf lowering), low-impact cardio, myofascial techniques when pain-free, biomechanical correction. MSK ultrasound helpful for grading >Grade 1. Surgery reserved for Grade III only.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric strengthening is a core modality for tendon and muscle injuries; progressive loading from concentric to eccentric is the standard rehabilitation sequence for calf injuries after gastrocnemius strain","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery is not indicated for p","D":"Equinus casting is reserved fo"}},{"id":"sdl7_q33","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of gastrocnemius strain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old male soccer player has a Grade I gastrocnemius strain with pain on unilateral calf raise. He asks when he can return to full competitive soccer. Which return-to-sport timeline is most appropriate for Grade I gastrocnemius strain?","choices":{"A":"Return in 16 to 21 days following rehabilitation with progressive concentric and eccentric calf strengthening, as appropriate for Grade II gastrocnemius strain management","B":"Return in 6 months following surgical repair and sport-specific rehabilitation as appropriate for Grade III complete gastrocnemius rupture with complete defect at the MTJ","C":"Return in 10 to 12 days following ice, relative rest, heel lift, and progressive strengthening once pain on unilateral calf raise has fully resolved with exercise","D":"Return in 4 to 6 weeks contingent upon achieving a pain-free single-leg calf raise and full ankle dorsiflexion range of motion compared to the uninjured contralateral side","E":"Return in 3 to 5 days following acute swelling resolution because Grade I strains involve only microtears and do not require any significant time away from competitive sport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain (microtears only, pain on single-leg calf raise) has an expected return-to-sport of 10-12 days once the athlete can perform a pain-free calf raise. The 16-21 day timeline applies to Grade 2. The 6-month timeline applies to Grade 3 post-surgery. A 4-6 week timeline is more consistent with Grade 2 criteria. A 3-5 day timeline is insufficient even for a Grade 1 strain and risks reinjury.","boardPrep":"Gastrocnemius strain return-to-sport timelines: Grade I = 10-12 days (pain-free calf raise), Grade II = 16-21 days (pain-free active plantarflexion, full dorsiflexion), Grade III = 6 months post-surgery. Always compare to contralateral side. Sport-specific activity progression before full return to competitive play.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Gastrocnemius strain: Grade I returns in 10-12 days, Grade II in 16-21 days, Grade III in 6 months post-surgery; these timelines assume progressive pain-free rehabilitation with restoration of strength and ROM before sport clearance","whyNot":{"A":"The 16-21 day timeline applies","B":"The 6-month timeline applies t","D":"A 4-6 week timeline is more co","E":"A 3-5 day timeline is insuffic"}},{"id":"sdl7_q34","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has had medial ankle pain for 2 years and a collapsed arch that now feels stiff. Standing, her heel sits in valgus and she cannot rise onto the ball of that foot. When the examiner tries to swing the hindfoot back to neutral, it will not move because the subtalar joint is rigid. How should her posterior tibial tendon dysfunction be graded and managed?","choices":{"A":"Grade 1, because the tendon is still painful and swollen; treat with activity modification, analgesics, arch support, and a supervised physical therapy program","B":"Grade 2, because the heel raise fails with pes planus and forefoot abduction; treat with the same arch support, analgesics, and therapy used for grade 1 disease","C":"Grade 2, because a rigid subtalar joint is expected once the arch collapses; treat with a molded arch support so the foot conforms and the arch is restored","D":"Grade 3, because grade 2 findings now come with a fixed subtalar joint; refer to an orthopedic foot and ankle surgeon, since the rigid deformity needs surgery","E":"Grade 3, because she is older than 60 with symptoms beyond 1 year; treat with a corticosteroid injection into the tendon sheath and a period of casting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A rigid subtalar joint is the feature that defines the most advanced grade in the course scheme: grade 3 is grade 2 disease plus a fixed subtalar joint, and it is referred to an orthopedic foot and ankle surgeon. The office orthopedics reading adds that an untreated collapsed arch becomes rigid and cannot be corrected without surgery. Grade 1 is a painful, swollen tendon with a preserved heel raise and no deformity. Grade 2 adds a failed heel raise with flatfoot and forefoot abduction but remains flexible, and it is treated like grade 1 with activity modification, analgesics, arch support, and physical therapy. An arch support works while the foot can still conform to it. Grading does not depend on age or symptom duration, and corticosteroid injection is an adjunct, not the plan for a fixed deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) inverts the foot and supports the medial arch; failure → adult-acquired flatfoot. PTTD grade 3 = fixed subtalar joint → surgical referral. COMLEX: Flexible deformity → orthotic/UCBL brace + inversion strengthening; rigid → surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — orthotic and footwear prescription for foot alignment problems"},{"id":"sdl7_q35","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has several months of atraumatic medial arch pain on the right. He is examined standing in shorts with shoes and socks removed, and the examiner looks at both heels from behind. Which finding seen from this position most supports posterior tibial tendon dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"The right heel tilts medially and the forefoot adducts, so fewer lateral toes are visible on the right than on the left when the feet are viewed from behind","B":"Both heels sit vertically with a high medial arch, so the toes are hidden behind the heels, the expected appearance of a tibialis posterior tendon tear","C":"The right heel tilts laterally and the foot rotates externally, so more lateral toes are visible on the right than on the left, the too many toes sign","D":"A firm swelling at the posterosuperior right calcaneus lateral to the Achilles tendon becomes red where the heel counter of the shoe rubs it","E":"The right calf shows a palpable gap at the medial gastrocnemius musculotendinous junction, and squeezing the calf fails to plantarflex the foot"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading says that in a patient over 50 with atraumatic medial arch pain, PTTD is the most likely diagnosis. Viewed from behind while standing, the collapsed arch lets the heel tilt laterally and the foot rotate externally, so more lateral toes are visible on the affected side, the too many toes sign. The reading pairs this with the single-leg heel rise, which is nearly impossible with PTTD. A medially tilted heel with forefoot adduction and a high arch describes a cavovarus foot, the opposite pattern. A painful bump at the posterosuperior calcaneus rubbed by the heel counter is a posterior heel problem related to retrocalcaneal irritation, not PTTD. A gap at the musculotendinous junction with a failed calf squeeze describes a grade III gastrocnemius rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTTD = tendon swells and cannot glide → arch collapses, heel goes into valgus, forefoot abducts → \"too many toes\" sign + failed single-leg heel rise. COMLEX: Examine standing from behind with shoes off; risk rises after age 40 and disease is usually unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastrocnemius strain grading with the Thompson test"},{"id":"sdl7_q36","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old female with posterior tibial tendinitis asks about nitroglycerin patches after seeing them mentioned as a treatment option online. Which statement best reflects current evidence for nitroglycerin patches in tendinopathy management?","choices":{"A":"Nitroglycerin patches are proven in multiple high-quality randomized controlled trials to significantly accelerate tendon collagen synthesis and reduce pain scores reliably","B":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive therapy option for tendinopathy management but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent overall","C":"Nitroglycerin patches are strictly contraindicated in tendinopathy because nitric oxide increases local vasodilation and paradoxically worsens inflammatory pain over time","D":"Nitroglycerin patches are the recommended first-line adjunctive therapy in all major orthopedic and sports medicine tendinopathy clinical practice guidelines worldwide","E":"Nitroglycerin patches are reserved exclusively for post-surgical tendon repair failure and should not be used for any conservative tendinopathy management in any patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitroglycerin patches have been explored as an adjunctive option for tendinopathy but current evidence remains limited and results are inconsistent — they are listed in the course slides with acknowledged uncertainty, not as first-line therapy. They are not proven by multiple high-quality RCTs. They are not contraindicated (they have been studied in this indication). They are not endorsed as first-line by all major guidelines. The established first-line treatments for tendinopathy are eccentric physical therapy and orthotics.","boardPrep":"PTTD adjunctive therapies: corticosteroid injections (use caution, rupture risk), regenerative injections/PRP (investigational), nitroglycerin patches (limited/mixed evidence). The course slides list all three as options without strong endorsement. For boards: eccentric PT and orthotics = established first-line. The adjunctive therapies have limited evidence and are used selectively.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 6 — Analgesics: topical agents including nitroglycerin patches, topical NSAIDs, and local anesthetics may have roles in musculoskeletal pain management; evidence quality varies significantly by agent and specific clinical indication","whyNot":{"C":"They are not contraindicated (","D":"They are not endorsed as first"}},{"id":"sdl7_q37","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old recreational runner with Achilles tendinopathy says his worst pain comes with the first few steps out of bed in the morning and eases as he keeps walking. His physician recommends a night splint along with calf stretches. Which explanation best accounts for both the morning pain and the benefit of the splint?","choices":{"A":"Overnight immobility lets inflammatory cells settle in the paratenon, and the splint compresses the tendon sheath so the cells are pushed back into the circulation by morning","B":"The ankle dorsiflexes during sleep and overstretches the tendon all night, and the splint holds the foot in plantarflexion so that the tendon can rest in a shortened position","C":"Morning pain comes from retrocalcaneal bursal fluid that collects overnight, and the splint squeezes the bursa so the fluid drains before the patient bears weight","D":"Morning pain reflects calcaneal apophyseal stress that peaks with the first weight bearing of the day, and the splint protects the growth plate during sleep","E":"The foot drifts into plantarflexion during sleep, so the Achilles shortens and is abruptly stretched on the first step; the splint keeps a gentle stretch on it overnight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The office orthopedics reading explains that during sleep the foot drifts into plantarflexion, relaxing and shortening the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia. With the first step, these structures are stretched abruptly, which is painful when they are inflamed and lack compliance; this is why Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis both hurt most on the first steps of the day. A night splint keeps a gentle stretch across the tendon during the night. Inflammatory cells being pushed into the circulation is not the mechanism. The foot does not dorsiflex during sleep, and holding the ankle in plantarflexion would worsen the morning stretch. Retrocalcaneal bursitis is a separate diagnosis, and the splint does not drain a bursa. Calcaneal apophysitis is a condition of skeletally immature children, not a 41-year-old.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First-step pain = Achilles tendinopathy or plantar fasciitis (structures shorten overnight in plantarflexion). Treatment: calf stretches, night splints, NSAIDs, eccentric loading; avoid corticosteroid injection near the Achilles (rupture risk). COMLEX: Gastrosoleus mobilization and correction of running mechanics address the load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 6 — retrocalcaneal bursitis often confused with plantar fasciitis"},{"id":"sdl7_q38","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female distance runner with mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy is prescribed an eccentric loading protocol. She asks why eccentric exercises are specifically recommended over concentric-only exercises for tendinopathy rehabilitation. Which mechanism best explains the therapeutic benefit?","choices":{"A":"Eccentric loading acutely elevates local glucocorticoid levels within tendon tissue, suppressing inflammatory cytokine production and reducing tendon pain signaling pathways","B":"Eccentric loading applies mechanical tension that stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis, fiber reorganization, and tendon matrix remodeling","C":"Eccentric loading immediately relieves pain by activating descending inhibitory spinal pain control pathways activated by high-intensity mechanical input during the exercise","D":"Eccentric loading compresses and reduces the size of the retrocalcaneal bursa by mechanically displacing fluid, reducing bursitis-related pain components during activity","E":"Eccentric loading relaxes the paratenon sheath surrounding the Achilles tendon, reducing adhesion formation between the tendon and paratenon during plantarflexion movements"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eccentric loading of the Achilles tendon stimulates tenocyte mechanotransduction, promoting collagen synthesis and reorganization of the disorganized tendon matrix in tendinopathy. This is the established mechanism underlying the Alfredson eccentric protocol. Eccentric loading does not acutely elevate local glucocorticoids. It does not work through descending pain inhibitory pathways. It does not compress the retrocalcaneal bursa or relax the paratenon sheath mechanically.","boardPrep":"Eccentric calf loading (Alfredson protocol): stand on step, raise on two feet, lower on single affected foot. Mechanism: mechanotransduction in tenocytes → collagen synthesis and fiber reorganization in the tendon matrix. Mid-portion responds better to eccentric loading; insertional may worsen with full ROM eccentric (avoid dorsiflexion past neutral in insertional disease). Multiple RCTs support eccentric loading as first-line PT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Physical therapy: eccentric exercise involves muscle-tendon unit contraction while lengthening; eccentric calf lowering places controlled tensile load on the Achilles, stimulating collagen remodeling in tendinopathy through tenocyte mechanotransduction","whyNot":{"C":"It does not work through desce"}},{"id":"sdl7_q39","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Achilles tendinopathy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old male with chronic mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy has had 3 months of eccentric exercises with incomplete improvement. He asks whether corticosteroid injection would speed his recovery. Which response best reflects current evidence regarding corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection is the established gold standard first-line treatment for Achilles tendinopathy and should be offered before any physical therapy is attempted for this patient","B":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the Achilles tendon substance is safe and recommended before attempting any further physical therapy or rehabilitation program","C":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture with intratendinous injection","D":"Corticosteroid injection is beneficial only for insertional Achilles tendinopathy into the retrocalcaneal bursa and is absolutely contraindicated for mid-portion disease","E":"Corticosteroid injection is absolutely contraindicated for all forms of tendinopathy and may only be used after complete tendon rupture and surgical repair has fully healed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection for Achilles tendinopathy has uncertain long-term benefit and carries significant risk of tendon weakening and rupture, particularly with intratendinous injection. The evidence for corticosteroid injection in tendinopathy is uncertain. It is not the gold standard first-line treatment — eccentric loading is. Intratendinous injection is specifically dangerous. Corticosteroid injection into the bursa (not the tendon) is appropriate for retrocalcaneal bursitis, but not for mid-portion tendinopathy.","boardPrep":"Corticosteroid injection in Achilles tendinopathy: NOT first-line. Risks include tendon rupture, especially intratendinous injection. Peritendinous injection (into paratenon) carries lower risk. Evidence: may provide short-term pain relief but inferior long-term outcomes vs eccentric loading. The evidence is uncertain. Contrast with retrocalcaneal bursitis: corticosteroid injection into the bursa is an appropriate adjunctive option.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs and corticosteroids: systemic and local corticosteroid effects include tendon collagen weakening and increased rupture risk; intratendinous injection carries the highest risk of subsequent Achilles tendon rupture","whyNot":{"B":"Intratendinous injection is sp"}},{"id":"sdl7_q40","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old female runner has posterior heel pain worse with dorsiflexion. Tenderness is at the posterior calcaneus just anterior to the Achilles tendon with local swelling. No plantar heel tenderness is present. She was previously treated for plantar fasciitis without success. Which finding most reliably distinguishes retrocalcaneal bursitis from plantar fasciitis?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis produces lateral calcaneal tenderness worse with inversion stress while plantar fasciitis produces medial calcaneal tenderness at the plantar fascia enthesis","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles worsened by dorsiflexion, while plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst on first steps","C":"Plantar fasciitis causes pain at the Achilles musculotendinous junction while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at the plantar fascial calcaneal attachment point","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes pain exclusively at night while plantar fasciitis is exclusively a first-morning-step (post-static dyskinesia) symptom and not a nocturnal complaint","E":"Plantar fasciitis causes posterior ankle swelling anterior to the Achilles tendon while retrocalcaneal bursitis causes diffuse inferior heel pain with prolonged weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes posterior calcaneal tenderness deep to the Achilles tendon worsened by dorsiflexion (which compresses the bursa between the calcaneus and the deep Achilles surface). Plantar fasciitis causes inferior/medial heel pain worst with first morning steps (post-static dyskinesia). These two conditions are frequently confused because both involve the heel, but the location of tenderness (posterior deep vs inferior) and the aggravating movement (dorsiflexion vs initial weight-bearing) clearly distinguish them.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis vs plantar fasciitis: retrocalcaneal = posterior calcaneus deep to Achilles, dorsiflexion worsens (compresses bursa); plantar fasciitis = inferior/medial heel, worse first steps in morning (post-static dyskinesia), point tenderness at plantar fascia origin. Course slides: retrocalcaneal bursitis \"often confused with plantar fasciitis.\" Dorsiflexion worsening = key distinguishing feature for bursitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy; distinguish from plantar fasciitis (inferior heel), Achilles tendinopathy (tendon mid-substance or insertion), and superficial calcaneal bursitis (skin-tendon interface irritated by shoe counter)","whyNot":{"C":"Plantar fasciitis causes infer"}},{"id":"sdl7_q41","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male has retrocalcaneal bursitis confirmed on ultrasound. After 3 months of cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching, he has only partial improvement. Which is the most appropriate next management step?","choices":{"A":"Proceed directly to surgical bursectomy with calcaneal exostectomy as surgery is the definitive treatment and should be offered after any conservative management failure","B":"Begin a 2-week oral prednisone taper combined with 6 weeks of complete non-weight bearing immobilization to reduce residual bursal inflammation via systemic corticosteroid","C":"Administer platelet-rich plasma injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as the current evidence-based second-line treatment endorsed by all major orthopaedic specialty societies","D":"Repeat diagnostic ultrasound to reassess the bursa size and vascularity before initiating any additional therapeutic intervention for this patient with refractory bursitis","E":"Offer corticosteroid injection into the retrocalcaneal bursa as an adjunctive treatment option for refractory cases that have failed the initial conservative management program"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After failure of initial conservative management (cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching), the appropriate next step for retrocalcaneal bursitis is corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa — not into the Achilles tendon. Surgery is rarely indicated and not the next step after only 3 months of conservative management. Oral corticosteroid taper is not the standard approach for localized bursitis. PRP is not endorsed as the current second-line standard for retrocalcaneal bursitis.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis management ladder: (1) Cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, Achilles stretching. (2) Corticosteroid injection INTO the bursa (appropriate, lower rupture risk than intratendinous). (3) Surgery — rarely indicated. Key: injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory bursitis; injection INTO the Achilles tendon is contraindicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 6 — Retrocalcaneal bursitis: cold compresses, analgesics, footwear modification, and Achilles stretching are first-line; corticosteroid injection into the bursa is appropriate for refractory cases; surgery is rarely needed and reserved for truly refractory cases","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery is rarely indicated an","B":"Oral corticosteroid taper is n","C":"PRP is not endorsed as the cur"}},{"id":"sdl7_q42","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of retrocalcaneal bursitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female triathlete is diagnosed with retrocalcaneal bursitis. Ultrasound also shows concurrent insertional Achilles tendinopathy at the same site. She asks whether the two conditions are related. Which statement best reflects the pathophysiologic relationship between retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy?","choices":{"A":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis and insertional Achilles tendinopathy have entirely distinct and unrelated pathophysiologic mechanisms with no consistent clinical association between them","B":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis invariably develops as a late complication of insertional Achilles tendinopathy and only occurs after tendinopathy has been present for several years","C":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis is highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus, as the bursa is mechanically stressed by the same disease process","D":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis always precedes Achilles tendinopathy and must be fully resolved before initiating any Achilles tendinopathy rehabilitation treatment in athletes","E":"Insertional Achilles tendinopathy exclusively affects athletes over age 60 while retrocalcaneal bursitis exclusively affects younger athletes under 30 years of age in sports"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slides state that retrocalcaneal bursitis is \"highly associated with Achilles tendinosis and enthesopathy of the calcaneus\" — the same mechanical forces that stress the Achilles tendon also compress the adjacent bursa between the thickened tendon and the calcaneus (or Haglund deformity). They do not have entirely unrelated mechanisms. The two conditions coexist frequently from early in the disease course and should be treated simultaneously with a unified rehabilitation approach.","boardPrep":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis: the course slide states \"inflammation highly associated with Achilles tendinosis, enthesopathy of the calcaneus.\" The bursa sits between calcaneus and Achilles — when the Achilles is thickened (tendinosis) or the calcaneus is irregular (enthesopathy/Haglund deformity), bursal compression increases. Treat both simultaneously. Haglund deformity = posterosuperior calcaneal prominence causing increased bursal compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: insertional type involves the calcaneal enthesis; the retrocalcaneal bursa lies immediately adjacent to this enthesis and is compressed by the same Achilles forces, explaining their frequent coexistence on ultrasound or MRI","whyNot":{"B":"The two conditions coexist fre","D":"The two conditions coexist fre"}},{"id":"sdl7_q43","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy who plays travel soccer has had Sever disease for 5 months. He has followed activity reduction, heel cushioning inserts, cold compresses, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, footwear modification, and a course of physical therapy, but heel pain still stops him from running. Which is the next step in management for this recalcitrant case?","choices":{"A":"Surgical excision of the calcaneal apophysis to remove the painful ossification center so that the Achilles tendon can reattach directly to mature calcaneal bone","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the apophysis at the Achilles insertion to settle the traction inflammation so that the boy can return to full practice this season","C":"Percutaneous tenotomy of the distal Achilles tendon to lengthen the tendon and reduce the tensile load it places on the posteroinferior calcaneal growth plate","D":"A period of immobilization to unload the apophysis from repetitive Achilles traction, the course option listed for recalcitrant cases of calcaneal apophysitis","E":"Permanent withdrawal from running sports until the apophysis fuses, since continued play leads to growth arrest and a shortened calcaneus in most children"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists activity reduction, footwear modification, heel cushioning, cold compresses, Achilles stretching, NSAIDs, and physical therapy as treatment, and immobilization for recalcitrant cases. Sever disease is a traction apophysitis that lasts 3 to 4 years and resolves when the apophysis fuses, so unloading the growth plate is the escalation. Surgical excision of the apophysis is not a treatment for this self-limited condition. Injecting steroid at a pediatric tendon insertion is not part of the course plan. Achilles tenotomy is a procedure for chronic adult tendinopathy, not for a child's apophysis. Permanent withdrawal from sport is unnecessary because the condition resolves at skeletal maturity, and growth arrest is not a described outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sever disease = traction apophysitis of the posteroinferior calcaneus at the Achilles insertion; self-limited, lasts 3–4 years, resolves with apophyseal fusion. COMLEX: Conservative care first; immobilization for recalcitrant cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — other traction apophysitides of the growing athlete","whyNot":{"A":"Surgical excision of the apoph","B":"Injecting steroid at a pediatr","C":"Achilles tenotomy is a procedu"}},{"id":"sdl7_q44","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old female gymnast has bilateral Sever disease. Her parents ask about the expected course and long-term prognosis. Which statement is most accurate regarding the natural history of Sever disease?","choices":{"A":"Sever disease requires surgical resection of the apophyseal ossification center to achieve pain-free return to gymnastics and prevent long-term skeletal deformity of the calcaneus","B":"Sever disease resolves within 2 weeks with complete rest and will not recur upon returning to gymnastics if heel cushioning is consistently worn during all athletic activity","C":"Sever disease is a permanent condition that persists throughout adulthood and requires lifelong footwear modification and Achilles stretching to manage symptoms indefinitely","D":"Sever disease will inevitably progress to calcaneal apophyseal stress fracture requiring surgery if the child participates in any form of physical activity during treatment","E":"Sever disease is a self-limited condition that typically lasts 3 to 4 years until the calcaneal apophysis fuses with skeletal maturity, after which symptoms fully resolve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sever disease is self-limited, resolving when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity — a process that takes approximately 3-4 years after onset. Surgery is NEVER indicated because the condition resolves completely without intervention at maturity, leaving no permanent deformity or sequelae. It does not resolve in 2 weeks with rest, nor does it persist permanently into adulthood. Progression to stress fracture is not inevitable with appropriate activity modification and symptom management.","boardPrep":"Sever disease prognosis: self-limited, resolves when the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity (3-4 years). No permanent deformity or long-term sequelae. Surgery NOT indicated. Contrast: Legg-Calve-Perthes (AVN, can cause permanent hip deformity) vs Sever (resolves fully at maturity). Osgood-Schlatter is also self-limited at skeletal maturity. Sever disease = purely traction apophysitis with benign prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children is a distinct condition with potential for permanent deformity; unlike LCP, Sever disease resolves completely without sequelae once the calcaneal apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity","whyNot":{"A":"Surgery is NEVER indicated bec","B":"It does not resolve in 2 weeks","C":"It does not resolve in 2 weeks"}},{"id":"sdl7_q45","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever Disease).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has several months of heel pain that started gradually and is worse during and after running practice. Her mother notes the pain is clearly worse on days she wears thin-soled flats or cleats and better in cushioned sneakers. There is tenderness at the posteroinferior calcaneus. Which statement best links this history to the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Pain worse in cleats points to a calcaneal stress fracture, because studs concentrate force on the bone and produce a focal break in the cortex","B":"Pain worse in flat shoes points to plantar fasciitis, because thin soles let the fascia overstretch with each step and pull on the medial tubercle","C":"Insidious, chronic heel pain worse with activity and with footwear lacking heel cushioning is the course presentation of calcaneal apophysitis","D":"Pain linked to shoe type points to retrocalcaneal bursitis, because a rigid heel counter compresses the bursa between the tendon and the bone","E":"Pain worse in thin soles points to tarsal tunnel syndrome, because the flattened arch stretches the tibial nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes Sever disease as insidious in onset, chronic, worse with activity, and worse with footwear lacking heel cushioning; heel cushioning and footwear modification are part of treatment. Tenderness at the posteroinferior calcaneus, the site of the apophysis at the Achilles insertion, fits. Plantar fasciitis causes inferior heel pain worst with the first steps of the day. A calcaneal stress fracture would follow a training change and show focal bony tenderness; footwear sensitivity of this kind is the listed feature of apophysitis. Retrocalcaneal bursitis is more common in adults and causes posterosuperior pain worse with dorsiflexion. Tarsal tunnel syndrome is a neuropathic, burning plantar pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sever disease: boys ~8 y, girls ~6 y, 2/3 bilateral; repetitive Achilles traction with running and jumping. COMLEX: Heel cups and cushioned footwear are simple first-line interventions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — how footwear does and does not relate to running injuries"},{"id":"sdl7_q46","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old runner has 6 months of exertional lateral leg pain that begins partway through each run and fades with rest. Exertional compartment syndrome was suspected, but pre- and post-exercise compartment pressure testing is normal. Burning pain radiates to the dorsum of the foot after long runs. According to the course, how should the physician interpret the normal pressures?","choices":{"A":"Normal pressures exclude any structural cause, so the pain should be managed as a functional disorder with reassurance and a gradual return to mileage","B":"Normal pressures point to medial tibial stress syndrome, so a bone scan should be ordered to look for diffuse uptake along the posteromedial tibia","C":"Normal pressures confirm early exertional compartment syndrome, so fasciotomy of the lateral compartment should be scheduled before nerve damage","D":"Normal pressures point to popliteal artery entrapment, so ankle-brachial indices with the foot in forced plantarflexion are the next step","E":"A lower extremity nerve injury should be considered, since it is suspected when the picture fits compartment syndrome but pressures are normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course introduces lower extremity nerve injuries as the diagnosis to consider when the presentation is consistent with exertional compartment syndrome but compartment pressures are normal. The nerves typically involved are the common peroneal, superficial peroneal, and saphenous, and burning pain radiating to the dorsum of the foot suggests a peroneal nerve source; nerve conduction studies and EMG localize the lesion. Normal pressures do not exclude a structural cause. MTSS causes diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness, not lateral burning pain radiating to the foot. Normal pressures argue against, not for, compartment syndrome, so fasciotomy is not indicated. Popliteal artery entrapment is not part of the course differential for this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Common peroneal nerve wraps the fibular neck → foot drop and dorsal foot numbness; superficial peroneal → lateral leg/dorsal foot sensation and eversion. COMLEX: ECS-like leg pain with normal pressures → think nerve entrapment, confirm with NCS/EMG.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — lumbar radiculopathy as another neurogenic source of leg pain","whyNot":{"B":"MTSS causes diffuse posteromed"}},{"id":"sdl7_q47","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old soccer player has superficial peroneal nerve entrapment at the crural fascia above the lateral malleolus. Over 4 months she has completed running gait analysis, physical therapy, anti-inflammatories, TENS, and a trial of a neuromodulator, and burning pain over the dorsolateral foot persists. Which option does the course list as an invasive next step?","choices":{"A":"Fasciotomy of all four leg compartments, because entrapment of the nerve reflects a chronic rise in compartment pressure that a release will relieve","B":"Transfer of the peroneus longus tendon, because entrapment weakens eversion and a transfer restores the lateral pull that stabilizes the ankle","C":"Ultrasound-guided hydrodissection of the nerve at its fascial exit point, with steroid injection or surgical release as the other invasive options","D":"Below-knee cast immobilization for 6 weeks, because rest of the ankle allows the nerve to remyelinate at the site where it pierces the fascia","E":"Amputation of the sensory branch at the ankle, because cutting the distal nerve stops the burning pain without causing any loss of sensation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides treatment of lower extremity nerve injuries into conservative care (running gait analysis, neuromodulators, TENS, physical therapy, anti-inflammatories) and invasive care (steroid injection, hydrodissection, surgical release). After failed conservative care, hydrodissection of the nerve at its exit through the crural fascia is a listed invasive option. A four-compartment fasciotomy treats compartment syndrome, which is not present. Superficial peroneal entrapment is a sensory problem in this patient with no weakness, so a tendon transfer has no role. Casting is not a listed treatment and would cause stiffness. Cutting the nerve would cause numbness in its distribution and is not a course option.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superficial peroneal nerve pierces the crural fascia ~proximal to the lateral malleolus; entrapment → burning dorsolateral foot and sinus tarsi numbness ± palpable fascial defect. COMLEX: Escalate from gait retraining and neuromodulators to injection, hydrodissection, or surgical release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 6 — other analgesics used for neuropathic and musculoskeletal pain","whyNot":{"B":"Superficial peroneal entrapmen","D":"Casting is not a listed treatm","E":"Cutting the nerve would cause "}},{"id":"sdl7_q48","sdlNumber":7,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male runner is diagnosed with saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee. He has pain and numbness in the medial knee region extending to the medial calf. He denies foot drop or ankle weakness. Which statement most accurately characterizes the saphenous nerve and its clinical features?","choices":{"A":"Saphenous nerve injury causes foot drop and decreased sensation over the lateral dorsum of the foot from compression of the nerve at the lateral fibular head on examination","B":"Saphenous nerve injury causes burning pain in the lateral ankle and dorsolateral foot without any motor deficits due to its purely sensory function along the lateral lower leg","C":"Saphenous nerve injury causes complete loss of ankle plantarflexion strength from gastrocnemius and soleus denervation as the nerve innervates the posterior calf compartment","D":"Saphenous nerve entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and calf with no motor deficits because it is a purely sensory nerve branch","E":"Saphenous nerve injury causes wrist drop from compression at the medial elbow, as the saphenous nerve is the lower extremity equivalent of the radial nerve in the upper extremity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The saphenous nerve is the terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve and is PURELY SENSORY — it carries no motor fibers. Entrapment at the medial knee fascia causes pain and numbness at the medial knee and medial calf without any motor deficit, distinguishing it from the common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) and differentiating it from other lower extremity entrapment neuropathies. Foot drop, plantarflexion weakness, and lateral foot symptoms are all inconsistent with saphenous nerve pathology.","boardPrep":"Saphenous nerve: terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve. Location: entrapment at medial knee fascia (between sartorius and gracilis). PURELY SENSORY — no motor deficit (key board fact). Symptoms: pain and numbness at medial knee and medial calf. Compare: common peroneal (motor + sensory, foot drop) vs superficial peroneal (sensory, dorsolateral foot) vs saphenous (purely sensory, medial knee/calf, NO motor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Upper extremity nerve injuries: axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves each carry both motor and sensory fibers; the saphenous nerve is purely sensory (femoral nerve branch) with no motor component, making it analogous to the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm in the upper extremity","whyNot":{"A":"Foot drop, plantarflexion weak","B":"Foot drop, plantarflexion weak","C":"Foot drop, plantarflexion weak"}}]};
